var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"architecture/mixed_precision/#mixed","page":"Mixed precision","text":"A solve has up to three precisions:\n\nprecision holds set by\nworking precision T the iterate, the residual, the Jacobian buffer, the stopping test and the status the element type of x₀\nfactorisation precision TF the factorisation and the triangular solves the type parameter of the linear method\nrefinement residual -F(x) - J d in a refinement pass T\n\nThe working precision sets the attainable accuracy of Newton's method, because the residual and the update are computed in T. The factorisation precision changes the rate of convergence, not the limit (Kelley 2022; Higham and Mary 2022).","title":"Mixed precision"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/mixed_precision/#The-factorisation-precision-is-a-type-parameter","page":"Mixed precision","text":"LUFactorization(Float32) is LUFactorization{Float32}(), and the same holds for LDLTFactorization, CholeskyFactorization, QRFactorization and SVDFactorization. A matrix that is positive definite in the working precision can fail to be positive definite in a lower TF; the factor is then SINGULAR, as any other failed factorisation in TF. The default LUFactorization() is LUFactorization{Nothing}(), which factorises in T. For a complex problem the precision stays real: LUFactorization(Float32) factorises a ComplexF64 Jacobian in ComplexF32. factor_eltype gives the element type of the factors:\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers\n\njulia> GeometricSolvers.factor_eltype(LUFactorization(Float32), ComplexF64) === ComplexF32\ntrue\n\njulia> GeometricSolvers.factor_eltype(LUFactorization(), Float64)\nFloat64\n\nThe precision is a type parameter and not a field, so the method stays isbits and enters a kernel, and the factor type that init_linear builds carries both the precision and the backend, which selects any vendor method by dispatch.","title":"The factorisation precision is a type parameter"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/mixed_precision/#The-scaled-mixed-precision-solve","page":"Mixed precision","text":"The boundary between the two precisions is one method of ldiv!!, the one for a factor whose element type differs from that of the right-hand side (src/linear/mixed.jl):\n\nfactorize!! divides the matrix by a power of two 2^e near its largest entry, converts it to TF, and factorises. A power of two is exact in every binary format, so the scaling adds no rounding error, and the largest entries of a Float64 matrix stay in the Float32 range. One factor of scale does not cover an arbitrary dynamic range: an entry far below the largest one underflows in TF, and the factor can then be singular, as for operatornamediag(2^128 2^-128). An LU, LDLᵀ or QR factor records the zero pivot, and the remedy chain then refactorises in T. An SVD factor drops the direction and records no failure, so the chain refactorises in T only after a failed refinement or two slow steps, and the solve can end on STALLED before that.\nldiv!! scales the right-hand side by s = b_infty, converts it to TF, and solves. The triangular solve in TF can overflow where the solution in T is finite: for operatornamediag(1 2^-128) and b = (0 1), the step 2^128 exceeds floatmax(Float32).\nThe solution is multiplied by s cdot 2^-e in the wider of the two precisions and only then converted to T, so this rescaling adds no overflow or underflow beyond that of the solution itself.\n\nThe bound that the test suite verifies for this solve is on the page Numerical Stability and Backward-Error Bounds.\n\njulia> F!(y, x, p) = (y[1] = x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 - p; y[2] = x[1] - x[2]; y);\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem(F!);\n\njulia> solver = init(prob, [1.0, 0.5], Newton(; linsolve = LUFactorization(Float32)), 2.0);\n\njulia> x, status = solve!(solver);\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && x ≈ [1.0, 1.0]\ntrue\n\njulia> eltype(stored_factors(solver))\nFloat32\n\nstored_factors returns the packed L and U of the last factorisation in TF.","title":"The scaled mixed-precision solve"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/mixed_precision/#Refinement:-NoRefinement,-Refinement,-GMRESRefinement","page":"Mixed precision","text":"The refinement field of Newton corrects the linear direction in T before the globalization:\n\nNoRefinement (the default) takes the direction of the solve in TF as it is. The step is then an inexact Newton step, and the rate slows towards linear as the error of the solve grows.\nRefinement(T; η, maxiter) is the LU-IR loop: it forms the residual r = -F(x) - J d in T, solves for a correction with the factor in TF, and stops when F(x) + J d le max(eta sqrtvarepsilon(T))F(x), or with a failure when a pass does not decrease that norm. The theory of iterative refinement in three precisions is that of Carson and Higham (2018).\nGMRESRefinement(T; η, maxiter, gmres_maxiter, gmres_tol) runs the same passes first and, where they fail, a GMRES in T left-preconditioned by the factors promoted to T: the GMRES-IR variant SDD of Amestoy, Buttari, Higham, L'Excellent, Mary and Vieublé (2024). The promoted copy is promoted_factors.\n\njulia> for ref in (Refinement(), GMRESRefinement())\n           method = Newton(; linsolve = LUFactorization(Float32), refinement = ref)\n           x, status = solve(prob, [1.0, 0.5], method, 2.0)\n           println(status.code, \" \", x ≈ [1.0, 1.0])\n       end\nSUCCESS true\nSUCCESS true","title":"Refinement: NoRefinement, Refinement, GMRESRefinement"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/mixed_precision/#The-remedy-chain","page":"Mixed precision","text":"A step whose factorisation in TF fails, a step whose refinement stages all fail, or two consecutive steps whose linear model is inaccurate in TF and which lower the residual by less than half, make the solve refactorise the Jacobian in T. The stages of Refinement are the LU-IR passes; GMRESRefinement runs GMRES-IR after them, so it refactorises in T only when GMRES-IR fails too. The chain of a step is therefore\n\nLU-IR  →  GMRES-IR (with GMRESRefinement)  →  refactorisation in T\n\nSolverStatus counts each failed stage in step_failures and records a factorisation in T in promoted. A refinement drives the linear residual J d + F to zero, so it does not apply to a LeastSquaresProblem with more residuals than unknowns, nor to the damped LevenbergMarquardt step; a GMRES solve has no factor to refine with.\n\nA value iterate (Number, SVector) has no Krylov basis, so a GMRESRefinement there runs the LU-IR passes alone.","title":"The remedy chain"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/mixed_precision/#When-a-lower-factorisation-precision-pays","page":"Mixed precision","text":"A factorisation in TF below T saves time and memory only where the factorisation dominates the cost of a step, that is where the evaluation of F and J costs less than the O(n^3) factorisation (Kelley 2022). In R1 the gain is registers and memory, not operations.\n\nIts applicability depends on the condition of the Jacobian. Without refinement, Newton's method converges while the error of the solve in TF, roughly kappa(J)varepsilon(T_F) times a modest function of n, stays below one (Higham and Mary 2022); the convergence slows as it grows. LU-IR converges under a condition of the same form and then recovers the iteration count of a solve in T (Carson and Higham 2018). GMRES-IR with promoted factors extends the range of condition numbers further (Amestoy et al. 2024). Where these conditions fail, the remedy chain above refactorises in T, and status.promoted records it.\n\nThe repository holds scripts/gmres_ir_comparison.jl, which compares the Newton iteration counts and the linear residuals of Refinement and GMRESRefinement over a range of condition numbers.","title":"When a lower factorisation precision pays"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/mixed_precision/#Mixed-precision-by-backend","page":"Mixed precision","text":"The pairs T = Float64 with TF = Float32 (and their complex forms) run on the CPU, on CUDA and on ROCm. On CUDA, a Float32 factor of a Float64 problem reaches the cuSOLVER method for Float32, and on ROCm a Float32 LU or QR factor reaches the rocSOLVER method. Metal has no Float64, so T = Float32 there and the pair with TF = Float32 does not apply; see Device precision.\n\nA residual can itself be less accurate than T, for instance when it calls a Float32 computation inside a Float64 solve. The limit of the solve is then the accuracy of F, and the tolerance must respect it: a tolerance below that floor ends on STALLED. The BatchEliminated problem shows this with a Float32 batch inside a Float64 outer solve.","title":"Mixed precision by backend"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/mixed_precision/#References","page":"Mixed precision","text":"P. Amestoy, A. Buttari, N. J. Higham, J.-Y. L'Excellent, T. Mary and B. Vieublé, Five-Precision GMRES-Based Iterative Refinement, SIAM Journal on Matrix Analysis and Applications 45(1), 529–552 (2024). doi:10.1137/23M1549079\nE. Carson and N. J. Higham, Accelerating the Solution of Linear Systems by Iterative Refinement in Three Precisions, SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 40(2), A817–A847 (2018). doi:10.1137/17M1140819\nN. J. Higham and T. Mary, Mixed precision algorithms in numerical linear algebra, Acta Numerica 31, 347–414 (2022). doi:10.1017/S0962492922000022\nC. T. Kelley, Newton's Method in Mixed Precision, SIAM Review 64(1), 191–211 (2022). doi:10.1137/20M1342902","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api/nonlinear/#Nonlinear-solvers","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"A nonlinear solver holds its algorithmic configuration (linsolve, globalization, jacobian, refinement, regularization and reuse for Newton; damping and globalization for Picard) and nothing about the problem or the iterate. GaussNewton constructs a Newton configured for LeastSquaresProblem (linsolve = QRFactorization(), globalization = Static()). The section The solve interface describes how a method enters a solve. default_method gives the method of an init or a solve call that names none.","title":"Nonlinear solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/nonlinear/#Mixed-precision-refinement","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"A Newton step whose factorisation runs in a precision TF below the working precision T of the iterate — linsolve = LUFactorization(Float32) on a Float64 problem — corrects its linear direction in T only with refinement = Refinement() or GMRESRefinement(); the default NoRefinement takes the direction of the solve in TF as it is. Refinement is the LU-IR loop of Kelley 2023 (Newton's Method in Three Precisions, doi:10.61208/pjo-2023-030): the factor's TF solve, the residual -F(x) - J d in T, and the correction that solve gives, to the tolerance max(η, √eps(T)) ‖F(x)‖. GMRESRefinement adds the SDD variant of Amestoy et al. 2024 in front of the refactorisation of the Jacobian in T: where the LU-IR passes fail, a left-preconditioned GMRES in T solves for a correction with a copy of the same Float32 factors promoted to T, so the step reaches the accuracy of a factorisation in T without forming one. The remedy chain of a step is therefore LU-IR -> GMRES-IR -> refactorisation in T, and SolverStatus counts each failed stage in step_failures and records a factorisation in T in promoted.\n\nThe buffers of a mixed-precision solve are readable after it: stored_factors is the packed L and U of the factorisation in TF, promoted_factors the working-precision copy of it that the GMRES-IR stage promotes (or the factors of the refactorisation, where the step promoted), preconditioned_buffer the slot of the preconditioned product the inner GMRES forms, and linear_residual_buffer the slot of the linear residual -F(x) - J d. scripts/gmres_ir_comparison.jl runs both methods at κ∞ ∈ {1e4, 5e7, 2e8, 5e10}, prints their Newton iteration counts and linear residuals, and recomputes both from the raw output of the solves.","title":"Mixed-precision refinement"},{"category":"function","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.default_method","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"default_method(prob, x₀)\n\nReturn the method that init(prob, x₀) and solve(prob, x₀) take when no method is given, from the type of prob, its Jacobian prototype and the storage of x₀:\n\nproblem iterate method\nNonlinearProblem with a SparseMatrixCSC jacobian_prototype Vector{Float64} or Vector{ComplexF64} Newton(; linsolve = UMFPACKFactorization())\nNonlinearProblem, every other prototype and iterate any other Newton()\nStationarityProblem any Newton()\nLeastSquaresProblem any GaussNewton()\n\nThe LUFactorization() of Newton() selects its factor from the storage of the Jacobian, so the rule reads no array type and no size of a dense iterate. A SparseMatrixCSC prototype on any other iterate takes Newton() as well: a Vector{Float32} gets the Sparspak factor where Sparspak is loaded, and init raises otherwise, as it does when the caller names Newton(). No default method is matrix-free and none is a combinator.\n\ninit(prob, x₀; kwargs...) and solve(prob, x₀; kwargs...) on a NonlinearProblem, a StationarityProblem or a LeastSquaresProblem call init(prob, x₀, default_method(prob, x₀), nothing; kwargs...) and solve(prob, x₀, default_method(prob, x₀), nothing; kwargs...).\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem((y, x, p) -> (y .= x .^ 2 .- 4; y));\n\njulia> default_method(prob, ones(3)) === Newton()\ntrue\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, ones(3));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && x ≈ [2.0, 2.0, 2.0]\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.default_method"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.Newton","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"Newton(; linsolve = LUFactorization(), globalization = Static(), jacobian = nothing,\n         refinement = NoRefinement(), regularization = 0, reuse = nothing,\n         forcing = nothing, preconditioner = nothing)\n\nNewton's method for NonlinearProblem, StationarityProblem and, as GaussNewton configures it, for LeastSquaresProblem, with linear solver linsolve (LUFactorization, LDLTFactorization, CholeskyFactorization, QRFactorization, SVDFactorization or the matrix-free GMRES), globalization globalization (Static, Backtracking, Bisection, StrongWolfe, the trust region DogLeg or the damped step LevenbergMarquardt), Jacobian selector jacobian, iterative refinement refinement (NoRefinement, Refinement or GMRESRefinement), diagonal regularisation regularization, and factor-reuse period reuse.\n\nFields\n\nlinsolve::L: the linear-solver method. A LeastSquaresProblem takes none of LUFactorization, LDLTFactorization and CholeskyFactorization outside the stacked LevenbergMarquardt step. With more residuals than unknowns it takes no factor in a lower precision than the element type of the iterate, because a Gauss–Newton step with such a factor stops off the least-squares point; a factor in the same or a higher precision is taken, and the stacked LevenbergMarquardt step, which reads no factor, takes any. Each refusal raises an ArgumentError where the state is built. A factor in a higher precision on more residuals than unknowns is not promoted to the working precision after a stall: at a nonzero-residual least-squares point F + J d approx F, which on a square problem reads as a stall. It is promoted where the factorisation fails.\nglobalization::G: the globalization method. A line search takes its merit at α = 0 from the state, where start!! sets it to norm2(F(x₀)) and each step!! to the merit of the residual it returns. A step!! with a line search therefore takes only the (x, Fx) that the last start!! or step!! on its state returned, as in solve!!. A line-search step on a state that no start!! saw, or that solve!! returned, returns NONFINITE and keeps its iterate, and so does a LevenbergMarquardt step, whose damping parameter that state holds as NaN.\njacobian::J: nothing (use prob.J when present, or AutoForwardDiff() otherwise), an AbstractADType, or a custom Jacobian callable: J(x, p), which returns the Jacobian, on a value iterate (a Number or a StaticArray), and J!(J, x, p), which writes it into J, on an array. A Newton with GMRES refuses a callable with an ArgumentError, because a GMRES solve forms no Jacobian matrix. AutoForwardDiff() writes a dense Jacobian only, so with a sparse jacobian_prototype it raises an ArgumentError where the state is built; such a problem takes an analytic J or an AutoSparse back end. Adapting Newton with ToReal (adapt(ToReal{R}(), m)) converts floating-point values captured inside a jacobian closure to R, while a function with no captured floats passes through unchanged.\nrefinement::RF: the refinement setting (NoRefinement, Refinement or GMRESRefinement). A Refinement refines the direction of the system that the factor solves, the regularised (J + μ I) d = -F when μ > 0; a GMRESRefinement runs the same passes and, where they fail on a factorisation below the working precision, a GMRES-IR stage with the promoted factors before the step refactorises in T. A LevenbergMarquardt globalization takes no refinement: a refinement corrects the solve of J d = -F, which the damped step does not take, and the constructor raises an ArgumentError. A LeastSquaresProblem takes no refinement either: a refinement drives the linear residual J d + F to zero, which a least-squares step with more residuals than unknowns does not reach, and the refusal raises an ArgumentError where the state is built.\nregularization::R: non-negative finite diagonal shift μ  0 added as J + μ I before factorisation when μ > 0. A LeastSquaresProblem with more residuals than unknowns takes none, and μ > 0 raises an ArgumentError where the state is built.\nreuse::K: nothing (K = Nothing, factorise at every step) or Int32(k) (K = Int32, reuse the factor for up to k consecutive steps, refreshing early after a STALLED step and, within the step, after a failed refinement of a Refinement or a GMRESRefinement on a reused factor, which counts one step-rule failure). A line search along a reused factor that fails without a step is retried once, in the same step, from a fresh factor. A DogLeg or LevenbergMarquardt globalization takes no reuse: its model needs the Jacobian of the current iterate, and the constructor raises an ArgumentError.\n\nKeywords of a GMRES linear method\n\nforcing and preconditioner configure the GMRES of linsolve, and a Newton that takes either of them with any other linear method raises an ArgumentError. A Newton with GMRES forms no factor, so it takes no reuse, no Refinement and no LevenbergMarquardt globalization, and a DogLeg needs an unrestarted GMRES without a preconditioner. A JVPFunction in jacobian needs a GMRES, because a factorising method needs a Jacobian matrix. Each refusal is an ArgumentError.\n\nforcing: an AbstractForcingTerm that replaces the forcing term of linsolve, and so the relative tolerance of every Newton step's inner solve.\npreconditioner: the right preconditioner of linsolve, which refresh! is called on once per Newton step and apply_preconditioner! on before every Jacobian-vector product.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Newton"},{"category":"function","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.GaussNewton","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"GaussNewton(; linsolve = QRFactorization(), globalization = Static(),\n              jacobian = nothing, refinement = NoRefinement(),\n              regularization = 0, reuse = nothing)\n\nGauss–Newton method for LeastSquaresProblem and NonlinearProblem. Returns a Newton solver configured with linsolve = QRFactorization() by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.GaussNewton"},{"category":"function","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.QuasiNewton","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"QuasiNewton(k; linsolve = LUFactorization(), globalization = Static(), jacobian = nothing,\n            refinement = NoRefinement(), regularization = 0)\n\nConstruct Newton with factor-reuse period reuse = Int32(k) (k ≥ 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.QuasiNewton"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.Picard","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"Picard(; damping = 1, globalization = Static())\n\nDamped fixed-point (Picard) iteration for a NonlinearProblem, a StationarityProblem or a LeftPreconditionedProblem, with search direction d = -mathrmdampingF(x p) and globalization globalization (Static, Backtracking, Bisection or StrongWolfe).\n\nFields\n\ndamping::D: positive finite damping factor omega  0, stored in floating-point.\nglobalization::G: the globalization method. Any line search uses MeasuredSlope, and takes its merit at α = 0 from the state, where start!! sets it to norm2(F(x₀)) and each step!! to the merit of the residual it returns. A step!! with a line search therefore takes only the (x, Fx) that the last start!! or step!! on its state returned, as in solve!!. A line-search step on a state that no start!! saw, or that solve!! returned, returns NONFINITE and keeps its iterate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Picard"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.NoRefinement","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"NoRefinement()\n\nThe default iterative-refinement setting of Newton, which returns the linear solve's step unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.NoRefinement"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.Refinement","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"Refinement(T = Float64; η = √eps(float(real(T))), maxiter = 5)\nRefinement(η, maxiter = 5)\n\nIterative refinement of the linear direction d in Newton using the existing factor fac and Jacobian-vector products in the working precision T of the problem: a step that factorised at x multiplies by the regularised Jacobian it holds, and a step along a reused factor takes jvp!! at x.\n\nThe keyword constructor takes the real type T of η, and the positional one the type of η.\n\nAt each inner pass (1 ≤ iter ≤ maxiter), refine!! forms the linear residual r = -F(x) - J(x) d in T, solves fac \\ r in the factor precision TF for the correction δd, updates d ← d + δd, and stops as soon as\n\nF(x) + J(x) d le max(eta sqrtvarepsilon(R)) F(x)\n\nfor R = real(T). If a pass fails to strictly decrease ‖F(x) + J(x) d‖ (!(r_curr < r_prev)) or if maxiter passes finish above the threshold, refine!! returns the best direction reached together with failed = true.\n\nBoth maxiter ≥ 1 (Int32) and 0 < η < 1 (R <: Real) are validated at construction. Refinement{R} is isbits, and ToReal adapts η to the real type of the iterate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Refinement"},{"category":"type","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.GMRESRefinement","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"GMRESRefinement(T = Float64; η = √eps(float(real(T))), maxiter = 5, gmres_maxiter = 30,\n                gmres_tol = 1e-6)\nGMRESRefinement(η, maxiter = 5, gmres_maxiter = 30, gmres_tol = 1e-6)\n\nThe mixed-precision iterative refinement of Newton of Amestoy et al. 2024, their variant SDD, in the five precisions (u_f, u, u_p, u_g, u_r) = (Float32, Float64, Float64, Float64, Float64): the factorisation is in a factorisation precision TF below the working precision T of the iterate, and the linear direction of a step is corrected in T first by Refinement passes with the low-precision factors, then, where those fail, by a preconditioned GMRES in T on a copy of the same factors promoted to T (Kelley 2023, p. 7; Higham and Mary 2022, pp. 374–375).\n\nThe keyword constructor takes the real type T of η and of gmres_tol, and the positional one the types of its arguments. Both maxiter ≥ 1 and gmres_maxiter ≥ 1 (Int32) and 0 < η < 1, 0 < gmres_tol < 1 (R <: Real) are validated at construction.\n\nFields\n\nη::R, maxiter::Int32: the tolerance and the pass count of the LU-IR passes, as in Refinement.\ngmres_maxiter::Int32: the cap on the inner GMRES iterations of one correction, lowered to the number of unknowns of the solve. A correction that reaches the cap without meeting its tolerance is a refinement failure.\ngmres_tol::R: the relative tolerance of the inner GMRES, on the true linear residual -F(x) - J d_2 of the direction it returns, which the stage recomputes in T and accepts up to ten times, so that the difference between the Arnoldi estimate and the true residual cannot pass a correction that the estimate alone would.\n\nA Newton step whose factorisation is already in T runs the LU-IR passes alone: with no low-precision factor there is no GMRES-IR stage, and the refinement is then a Refinement. A value iterate (Number or StaticArray) has no Krylov basis, so it too runs the LU-IR passes alone, and a step that fails them refactorises in T as with a Refinement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.GMRESRefinement"},{"category":"function","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.stored_factors","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"stored_factors(factor)\n\nThe n × n storage of the factors of a factorisation, in the element type of the factor: the packed LU (U on and above the diagonal, the multipliers of the unit L below it) of an LUFactorization, which is the storage that promote_lu_factors! copies into the working precision. It is nothing for a solve that holds no factor, and for a factor that is no LU factor: a QR, SVD or LDLᵀ factorisation has no GMRES-IR stage that promotes it.\n\n\n\n\n\nstored_factors(solver)\n\nThe factors of the factorisation that the last step of solver took, in the factorisation precision TF: stored_factors of the factor of the state, the packed L and U of an LUFactorization with its ipiv in the factor itself. It is nothing for a solve that holds no factor — a matrix-free GMRES solve, and a value iterate whose factor no step stored — and for a factorisation that is no LU factorisation. stored_factors(state) reads the state that init_state returned instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.stored_factors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.promoted_factors","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"promoted_factors(solver)\n\nThe factors of the working-precision copy of stored_factors that a GMRESRefinement promotes, in the element type of the iterate T: T.(stored_factors(solver)) while the GMRES-IR stage that promoted them is the one that holds them, and the refactorised factors of the Jacobian where the step promoted to T instead. The storage belongs to the state, and init leaves it zeroed, so a read before the first promotion reads zeros. It is nothing for a solve that keeps no working-precision factor slot: a factorisation already in T, a value iterate, and a matrix-free GMRES solve; and for a factorisation that is no LU factorisation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.promoted_factors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.preconditioned_buffer","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"preconditioned_buffer(solver)\n\nThe buffer of the preconditioned product hatU^-1 hatL^-1 P (J v) that the GMRES-IR stage of a GMRESRefinement forms for each Krylov vector v, in the working precision T, or nothing for a solve that forms none. preconditioned_buffer(state) reads the state that init_state returned instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.preconditioned_buffer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/nonlinear/#GeometricSolvers.linear_residual_buffer","page":"Nonlinear solvers","text":"linear_residual_buffer(solver)\n\nThe buffer of the linear residual r = -F(x) - J d of the last step of solver, in the working precision T, which the defect-correction passes and the GMRES-IR stage reuse. It is nothing for a solve that refines nothing, and for a value iterate, whose storage holds no buffer. It is not the slot of F(x₀) that residual_buffer names. linear_residual_buffer(state) reads the state that init_state returned instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.linear_residual_buffer"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/globalization/#globalization","page":"Globalization","text":"A globalization decides how far a step goes along the direction of the method. The package has two kinds:\n\nline searches (Static, Backtracking, Bisection, StrongWolfe) search along the direction d on the merit varphi(alpha) = F(x + alpha d)_2^2. They serve Newton and Picard.\ntrust-region and damped steps (DogLeg, LevenbergMarquardt) change the direction itself and need the Jacobian and its factor. They serve Newton only, which keeps the radius or the damping parameter in its state.\n\nA line search is scalar code: linesearch sees the line only through varphi and its derivative varphi, allocates nothing and never throws, so the same search runs on the host and inside a kernel. The slope varphi(0) of a Newton direction is known exactly (-2varphi(0) for an exact step); for an inexact or a Picard step it is one Jacobian–vector product. The iteration cap of a search is its own maxiter field.\n\nThe example of this page is F(x) = arctan(x) from x_0 = 3, where the full Newton step overshoots and the iterates grow:\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> atan(x));\n\njulia> solve(prob, 3.0, Newton())[2].code === SUCCESS\nfalse","title":"Globalization"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/globalization/#Static","page":"Globalization","text":"Static(α = 1.0) takes the fixed step α and evaluates nothing. It is the default and the common case inside an integrator, where the start is close to the solution.","title":"Static"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/globalization/#Backtracking","page":"Globalization","text":"Backtracking(; c₁ = 1e-4, p = 0.5, maxiter = 100) is the Armijo line search with safeguarded quadratic and cubic interpolation. It shrinks the trial step until the sufficient decrease condition varphi(alpha) - varphi(0) le c_1 alpha varphi(0) holds, with a round-off allowance. For an inexact step it tests the decrease of the residual norm against the linear residual of the step instead. It needs one residual evaluation per trial.\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, 3.0, Newton(; globalization = Backtracking()));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && abs(x) < 1e-8\ntrue","title":"Backtracking"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/globalization/#Bisection","page":"Globalization","text":"Bisection(; αmax = 65536.0, maxiter = 100) brackets a sign change of varphi from - to + and bisects it, so a bracketed search returns a minimiser of the merit along the line, never a maximiser. Where varphi stays negative up to αmax, it returns the step αmax, and after maxiter trials it ends with LINESEARCH_FAILED. It costs one varphi per trial: one for each doubling of the trial step while varphi stays negative, then one for each bisection. While the lower end of the bracket is 0, the bisections grow with log_2 log_2 of the ratio of the trial step to the step floor; a bracket with a positive lower end is bisected to a relative width of sqrtvarepsilon. It can take a step longer than the trial step.\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, 3.0, Newton(; globalization = Bisection()));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && abs(x) < 1e-8\ntrue","title":"Bisection"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/globalization/#StrongWolfe","page":"Globalization","text":"StrongWolfe(; c₁ = 1e-4, c₂ = 0.9, αmax = 65536.0, maxiter = 100) finds a step that satisfies the strong Wolfe conditions\n\nvarphi(alpha) - varphi(0) le c_1 alpha varphi(0) qquad\nvarphi(alpha) le c_2 varphi(0)\n\nin a bracketing phase, which doubles the trial step, and a zoom phase, which interpolates inside the bracket. A trial that fails the first condition costs one varphi, and a trial that passes it one varphi and one varphi.\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, 3.0, Newton(; globalization = StrongWolfe()));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && abs(x) < 1e-8\ntrue","title":"StrongWolfe"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/globalization/#DogLeg","page":"Globalization","text":"DogLeg(; radius = nothing) is the trust-region globalization on the dogleg curve of Pawlowski, Simonis, Walker and Shadid (2008). The step is the Newton step inside the radius, the Cauchy step when the Cauchy point lies outside it, and otherwise the point of the radius on the segment between them. A rejected trial shrinks the radius and chooses again on the same curve, without a new Jacobian or factorisation. A singular factor gives the Cauchy step, so DogLeg continues where a line search along a Newton direction has no direction.\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, 3.0, Newton(; globalization = DogLeg()));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && abs(x) < 1e-8\ntrue\n\nWith a GMRES linear solver, DogLeg runs on the Arnoldi relation of one unrestarted cycle without a preconditioner.","title":"DogLeg"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/globalization/#LevenbergMarquardt","page":"Globalization","text":"LevenbergMarquardt(R = Float64; μ₀, μ_min, μ_max, μ_decrease, μ_increase, ρ_accept, scaling) computes the trial step from the damped model (J^mathsfH J + mu D^mathsfT D) delta = -J^mathsfH F and adapts mu from the gain ratio of the actual and the predicted reduction. A rejected trial raises mu and solves again, without a new Jacobian. With SVDFactorization and scaling = :none each trial reuses one SVD of J; with the other factorisations, or with scaling = :diagonal, the stacked path reduces [J | -F] once per step and each trial eliminates only its own damping rows by Givens rotations, so J^mathsfH J is never formed. It serves a NonlinearProblem, a StationarityProblem and a LeastSquaresProblem.\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, 3.0, Newton(; globalization = LevenbergMarquardt()));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && abs(x) < 1e-8\ntrue\n\nscaling = :diagonal scales the damping by the column norms of J. The stacked path indexes scalars and runs on a host array only. LevenbergMarquardt takes no refinement and no factor reuse.","title":"LevenbergMarquardt"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/globalization/#How-to-choose","page":"Globalization","text":"Close to the solution, as in the stages of an implicit integrator, Static costs nothing beyond the Newton step.\nFrom a distant start, a line search or a trust region makes the merit decrease. A line search needs only residual evaluations and slopes along one direction. DogLeg and LevenbergMarquardt change the direction and continue where the Jacobian is singular or ill-conditioned.\nFor a least-squares problem, LevenbergMarquardt damps the Gauss–Newton step.\nOn a device array, the stacked LevenbergMarquardt path is refused with an ArgumentError, because it indexes scalars: only SVDFactorization with scaling = :none runs there.\n\nKNOWN_ISSUES.md in the repository lists the problems where a globalization is known to fail.","title":"How to choose"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/globalization/#References","page":"Globalization","text":"R. P. Pawlowski, J. P. Simonis, H. F. Walker and J. N. Shadid, Inexact Newton Dogleg Methods, SIAM Journal on Numerical Analysis 46(4), 2112–2132 (2008). doi:10.1137/050632166","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/regimes/#regimes","page":"The three regimes","text":"One solver loop serves three regimes. The storage of the iterate selects the regime. Most problems and methods work in each of them, but not all: solve_batch! takes no LeastSquaresProblem, and a Newton with GMRES needs an R3 iterate. Check the docstrings of the problem and the method for the regimes that they support.\n\nregime iterate entry point where it runs\nR1 value semantics Number or SVector solve, solve!! on the host, or in one GPU thread\nR2 batched an array of Number or SVector, one entry per system solve_batch! one work-item per system on a KernelAbstractions backend\nR3 in place an AbstractVector that is not static solve, init, solve! on the CPU or on a device array (CuArray, MtlArray, ROCArray)","title":"The three regimes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/regimes/#R1:-value-semantics","page":"The three regimes","text":"In R1 the iterate is a Number or an SVector, and every operation returns a new value. The state of the solve holds no buffers: each buffer slot is nothing. The problem, the method, the state, the Options and the SolverStatus are isbits when the functions of the problem are, so a kernel can hold all of them. The static factorisations (StaticLU, StaticQR, StaticLDLT, StaticCholesky, StaticSVD) are scalar loops that the compiler keeps in registers for an isbits element type.\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers, StaticArrays\n\njulia> F(x, p) = SVector(x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 - p, x[1] - x[2]);\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem(F);\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, SVector(1.0, 0.5), Newton(), 2.0);\n\njulia> x isa SVector{2, Float64} && x ≈ SVector(1.0, 1.0)\ntrue\n\njulia> isbits(prob) && isbits(status)\ntrue\n\nInside a KernelAbstractions.@kernel, the caller builds the state once on the host with init_state and calls solve!! in each work-item. solve_batch! does exactly that.\n\nR1 suits small systems: the static factorisations unroll over the size of the system, so the compile time and the register use grow with it.","title":"R1: value semantics"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/regimes/#R2:-batched-solves","page":"The three regimes","text":"solve_batch! solves a batch of independent small systems in one kernel launch on get_backend(X), one work-item per system. Each entry X[i] is the initial guess of system i and is overwritten with its solution. The kernel runs the R1 loop, so a batch takes every method that R1 takes.\n\nThe parameters are shared across the batch unless they are wrapped:\n\nPerSystem(data) gives system i the value data[i],\nBatchParams(shared, per_system) gives system i the tuple (shared, per_system[i]).\n\nThe form with a status vector writes one SolverStatus per system, and batch_summary reduces them to a BatchSummary:\n\njulia> X = [SVector(1.0, 0.5), SVector(2.0, 1.0), SVector(0.5, 0.1)];\n\njulia> S = Vector{SolverStatus{Float64}}(undef, length(X));\n\njulia> solve_batch!(X, S, prob, Newton(), PerSystem([2.0, 8.0, 0.5]));\n\njulia> X ≈ [SVector(1.0, 1.0), SVector(2.0, 2.0), SVector(0.5, 0.5)]\ntrue\n\njulia> batch_summary(S).all_converged\ntrue\n\nThe form without a status vector returns the count of each return code:\n\njulia> X = [SVector(1.0, 0.5), SVector(2.0, 1.0)];\n\njulia> solve_batch!(X, prob, Newton(), PerSystem([2.0, 8.0])).SUCCESS\n2\n\nOn a Vector the kernel runs on the KernelAbstractions CPU() backend. On a device, the batch, the status vector and any PerSystem data are arrays of that device. The following example is not run in this documentation, because it needs a GPU:\n\n# Device example (not run here): one GPU thread per system.\nusing GeometricSolvers, StaticArrays, CUDA        # or Metal, AMDGPU\n\nF(x, p) = SVector(x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 - p, x[1] - x[2])\nprob = NonlinearProblem(F)\n\nn = 10^6\nX = CuArray(fill(SVector(1.0f0, 0.5f0), n))      # MtlArray, ROCArray\nS = similar(X, SolverStatus{Float32})\np = PerSystem(CuArray(rand(Float32, n) .+ 1))\n\nsolve_batch!(X, S, prob, Newton(), p)\n\nThe kernel takes the problem by value, so every part of it must be isbits: a residual that captures a host array, or a sparse jacobian_prototype, cannot enter a device kernel. On Metal the element type is Float32.","title":"R2: batched solves"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/regimes/#R3:-in-place-arrays","page":"The three regimes","text":"In R3 the iterate is an AbstractVector that is not static. The residual and the Jacobian write into buffers, and init allocates every buffer once. The factorisation is LAPACK on a host Array and a device factorisation on a GPU array. See Linear solvers for the factor that each array type gets. CholeskyFactorization factorises on a host Array only: it has no device factorisation, and a GPU array raises an ArgumentError in init.\n\njulia> F!(y, x, p) = (y .= x .^ 2 .- p; y);\n\njulia> solver = init(NonlinearProblem(F!), ones(4), Newton(), [1.0, 4.0, 9.0, 16.0]);\n\njulia> x, status = solve!(solver);\n\njulia> x ≈ [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]\ntrue\n\nThe same code runs on a device array. The residual must not index scalars, so broadcast operations are the usual form. This example is not run here:\n\n# Device example (not run here).\nusing GeometricSolvers, CUDA        # Metal: MtlArray and Float32 only\n\nF!(y, x, p) = (y .= x .^ 2 .- p; y)\nx₀ = CUDA.ones(Float64, 1000)\np = CuArray(collect(1.0:1000.0))\n\nx, status = solve(NonlinearProblem(F!), x₀, Newton(), p)\n\nOn a device array, the default Jacobian is the own chunked forward mode of ForwardDiff, and the factor is DeviceLU, DeviceQR, DeviceLDLT or DeviceSVD. A matrix-free GMRES solve needs no factor at all.","title":"R3: in-place arrays"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/regimes/#How-to-choose-a-regime","page":"The three regimes","text":"The regime follows the size and the number of the systems.\n\nOne small system (a few unknowns, such as the stage equations of an implicit integrator for a low-dimensional system): R1 with an SVector. It allocates nothing and the static factorisations run in registers.\nMany independent small systems of the same form (one per particle, per cell or per sample): R2 with solve_batch!. One launch solves all of them, on the CPU or on a GPU.\nOne large system: R3 with a Vector or a device array, through init and solve! when it is solved more than once. For a large system whose Jacobian no array can hold, use the matrix-free GMRES. For a large sparse system on the host, use a sparse jacobian_prototype with Sparspak (see Sparse direct solves).\n\nThe size where R3 on a device starts to pay depends on the device, the element type and the cost of the residual; this documentation records no measurement of it.","title":"How to choose a regime"},{"category":"section","location":"api/globalization/#Line-searches","page":"Line searches","text":"A line search is scalar code: linesearch sees the line only through the merit φ and its derivative φ′, allocates nothing and never throws, so the same search runs on the host and inside a kernel. Its iteration cap, where it has one, is its own field. Along a LineFunction its LineSearchResult also carries the residual at the step, so the caller does not evaluate it again.","title":"Line searches"},{"category":"section","location":"api/globalization/#Trust-region-and-damped-steps","page":"Line searches","text":"DogLeg and LevenbergMarquardt are the trust-region and damped-step globalizations of Newton. Neither is a line search: each needs the Jacobian, its factor and a parameter that lives across iterations (the trust-region radius or the damping parameter μ), so Newton keeps that parameter in its state and runs the trial loop against the factor itself.","title":"Trust region and damped steps"},{"category":"type","location":"api/globalization/#GeometricSolvers.Static","page":"Line searches","text":"Static(α = 1.0)\n\nThe line search that takes the fixed step α, bounded by the caller's αmax, and evaluates nothing: the common case inside an integrator. It ignores the trial step and returns SUCCESS with φ = NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Static"},{"category":"type","location":"api/globalization/#GeometricSolvers.Backtracking","page":"Line searches","text":"Backtracking(; c₁ = 1e-4, p = 0.5, maxiter = 100)\n\nThe Armijo line search with a safeguarded quadratic and cubic interpolation step. It shrinks the trial step until the sufficient_decrease test\n\nφ(α) - φ(0)  minbigl(0 c_1 α φ(0) + τbigr)\n\nholds, where τ is the roundoff of φ(0). Each backtrack takes the minimiser of the model through φ(0), φ(0) and the last one or two trials, clamped to 01 α p α. A trial whose merit is not finite gives no model; the next trial is then the smaller of 01 α and the geometric mean of α and the step floor. The search stops at the step floor smallest_step, after two trials at steps below sqrtmathrmeps whose merit equals φ(0) bit for bit, or after maxiter trials.\n\nThe slope φ(0) comes from the step kind: -2φ(0) for an ExactStep, one evaluation for a MeasuredSlope, the number itself for a slope passed in. For an InexactStep with residual η the test is Eisenstat and Walker's r(x + α d)  1 - c_1 (1 - η(α)) r with η(α) = 1 - α + α η, and the model slope is -2(1 - η) φ(0).\n\nThe model is built from differences of the merit divided by φ(0), and the cubic step uses the form that does not cancel on a near-quadratic merit, so the trials do not depend on the scale of the merit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Backtracking"},{"category":"type","location":"api/globalization/#GeometricSolvers.Bisection","page":"Line searches","text":"Bisection(; αmax = 65536.0, maxiter = 100)\n\nThe line search that bisects φ for a minimiser of the merit along the line. It brackets from the anchor: the lower end starts at 0, where φ(0)  0, and the upper end at the trial step, doubled while φ  0, up to the smaller of its own αmax and the caller's. So the bracket always holds a sign change from - to +: a minimum, never a maximum. A φ that is not finite counts as not descending, so the bracket never grows into a region where the merit is not finite.\n\nIt then bisects the bracket. While the bracket spans more than a factor 2, the next trial is the geometric mean of its ends, with a lower end of 0 taken as the step floor smallest_step; after that it is the middle. It stops when the width is below sqrtmathrmeps times the upper end, or when the lower end is still 0 and the upper end is within a factor 2 of the floor. So the number of trials grows only with log_2 log_2 of the ratio of the trial step to the floor, and it stays bounded for αmax = Inf. While the lower end stays at 0, a search from the trial step α costs at most 3 + log_2 log_2(α  mathrmfloatmin(R)) evaluations, because the floor is at least floatmin(R): 10 in Float32 and 13 in Float64 for α = 1.\n\nIt returns the lower end of the bracket, where φ is finite and negative, its upper end if the lower end is 0, or the ceiling if φ is still negative there, and classifies it by the merit there: one evaluation of φ after one φ per trial.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Bisection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/globalization/#GeometricSolvers.StrongWolfe","page":"Line searches","text":"StrongWolfe(; c₁ = 1e-4, c₂ = 0.9, αmax = 65536.0, maxiter = 100)\n\nThe line search for a step with the strong Wolfe conditions\n\nφ(α) - φ(0)  c_1 α φ(0) qquad φ(α)  c_2 φ(0)\n\n0  c_1  c_2  1: Nocedal and Wright's Algorithms 3.5 and 3.6. The first condition is the sufficient_decrease test without a round-off allowance. The bracketing phase doubles the trial step up to the smaller of αmax and the caller's ceiling until a trial fails the first condition, does not decrease the merit, or has φ  0. The zoom phase then takes the minimiser of the cubic through the merit and its derivative at both ends of the bracket, or of the quadratic through both merits and the derivative at the lower end where the upper end has no derivative. The trial is safeguarded to the inner 80 % of the bracket, and it is the middle after a trial that shrinks the bracket by less than a factor 0.66, as in Moré and Thuente. The lower end is the trial with the lowest merit that meets the first condition, up to the roundoff of the larger of two merits: a trial within it of the lower end ties with it, and the sign of φ at the trial decides the side of the minimiser. A zoom trial that meets both conditions is accepted whatever its merit.\n\nφ is evaluated only at a trial that meets the first condition and, in the bracketing phase, decreases the merit: a trial that fails costs one φ, a trial that passes one φ and one φ.\n\nIt returns SUCCESS only for a step that meets both conditions and decreases the merit by more than its round-off. Every other search returns its lowest trial that meets the first condition, or else its last trial. At the ceiling, at a collapsed bracket or at the step floor smallest_step the code is STALLED if the merit is at its round-off floor and LINESEARCH_FAILED otherwise; after maxiter trials it is LINESEARCH_FAILED.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StrongWolfe"},{"category":"type","location":"api/globalization/#GeometricSolvers.DogLeg","page":"Line searches","text":"DogLeg(; radius = nothing)\n\nThe trust-region globalization of Newton on the dogleg curve: Newton(; globalization = DogLeg()). It is not a LineSearch: it needs the Jacobian, its factor and a radius that lives from one iteration to the next, so Newton holds the radius in its state and runs the trial loop with its own Jacobian.\n\nThe defaults are Procedure 4.1 of Pawlowski, Simonis, Walker and Shadid (2008). The step is the Newton step when it lies in the radius δ, else the Cauchy step along -J^* F scaled to δ when the Cauchy point lies outside δ, else the point of norm δ on the segment from the Cauchy point to the Newton step. J is the matrix that was factorised, regularisation included. A trial is accepted when the predicted reduction mathrmpred = F(x) - F(x) + J s is positive and the actual reduction mathrmared = F(x) - F(x + s) satisfies mathrmared  10^-4 mathrmpred, with unsquared norms; a rejected trial shrinks δ to max(δ4 10^-6) and chooses again on the same curve, with no new Jacobian or solve. So a rejected Newton step that lies well inside δ is tried again, at one evaluation of F each time, until δ falls below its norm. A rejection at δ = 10^-6 returns LINESEARCH_FAILED with the iterate kept. After an accepted step with ratio ρ = mathrmared  mathrmpred, ρ  01 sets δ to max(s^N 10^-6) when the Newton step s^N is shorter than δ and to max(δ4 10^-6) otherwise, and ρ  075 on the boundary sets it to min(4δ 10^10). An accepted step other than the whole Newton step sets limited in its StepInfo: the radius or the Cauchy point, not the distance to a root, sets its norm, so the solve applies only the residual test after it.\n\nradius is the initial radius. It must be positive and finite as given; DogLeg stores it converted to float(typeof(radius)), and ToReal to the iterate's real type, clamped to 10^-6 10^10 after each conversion. With nothing, the first step starts the radius at the norm of the first Newton step, clamped the same way, except that a norm below 10^-6 starts it at 2 cdot 10^-6, as Procedure 4.1 does. A failed factor, or a Newton step whose norm is zero or not finite, gives no Newton step: the step is then the Cauchy point, cut to the radius when it lies outside, and a first radius starts at the length of the Cauchy point, by the same rule. The Cauchy direction is formed from the unit residual, tilde g = J^* F  F, so no norm of J^* F occurs, and a common scale of F and J leaves the Cauchy point unchanged up to rounding, as long as the norm of tilde g is finite. Where the Cauchy point is needed and tilde g is zero or its norm is not finite, or J tilde g  tilde g is zero or its norm is not finite, the step returns STALLED with the iterate kept. At F(x) = 0 the step returns SUCCESS with the iterate and the radius kept, and makes no trial.\n\nDogLeg takes no factor reuse: Newton(; globalization = DogLeg(), reuse = k) raises an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DogLeg"},{"category":"type","location":"api/globalization/#GeometricSolvers.LevenbergMarquardt","page":"Line searches","text":"LevenbergMarquardt{R, S}\nLevenbergMarquardt(::Type{R} = Float64; μ₀ = R(1e-6), μ_min = max(R(1e-15), 4 * eps(R)),\n                   μ_max = R(1e12), μ_decrease = R(1) / R(3), μ_increase = R(4),\n                   ρ_accept = zero(R), scaling = :none) where {R <: AbstractFloat}\n\nAdaptive Levenberg–Marquardt damping for Newton, computing the trial step from the damped linear model (J(x)ᴴ J(x) + μ Dᵀ D) δ = -J(x)ᴴ F(x) with damping parameter μ > 0 adapted from the gain ratio\n\nrho = fracF(x)_2^2 - F(x + delta)_2^2F(x)_2^2 - F(x) + J(x)delta_2^2\n\nμ is absolute, in the units of J(x)ᴴ J(x): scaling the residual by k scales J(x)ᴴ J(x) by k² and leaves μ as it is, so a damping of μ on k J(x) acts as μ / k² does on J(x), and the μ → 0 limit is the Newton step, truncated to the numerical rank as the solve of SVDFactorization truncates it, whatever the scale. With scaling = :diagonal, D is instead the diagonal of the column norms of J(x), floored at √eps of the largest of them.\n\nWith SVDFactorization and scaling = :none, J(x) = U Σ Vᴴ is factorised once per outer step and each trial μ evaluates the damped pseudoinverse step from (U, Σ, V) without refactoring J(x). With QRFactorization, LUFactorization, LDLTFactorization or CholeskyFactorization (or when scaling = :diagonal), [J(x) | -F(x)] is reduced once per outer step to an upper-triangular (R₀, c₀) and each trial μ copies that pair and eliminates only its own damping rows √μ D by Givens rotations, without forming J(x)ᴴ J(x); for a LeastSquaresProblem the rows of J(x) past its n columns are eliminated into the same n × n pair. This reduction reads no symmetry, so it serves the Hessian of a StationarityProblem as any other J(x). That path indexes scalars and runs on a host array: an init on a device array iterate raises an ArgumentError for it. Neither path reads a sparse factor, so an init on a non-static array iterate whose problem carries a sparse jacobian_prototype raises an ArgumentError too. A μ below 4 eps of the working type, which only an initial μ₀ below the schedule's floor can be, is taken as no damping: the truncated Newton step, the μ → 0 limit.\n\nA solve starts at μ₀. A trial is accepted when ρ > ρ_accept, and an accepted step sets μ to max(μ · μ_decrease, max(μ_min, 4 eps(R))). A rejected trial, or one whose step is not finite, sets μ to max(μ · μ_increase, max(μ_min, 4 eps(R))) and solves again within the same step, with no new Jacobian, until a trial is accepted or μ > μ_max, which returns STALLED with the iterate kept. A step accepted at a μ above μ₀ sets limited in its StepInfo. The parameters satisfy 0 < μ_min ≤ μ₀ ≤ μ_max < Inf, 0 < μ_decrease < 1 < μ_increase, 0 ≤ ρ_accept < 1 and scaling ∈ (:none, :diagonal), else the constructor raises an ArgumentError; ToReal converts them to the real type of the iterate. Newton(; globalization = LevenbergMarquardt(), reuse = k) raises an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LevenbergMarquardt"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/composition/#composition","page":"Composition and preconditioning","text":"A combinator is a nonlinear method whose members are nonlinear methods. It enters the loop through the same init_state, start!!, step!! and finish!! as Newton and Picard, so a combinator runs in each regime that its members run in, and combinators nest. The composition follows Brune, Knepley, Smith and Tu (2015). The last two rows of the table are the preconditioning methods: RAS and LeftPreconditioned take a NonlinearProblem with a mutable vector iterate, so they run in R3 only.\n\ncombinator one outer step\nAdditive(m₁, m₂, …) each member steps from the same iterate; the candidates are combined with weights\nMultiplicative(m₁, m₂, …) the members step in sequence, each from the result of the last\nRepeat(m, k) k steps of m\nSwitch(m₁, m₂; threshold) m₁ until F(x) le textthreshold cdot F(x_0), then m₂ for the rest of the solve\nAnderson(m, depth) one step of m, accelerated by Type-II Anderson mixing\nBroyden(m, depth) one step of m, accelerated by Type-II Broyden multisecant updates\nRAS(partition) restricted additive Schwarz: local Newton solves on overlapping blocks\nLeftPreconditioned(outer, inner) outer on the residual y - N(y) of one step N of inner\n\nFallback(m₁, m₂, …) composes whole solves instead of steps. It has no step!!: its solve!! runs the solve of each member in turn, each from x₀, and stops at the first member that returns SUCCESS. So it is not a member of the combinators of the table.\n\nA combinator takes a NonlinearProblem or a StationarityProblem and refuses a LeastSquaresProblem. A combinator and a preconditioning method have no implicit differentiation. A combinator other than Repeat reports 0 in f_calls and jvp_calls: the members of a combinator of the table count only their own steps, and Fallback does not add the counts of the solves of its members.\n\nThe examples of this page use the fixed-point problem x = cos x:\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers\n\njulia> fp = NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x - cos(x));\n\njulia> root = 0.7390851332151607;","title":"Composition and preconditioning"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/composition/#Additive","page":"Composition and preconditioning","text":"Additive runs one step of each member from the same iterate x and forms x_+ = x + sum_i w_i (x_i - x). OptimalWeights chooses the weights that minimise the linearised residual F(x) + sum_i w_i (F(x_i) - F(x))_2, with a safeguard that falls back to the best candidate; FixedWeights holds them constant. A member whose step fails hard is left out of the combination.\n\njulia> x, status = solve(fp, 1.0, Additive(Newton(), Picard()));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && isapprox(x, root; atol = 1e-8)\ntrue\n\njulia> x, status = solve(fp, 1.0, Additive(Newton(), Picard(); weights = FixedWeights(0.5, 0.5)));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && isapprox(x, root; atol = 1e-6)\ntrue","title":"Additive"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/composition/#Multiplicative-and-Repeat","page":"Composition and preconditioning","text":"Multiplicative threads (x, F(x)) through its members in order within one outer step: Multiplicative(Picard(), Newton()) is a Picard step followed by a Newton step. Repeat(m, k) takes k steps of m per outer step. Between members, sync_merit passes the current merit to the line search of the next member.\n\njulia> x, status = solve(fp, 1.0, Multiplicative(Picard(), Newton()));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && isapprox(x, root; atol = 1e-8)\ntrue\n\njulia> x, status = solve(fp, 1.0, Repeat(Picard(), 3));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && isapprox(x, root; atol = 1e-6)\ntrue","title":"Multiplicative and Repeat"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/composition/#Switch","page":"Composition and preconditioning","text":"Switch(first, second; threshold = 1e-2) runs first until the residual has dropped by the factor threshold, then latches to second. The common pattern is a robust method with a large convergence region first, and Newton for the fast local convergence after it:\n\njulia> x, status = solve(fp, 1.0, Switch(Anderson(), Newton(); threshold = 1e-2));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && isapprox(x, root; atol = 1e-10)\ntrue","title":"Switch"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/composition/#Anderson-and-Broyden","page":"Composition and preconditioning","text":"Anderson(member, m) accelerates the fixed-point map of member with Type-II Anderson mixing of depth m (Walker and Ni 2011). The history of differences is filtered by the length and angle filters of Pollock and Rebholz (2023) and the weight solve is regularised. The history restarts when the residual grows, and on a failed solve the iterate with the smallest residual is restored. Anderson(m) wraps Picard().\n\nBroyden shares that history, its filters and its safeguards, and solves the lower-triangular Type-II Broyden multisecant system of Fang and Saad (2009) by forward substitution instead of the full normal equations. Broyden(m) wraps Picard(; damping = 0.5).\n\njulia> x, status = solve(fp, 1.0, Anderson(Picard(), 2));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && isapprox(x, root; atol = 1e-8)\ntrue\n\njulia> x, status = solve(fp, 1.0, Broyden(2));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && isapprox(x, root; atol = 1e-8)\ntrue\n\nIn R1 the depth is 1 ≤ m ≤ 4 and the state is isbits; in R3 any depth applies, with preallocated n times m difference matrices.","title":"Anderson and Broyden"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/composition/#Restricted-additive-Schwarz:-RAS","page":"Composition and preconditioning","text":"RAS(partition) is the one-level restricted additive Schwarz map of nonlinear preconditioning. A BlockPartition splits 1:n into overlapping subdomains, each with an owner set; the owner sets are disjoint and cover 1:n. One step restricts the iterate to each subdomain, takes up to max_iterations local Newton steps with that block's own factor, and writes back the owned indices only. The problem supplies one local residual F_i!(f_i, x_i, x, p) per block through the block_residuals keyword of NonlinearProblem, which reads the couplings outside the block from the frozen full iterate x. A block without its own Jacobian is differentiated with AutoForwardDiff().\n\njulia> n = 8;\n\njulia> cell(u, j) = u[j] * (1 + (j == 1 ? 0.0 : u[j - 1])^2 + (j == n ? 0.0 : u[j + 1])^2) - 1;\n\njulia> F!(r, x, p) = (for j in 1:n; r[j] = cell(x, j); end; r);\n\njulia> part = BlockPartition(n, 2; overlap = 1);\n\njulia> blocks = ntuple(2) do i\n           S = part.subdomains[i]\n           function (f, x_loc, x, p)\n               u = Vector{promote_type(eltype(x_loc), eltype(x))}(x)\n               u[S] .= x_loc\n               for (k, j) in enumerate(S)\n                   f[k] = cell(u, j)\n               end\n               return f\n           end\n       end;\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem(F!; block_residuals = blocks);\n\njulia> x_ras, status = solve(prob, zeros(n), RAS(part; max_iterations = 20, rtol = 1e-12));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS\ntrue\n\nWith rtol or atol positive, a local solve that does not converge ends the solve with MAXITERS, and ras_counts names the block.","title":"Restricted additive Schwarz: RAS"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/composition/#Nonlinear-preconditioning:-LeftPreconditioned","page":"Composition and preconditioning","text":"LeftPreconditioned(outer, inner) runs outer on the left-preconditioned residual r^ell(y) = y - N(y), where N is one step of inner. The zeros of r^ell are the fixed points of N. Every trial point of the globalization of outer costs one step of inner.\n\nLeftPreconditioned(Newton(), RAS(partition)) is RASPEN (Dolean, Gander, Kheriji, Kwok and Masson 2016). Its Jacobian is the exact form from the block factors of the last sweep, raspen_jvp! and raspen_jacobian!. RASPEN needs converged local solves.\nMultiplicative(RAS(partition), Newton()) is the composition of the same map with one Newton step, the nonlinear restricted additive Schwarz preconditioning of Cai and Li (2011).\nWith an inner method other than RAS, the derivative of r^ell is a central difference.\n\njulia> raspen = LeftPreconditioned(Newton(), RAS(part; max_iterations = 20, rtol = 1e-12));\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, zeros(n), raspen);\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && isapprox(x, x_ras; atol = 1e-7)\ntrue\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, zeros(n), Multiplicative(RAS(part; max_iterations = 2), Newton()));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && isapprox(x, x_ras; atol = 1e-7)\ntrue\n\nThe status of a preconditioned solve is that of the global residual F at the returned iterate.","title":"Nonlinear preconditioning: LeftPreconditioned"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/composition/#How-to-choose","page":"Composition and preconditioning","text":"A combinator changes the convergence region and the cost of a step, and the right choice depends on the problem. Some conditions under which each one applies:\n\nSwitch and Multiplicative with a cheap first member apply where Newton alone does not converge from the start but converges near the solution.\nAnderson and Broyden apply to a fixed-point iteration whose map is cheap and whose Jacobian is expensive or unavailable.\nAdditive with OptimalWeights combines methods whose steps are good in different directions.\nRAS and LeftPreconditioned apply to problems with local nonlinearities that a partition into blocks isolates, where a global Newton step is slowed by those blocks.","title":"How to choose"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/composition/#References","page":"Composition and preconditioning","text":"P. R. Brune, M. G. Knepley, B. F. Smith and X. Tu, Composing Scalable Nonlinear Algebraic Solvers, SIAM Review 57(4), 535–565 (2015). doi:10.1137/130936725\nX.-C. Cai and X. Li, Inexact Newton Methods with Restricted Additive Schwarz Based Nonlinear Elimination for Problems with High Local Nonlinearity, SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 33(2), 746–762 (2011). doi:10.1137/080736272\nV. Dolean, M. J. Gander, W. Kheriji, F. Kwok and R. Masson, Nonlinear Preconditioning: How to Use a Nonlinear Schwarz Method to Precondition Newton's Method, SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 38(6), A3357–A3380 (2016). doi:10.1137/15M102887X\nH.-r. Fang and Y. Saad, Two classes of multisecant methods for nonlinear acceleration, Numerical Linear Algebra with Applications 16(3), 197–221 (2009). doi:10.1002/nla.617\nS. Pollock and L. G. Rebholz, Filtering for Anderson Acceleration, SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 45(4), A1571–A1590 (2023). doi:10.1137/22M1536741\nH. F. Walker and P. Ni, Anderson Acceleration for Fixed-Point Iterations, SIAM Journal on Numerical Analysis 49(4), 1715–1735 (2011). doi:10.1137/10078356X","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/linear/#linear","page":"Linear solvers","text":"The linear solver of a Newton step is its linsolve. A linear-solver method is a LinearMethod: either a factorisation, which the state factorises into a factor of the type that the iterate selects, or the matrix-free GMRES.","title":"Linear solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/linear/#Factorisation-methods","page":"Linear solvers","text":"method factorisation matrix\nLUFactorization LU with partial pivoting square\nLDLTFactorization Bunch–Kaufman L D L^mathsfT or L D L^mathsfH with 1 times 1 and 2 times 2 pivots symmetric or Hermitian, reads the lower triangle of a real matrix and both triangles of a complex one\nCholeskyFactorization Cholesky L L^mathsfH without pivoting (LAPACK potrf on the host) Hermitian or real symmetric positive definite, reads the lower triangle and the real part of the diagonal\nQRFactorization Householder QR square, or m times n with m ge n for least squares\nSVDFactorization one-sided Jacobi SVD (static, device) or LAPACK gesvd (host) square or m ge n, rank-revealing\n\nEach method takes an optional factorisation precision: LUFactorization() factorises in the working type of the problem, and LUFactorization(Float32) in Float32. The precision is the type parameter TF of the method, so the method stays isbits and enters a kernel. The page Mixed precision describes when a lower precision applies.\n\nThe type of the iterate selects the factor:\n\niterate LU LDLT Cholesky QR SVD\nNumber, SVector (R1, R2) StaticLU StaticLDLT StaticCholesky StaticQR, StaticRectQR StaticSVD, StaticRectSVD\nhost Vector (R3) DenseLU DenseLDLT DenseCholesky DenseQR DenseSVD\nGPU array (R3) DeviceLU DeviceLDLT none: ArgumentError DeviceQR DeviceSVD\n\nThe static factors are scalar loops over SMatrix data with Int32 pivots. They return new values, isbits for an isbits element type, so they run in one GPU thread.\nThe dense factors call LAPACK (getrf, sytrf/hetrf, potrf, geqrf, gesvd) on buffers that init allocates once.\nThe device factors are KernelAbstractions kernels: panel pivoting and trailing updates for LU, column-norm reductions and reflector application for QR, the Bunch–Kaufman pivots for LDLT, and parallel one-sided Jacobi sweeps for the SVD. On CUDA and Metal, vendor libraries replace some of them; see CPU and GPU backends.\n\nA numerical failure of a factorisation does not throw: a zero pivot gives the status SINGULAR. A configuration or pattern error can throw an ArgumentError, for example a sparse factor whose pattern the regularisation changes. The SVD of an m times n matrix truncates at the rank cutoff sqrtmvarepsilon(T_F)sigma_1, or at mathrmrtolsigma_1 with SVDFactorization(; rtol), so its solve is the minimum-norm step on the numerical range. The bounds that the test suite verifies for each factor are on the page Numerical Stability and Backward-Error Bounds.\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers, StaticArrays\n\njulia> F(x, p) = SVector(x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 - p, x[1] - x[2]);\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem(F);\n\njulia> results = [solve(prob, SVector(1.0, 0.5), Newton(; linsolve = m), 2.0)[1]\n                  for m in (LUFactorization(), QRFactorization(), SVDFactorization())];\n\njulia> all(x -> x ≈ SVector(1.0, 1.0), results)\ntrue\n\nLDLTFactorization reads only the lower triangle, so it serves a symmetric Jacobian, such as the Hessian block of a StationarityProblem with E == U:\n\njulia> S(x, p) = (x[1]^2 + x[2]^2) / 2 + x[1]^4 / 4 - p * x[1] + x[1] * x[2];\n\njulia> x, status = solve(StationarityProblem(S), [0.5, 0.5], Newton(; linsolve = LDLTFactorization()), 1.0);\n\njulia> x ≈ [1.0, -1.0]\ntrue\n\nWith an SVDFactorization, the status reports the numerical rank of the last factor in rank, together with nullnorm and gradratio, which classify a stall; see Status and stopping test.","title":"Factorisation methods"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/linear/#Choosing-a-factorisation","page":"Linear solvers","text":"LUFactorization for a general square Jacobian.\nLDLTFactorization for a symmetric or Hermitian Jacobian; it needs about half the work of LU and handles an indefinite matrix with its 2 times 2 pivots.\nCholeskyFactorization for a Hermitian or real symmetric Jacobian that is positive definite at every iterate, such as the Hessian of a StationarityProblem with E == U, or J + mu I with a Hermitian positive semidefinite J and mu  0. It takes no pivots, and a Jacobian that is not positive definite gives SINGULAR, with the order of the first leading minor that is not positive in info. It runs on a host Array and in R1 and R2; in R3 a GPU array raises an ArgumentError.\nQRFactorization for a least-squares problem, and where an orthogonal factorisation is wanted.\nSVDFactorization for a rank-deficient or nearly singular Jacobian. It costs more than the others, and it gives the rank diagnostics.","title":"Choosing a factorisation"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/linear/#sparse","page":"Linear solvers","text":"When Sparspak is loaded, the package extension GeometricSolversSparspakExt provides a sparse LU for a host Vector iterate whose problem carries a SparseMatrixCSC{<:Any, Int} pattern, with an LUFactorization in the element type of the iterate. The first factorisation runs the symbolic ordering and factorisation, and every later step reuses them. The pattern comes from an explicit jacobian_prototype, or, when SparseMatrixColorings is loaded, from a sparsity detector of an AutoSparse back end (see Automatic differentiation).\n\nThe Jacobian then has to fill a sparse matrix: give the problem an analytic J! that writes the nonzeros of the prototype, or give Newton an AutoSparse back end.\n\njulia> using SparseArrays, Sparspak\n\njulia> n = 8;\n\njulia> function Fs!(r, u, p)\n           for j in 1:n\n               l = j == 1 ? 0.0 : u[j - 1]\n               q = j == n ? 0.0 : u[j + 1]\n               r[j] = u[j] * (1 + l^2 + q^2) - 1\n           end\n           return r\n       end;\n\njulia> function Js!(J, u, p)\n           rows, vals = rowvals(J), nonzeros(J)\n           for c in 1:n, k in nzrange(J, c)\n               j = rows[k]\n               l = j == 1 ? 0.0 : u[j - 1]\n               q = j == n ? 0.0 : u[j + 1]\n               vals[k] = c == j ? 1 + l^2 + q^2 : 2 * u[j] * u[c]\n           end\n           return J\n       end;\n\njulia> P = spdiagm(-1 => ones(n - 1), 0 => ones(n), 1 => ones(n - 1));\n\njulia> solver = init(NonlinearProblem(Fs!, Js!; jacobian_prototype = P), zeros(n), Newton());\n\njulia> x, status = solve!(solver);\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS\ntrue\n\njulia> nameof(typeof(solver.state.fac))\n:SparspakLU\n\nUMFPACKFactorization gives the sparse LU of UMFPACK, from SparseArrays, on the same pattern for a host Vector{Float64} or Vector{ComplexF64} iterate, with no package to load; it raises ArgumentError at init for another element type and for a problem with no sparse pattern. With CUDA and CUDSS loaded, an LUFactorization on a CuVector iterate with a CuSparseMatrixCSR jacobian_prototype and an analytic J! gives the sparse LU of cuDSS. QRFactorization, SVDFactorization, a factorisation precision other than the element type of the iterate, and every other complex or device iterate have no sparse factor. The section Factorisation and solve of the API lists which configurations take a detected pattern and which raise an error.","title":"Sparse direct solves"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/linear/#Matrix-free-Newton–Krylov:-GMRES","page":"Linear solvers","text":"GMRES(; maxiter = 30, restart = nothing, forcing, preconditioner, jvp_eltype) is a linear method without a factor. A Newton with it holds no n times n Jacobian: each inner iteration takes one Jacobian–vector product, from automatic differentiation or from a JVPFunction that the caller writes. The step solves J M^-1 u = -F(x) to the relative tolerance eta_k of the forcing term and returns d = M^-1 u.\n\njulia> F!(y, x, p) = (y[1] = x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 - p; y[2] = x[1] - x[2]; y);\n\njulia> jvp!(Jv, x, v, p) = (Jv[1] = 2x[1] * v[1] + 2x[2] * v[2]; Jv[2] = v[1] - v[2]; Jv);\n\njulia> method = Newton(; linsolve = GMRES(; maxiter = 10), jacobian = JVPFunction(jvp!));\n\njulia> solver = init(NonlinearProblem(F!), [1.0, 0.5], method, 2.0);\n\njulia> x, status = solve!(solver);\n\njulia> x ≈ [1.0, 1.0] && status.jvp_calls == jvp_calls(solver) > 0\ntrue\n\nThe forcing term sets eta_k. EisenstatWalkerChoice2 is the default and EisenstatWalkerChoice1 the other adaptive choice of Eisenstat and Walker (1996); FixedForcing(η) holds eta constant. An adaptive term solves loosely far from the root and tightly near it, which saves inner iterations where a tight solve buys nothing.\n\nA right preconditioner M is any object with methods of refresh!, called once per Newton step, and apply_preconditioner!, called before every product. A stored Jacobian factor that ldiv! applies is the frozen-Jacobian preconditioner of a Newton–Krylov method. The example below takes the diagonal of the Jacobian instead:\n\njulia> struct Jacobi\n           d::Vector{Float64}\n       end\n\njulia> GeometricSolvers.refresh!(P::Jacobi, prob, x, Fx, p) = (P.d .= (2x[1], -1.0); P);\n\njulia> GeometricSolvers.apply_preconditioner!(z, P::Jacobi, v) = (z .= v ./ P.d; z);\n\njulia> method = Newton(; linsolve = GMRES(), preconditioner = Jacobi(zeros(2)));\n\njulia> x, status = solve(NonlinearProblem(F!), [1.0, 0.5], method, 2.0);\n\njulia> x ≈ [1.0, 1.0]\ntrue\n\njvp_eltype = Float32 evaluates the product in Float32 and keeps the residual and the iterate in the working precision: a defect-correction solve. f_calls and jvp_calls count the evaluations of the last solve, and the status holds the same counts.\n\nGMRES needs an AbstractVector iterate, on the host or on a device. A Number or a static iterate has no Krylov basis here, and a LeastSquaresProblem has no square system. It takes no factor reuse and no Refinement.","title":"Matrix-free Newton–Krylov: GMRES"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/linear/#Eigensolver","page":"Linear solvers","text":"jacobi_eigen is a function on a static Hermitian or real symmetric matrix, not a linear method. It computes the eigenvalues and the unitary eigenvectors by cyclic two-sided Jacobi, on the rotations of the Jacobi SVD, and returns (values, vectors, status), all isbits, so it runs in a kernel.\n\njulia> A = SMatrix{2, 2}(2.0, 1.0, 1.0, 2.0);\n\njulia> values, vectors, status = jacobi_eigen(A);\n\njulia> sort(values) ≈ [1.0, 3.0] && status === SUCCESS\ntrue","title":"Eigensolver"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/linear/#References","page":"Linear solvers","text":"S. C. Eisenstat and H. F. Walker, Choosing the Forcing Terms in an Inexact Newton Method, SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 17(1), 16–32 (1996). doi:10.1137/0917003","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api/linear/#Linear-solver-methods","page":"Linear solvers","text":"The factorisation precision TF of a linear-solver method is a type parameter. Nothing selects the working type of the problem, a real float type a lower (or higher) precision, and a marker a vendor tensor-core path. These names are exported.","title":"Linear-solver methods"},{"category":"section","location":"api/linear/#Factorisation-and-solve","page":"Linear solvers","text":"A linear-solver method enters a solve through init_linear (and dense_factor), factorize!! and ldiv!!. For a static matrix (R1) factorize!! and ldiv!! return new values, isbits for an isbits element type, so they run inside a kernel: StaticLU is LU with partial pivoting and Int32 pivots, StaticLDLT is Bunch–Kaufman L D Lᵀ / L D Lᴴ with 1×1 and 2×2 diagonal blocks, StaticCholesky is Cholesky L Lᴴ without pivoting, StaticQR and StaticRectQR are Householder QR without column pivoting, and StaticSVD and StaticRectSVD are a one-sided Jacobi singular value decomposition whose solve truncates at the rank cutoff √n · eps(TF) · σ₁ for a square matrix and √m · eps(TF) · σ₁ for a rectangular one, m its row count, which is what makes a rank-revealing solve finite. SVDFactorization(; rtol) sets the cutoff to rtol · σ₁ on every route, R1 and R3. For a vector state (R3) with no sparse pattern (jacobian_pattern(prob, jacprep) === nothing), init_linear pre-allocates DenseLU, DenseLDLT, DenseCholesky, DenseQR or DenseSVD on a host Array and DeviceLU, DeviceLDLT, DeviceQR or DeviceSVD on an AbstractGPUArray, where a CholeskyFactorization raises an ArgumentError, and factorize!! and ldiv!! reuse its buffers in place: through LAPACK on the host, and on a device through the GeometricSolversMetalExt (Metal MPS lu! and ldiv! on MtlMatrix{Float32} and MtlVector{Float32}, with every MPS failure status reported as a singular factor) and GeometricSolversCUDAExt (CUDA cuSOLVER getrf!, getrs!, geqrf!, ormqr! with cuBLAS trsv!, and gesvdj! on CuMatrix{T} and CuVector{T} for T <: LinearAlgebra.BlasFloat, falling back to ka_factorize_svd! where gesvdj! does not converge, and calling it in place of gesvdj! where the rtol cutoff lies below √m · eps(real(T)), m the number of rows) package extensions where available and through KernelAbstractions otherwise. When Sparspak is loaded and jacobian_pattern is a SparseMatrixCSC{<:Any, Int} on a host Vector{T} (T <: AbstractFloat) — from an explicit jacobian_prototype, or detected automatically by GeometricSolversSparseADExt when SparseMatrixColorings is loaded — the GeometricSolversSparspakExt package extension pre-allocates a SparspakLU workspace and unfactorised Sparspak.SparseSolver in init_linear for an LUFactorization whose factor element type is T (LUFactorization() or LUFactorization(T)); the first factorize!! call runs symbolic ordering and symbolic factorisation, and subsequent factorize!! and ldiv!! calls reuse them across Newton steps. UMFPACKFactorization builds the sparse factor UMFPACKLU on such a pattern on a host Vector{Float64} or Vector{ComplexF64}, with no package to load: its first factorize!! runs UMFPACK's symbolic analysis, and every later one reuses it. It raises ArgumentError at init for any other element type and for a solve with no sparse pattern, such as one on a static or scalar iterate, or one whose detected pattern Newton does not use: under LevenbergMarquardt, and under regularization ≠ 0 where the pattern does not hold the whole structural diagonal. These two configurations have a host sparse factor, and has_sparse_factor names them. A detected pattern is used only where has_sparse_factor holds and, under a Newton regularization ≠ 0, only where the pattern holds the whole structural diagonal. Everywhere else — QRFactorization, SVDFactorization, mixed precision such as LUFactorization(Float64) on a Float32 iterate, an LUFactorization on a complex iterate, or no Sparspak — a detected pattern gives the dense factor and a dense Jacobian buffer, as a solve with no pattern does. An explicit jacobian_prototype outside these configurations and the cuDSS one below raises an error in init_linear or at the first factorize!!. When CUDA and CUDSS are loaded, the GeometricSolversCUDSSExt package extension builds a cuDSS factor for an LUFactorization in the element type of a CuVector{T} iterate (T <: LinearAlgebra.BlasFloat) with an explicit CuSparseMatrixCSR jacobian_prototype; its first factorize!! runs the cuDSS analysis and factorisation, and every later one the refactorisation. A LeastSquaresProblem takes no detected pattern, because the sparse factors are square: its m × n factor is dense. factorize!! states what a singular matrix gives: an SVD factor records its numerical rank in rank apart from its status, and exact_solve tells Newton whether the solve is exact or truncated; singular_values gives the singular values of a StaticSVD or StaticRectSVD. These names are not exported and are reached as GeometricSolvers.name.","title":"Factorisation and solve"},{"category":"section","location":"api/linear/#Matrix-free-Krylov-solves","page":"Linear solvers","text":"GMRES is a LinearMethod with no factor. The Jacobian enters a step only through a Jacobian–vector product, so a Newton with it holds no n × n Jacobian (state.J === nothing) and serves a system that no array can hold the Jacobian of: a small system whose every column costs a full evaluation of an expensive residual, or a device array. Its step solves J M^-1 u = -F(x) and returns d = M^-1 u to the relative tolerance that the forcing term returns.\n\nThe forcing term sets that tolerance. EisenstatWalkerChoice2 is the default, and EisenstatWalkerChoice1 and FixedForcing are the alternatives; a term is an AbstractForcingTerm, and compute_forcing is its rule. The right preconditioner of GMRES(; preconditioner = P) is refreshed by refresh! once per Newton step and applied by apply_preconditioner! before every product. A matrix-free problem supplies its own product as JVPFunction(jvp!) in the jacobian field of Newton, and the solve reports the exact counts of its residual and product evaluations in f_calls/jvp_calls and in the f_calls and jvp_calls fields of SolverStatus. A combinator other than Repeat reports 0 in those fields: the members of a step-level combinator count only their own steps, and Fallback does not add the counts of the solves of its members. GMRES(; maxiter) caps the inner iterations of one Newton step, and max_inner_iterations gives the largest count of the last solve.\n\nThe Arnoldi process of GMRES orthogonalises each new Krylov vector by classical Gram–Schmidt with a second pass (CGS2), which takes two reductions over the basis per inner iteration, and one more, or three where rnorm scales, on an iteration where the norm of the new vector is formed explicitly. GMRES(; mgs = true) selects modified Gram–Schmidt, which takes j + 1 reductions at inner iteration j; on a device, each reduction is a synchronisation. The inner GMRES of a GMRESRefinement orthogonalises by modified Gram–Schmidt whatever GMRES selects.\n\nThe matrix-free DogLeg runs on the Arnoldi relation of an unrestarted GMRES only (restart == maxiter), and a Newton with a preconditioner or a restarted GMRES under it raises an ArgumentError. These names are exported.","title":"Matrix-free Krylov solves"},{"category":"section","location":"api/linear/#Eigensolver","page":"Linear solvers","text":"jacobi_eigen is a function on a matrix and not a linear-solver method: cyclic two-sided Jacobi for a Hermitian or a real symmetric matrix, on the rotations and the round-robin ordering of the Jacobi SVD with a Frobenius-norm pair floor eps(R) * ‖H‖_F, for the case jacobi_eigen(im * M) of a real skew M that the ProjectedSkew exponential algorithm of GeometricOptimizers needs. It adds no method to LinearAlgebra.eigen, because a method of eigen for a StaticArrays type would be type piracy.","title":"Eigensolver"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.LUFactorization","page":"Linear solvers","text":"LUFactorization()\nLUFactorization(TF)\n\nLU factorisation with partial pivoting. LUFactorization() factorises in the working type of the problem, LUFactorization(Float32) in Float32, and LUFactorization(TF32()) carries a tensor-core marker (see TF32).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LUFactorization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.LDLTFactorization","page":"Linear solvers","text":"LDLTFactorization()\nLDLTFactorization(TF)\n\nBunch–Kaufman L D Lᵀ / L D Lᴴ factorisation with 1 × 1 and 2 × 2 diagonal pivot blocks for symmetric and Hermitian matrices, with the factorisation precision of LUFactorization. The factorisation reads the lower triangle and does not check that a real matrix is symmetric. A complex matrix whose diagonal entries are real to 64 eps of each entry is factorised as L D Lᴴ where each off-diagonal pair is Hermitian to 64 eps of the pair, or where its largest Hermitian mismatch |A[i, j] - conj(A[j, i])| is no larger than its largest symmetric mismatch |A[i, j] - A[j, i]|, and as the complex symmetric L D Lᵀ otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LDLTFactorization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.CholeskyFactorization","page":"Linear solvers","text":"CholeskyFactorization()\nCholeskyFactorization(TF)\n\nCholesky factorisation L Lᴴ without pivoting, for a Hermitian (real symmetric) positive definite matrix, with the factorisation precision of LUFactorization. It reads the lower triangle and the real part of the diagonal, and does not check that the matrix is Hermitian. A matrix that is not positive definite in the factorisation precision gives info = k, the order of its first leading minor that is not positive, and SINGULAR, without throwing; a non-finite entry anywhere in the matrix gives info = 1. A host Array iterate factorises with LAPACK potrf for a LinearAlgebra.BlasFloat element type and with a generic loop for the others; a static iterate always takes the generic loop. A device array iterate raises an ArgumentError, because the factorisation has no device factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.CholeskyFactorization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.QRFactorization","page":"Linear solvers","text":"QRFactorization()\nQRFactorization(TF)\n\nHouseholder QR factorisation, with the factorisation precision of LUFactorization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.QRFactorization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.SVDFactorization","page":"Linear solvers","text":"SVDFactorization(; rtol = nothing)\nSVDFactorization(TF; rtol = nothing)\n\nSingular value decomposition, with the factorisation precision of LUFactorization.\n\nThe factor counts a singular value at or below rtol · σ₁ as zero: its rank is the number of singular values above that cutoff, and its solve is the pseudo-inverse truncated at that rank. rtol = nothing is the cutoff √m · eps(R) · σ₁, with m the number of rows of the matrix and R the real type of the factor; it is also the tolerance of the Jacobi sweep of a static factor and of the own-kernel device factor, and the only cutoff at the level of the rounding noise. rtol = 0 keeps every nonzero singular value, also one of rounding noise.\n\nrtol is stored in real(TF) for a float TF, and in Float64 for TF = Nothing and for a precision marker; the factor converts it to its own real type, so that a Float64 rtol that rounds to 1 there gives rank 0 and the zero step in a direct factorize!!. ToReal converts rtol to its type and keeps TF. A stored rtol outside [0, 1), or not finite, raises an ArgumentError, also where ToReal rounds it to 1 in a solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.SVDFactorization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.UMFPACKFactorization","page":"Linear solvers","text":"UMFPACKFactorization()\n\nSparse LU factorisation of a SparseMatrixCSC Jacobian through UMFPACK, from SparseArrays, in the working type of the problem, which is Float64 or ComplexF64 on a host Vector. The Jacobian pattern is a SparseMatrixCSC{<:Any, Int} jacobian_prototype, or the pattern that the sparsity detector of an AutoSparse back end finds. init raises ArgumentError for another element type, such as Float32 or ComplexF32, for another iterate, such as a static or scalar one, and for a solve with no sparse pattern. A detected pattern that Newton does not use counts as none: under LevenbergMarquardt, and under regularization ≠ 0 where the pattern does not hold the whole structural diagonal. The first factorisation runs the symbolic analysis, and every later factorisation of the same factor reuses it (see UMFPACKLU). UMFPACK is distributed under the GPL with SparseArrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.UMFPACKFactorization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.TF32","page":"Linear solvers","text":"TF32()\n\nThe factorisation-precision marker for NVIDIA's TensorFloat-32 tensor cores. It is a type only: no factorisation method accepts a marker, so a marker selects no code path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.TF32"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.FP16","page":"Linear solvers","text":"FP16()\n\nThe factorisation-precision marker for half-precision tensor cores. See TF32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.FP16"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.BF16","page":"Linear solvers","text":"BF16()\n\nThe factorisation-precision marker for bfloat16 tensor cores. See TF32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.BF16"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.init_linear","page":"Linear solvers","text":"init_linear(method::LinearMethod, pattern, x::AbstractVector)\n\nBuild the linear factor for method on x: the dense host factor DenseLU, DenseQR, DenseSVD, DenseLDLT or DenseCholesky or device factor DeviceLU, DeviceQR, DeviceSVD or DeviceLDLT when pattern === nothing, or a sparse factor when pattern is a sparse matrix prototype: an extension's, or the UMFPACKLU of UMFPACKFactorization, which raises ArgumentError for pattern === nothing. A CholeskyFactorization has no device factor and raises an ArgumentError on a device array.\n\n\n\n\n\ninit_linear(method::LinearMethod, pattern, x::AbstractVector, m_rows)\n\nBuild the linear factor for method on x for a Jacobian of m_rows rows: the square factor of the three-argument method for m_rows === nothing, and for an Int the rectangular m_rows × length(x) factor of a LeastSquaresProblem, a DenseQR, DenseSVD, DeviceQR or DeviceSVD with a right-hand side buffer of m_rows entries, because its solve reads m_rows residual entries and writes length(x) step entries. Whether m_rows is an Int decides the buffer, so a factor's type is inferred from its call. LUFactorization, LDLTFactorization and CholeskyFactorization take the square factor whatever m_rows is: they factorise no rectangular matrix, and LevenbergMarquardt, the one globalization that takes them on a least-squares problem, solves its own stacked system and never factorises that factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.init_linear"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.has_sparse_factor","page":"Linear solvers","text":"has_sparse_factor(method::LinearMethod, x)\n\nWhether init_linear builds a sparse factor of method on the iterate x from a detected Jacobian pattern: false by default. It is true for UMFPACKFactorization on a Vector{Float64} or Vector{ComplexF64}, and the GeometricSolversSparspakExt extension returns true for an LUFactorization whose factor element type is T on a Vector{T}, T <: AbstractFloat. Where it is false, a detected pattern gives the dense factor, as a solve with no pattern does; an explicit jacobian_prototype does not consult it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.has_sparse_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.dense_factor","page":"Linear solvers","text":"dense_factor(method::LinearMethod, prob, x::AbstractVector, p)\n\nBuild the linear factor for method on x using jacobian_prototype(prob), with the rows of the Jacobian of prob: m × n for a LeastSquaresProblem of residual length m, and n × n otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.dense_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.factorize!!","page":"Linear solvers","text":"factorize!!(fac, method, J)\n\nFactorise the matrix J with the linear-solver method method and return the factors.\n\nFor a static matrix (R1), fac is nothing or an earlier factor of the same type, whose value is not used, and the result is a new StaticLU, StaticQR, StaticSVD, StaticLDLT or StaticCholesky for a square J, and a StaticRectQR or StaticRectSVD for the QR or SVD of an M × N matrix with M > N (the LU, the LDLᵀ and the Cholesky factorisation have no rectangular method). J is converted to the element type of the factors, factor_eltype. Where that is not the element type of J, J is first divided by the power of two 2^e that brings its largest |re| or |im| into [1, 2), or as close as the exponent range allows, and e is kept in the factor, so that the overall scale of J neither overflows nor underflows in the factor type (for Cholesky, e is taken from the lower triangle and the real part of the diagonal alone). A zero or non-finite J gives e = 0. A singular J does not throw. LU records the first zero pivot in info, QR the first zero diagonal entry of R, LDLᵀ the first zero pivot block (or 1 for a non-finite entry), Cholesky the order of the first leading minor that is not positive (or 1 for a non-finite entry), and returncode then gives SINGULAR. QR is not rank-revealing: a singular J whose R has no exact zero on the diagonal gives SUCCESS, and two equal rows, as a rule, give no exact zero. The SVD records its numerical rank in rank, the number of singular values above the cutoff rtol · σ₁ of SVDFactorization, by default √m · eps(real(TF)) · σ₁, m the number of rows of J, and a rank deficiency gives info == 0 and SUCCESS; its info is nonzero only where the sweep did not converge or a singular value is not finite, and rank is then 0. The SVD is prescaled at the working precision too, because the sweep squares the entries.\n\nFor a dense or device factor (R3), fac is a DenseLU, DenseQR, DenseSVD, DenseLDLT, DenseCholesky, DeviceLU, DeviceQR, DeviceSVD or DeviceLDLT built by init_linear. J is written into fac.factors, divided by 2^e as for a static matrix where its element type is not that of fac.factors, and fac.factors is factorised in place; J === fac.factors is factorised as it is. A DenseSVD or DeviceSVD divides J by 2^e unconditionally, J === fac.factors too: into fac.factors on the host before gesvd, and into fac.U on a device before the one-sided Jacobi sweep. For DenseSVD, info is 1 where J has a non-finite entry, which is refused before gesvd and leaves U, S and VT unwritten, gesvd's non-convergence code where it fails, or 1 where a singular value of J / 2^e is not finite; for DeviceSVD it is 1 where the Jacobi sweep reaches its sweep cap without converging or meets a non-finite entry; rank is the numerical rank as for a static matrix. An Inf in a dense J does not throw; the SVD, LDLᵀ and Cholesky factors then report SINGULAR, and the step that the LU or QR factors give is not specified.\n\nFor a sparse factor (R3), which init_linear builds from a sparse jacobian_prototype — the Sparspak factor of an extension and the UMFPACKLU of UMFPACKFactorization from a SparseMatrixCSC, and the cuDSS factor of an extension from a CuSparseMatrixCSR — J must have the sparsity pattern of that prototype, or ArgumentError is raised. A non-finite entry of J gives info = 1 and SINGULAR without throwing. So do a zero pivot and a non-finite factor of Sparspak, a singular matrix in UMFPACK's status, and a nonzero cuDSS info.\n\nA precision marker such as TF32 has no method for a static matrix: the call raises a MethodError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.factorize!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.ldiv!!","page":"Linear solvers","text":"ldiv!!(d, fac, r)\n\nSolve A d = r with the factors fac of A, and return d in the element type of r. For a rectangular A of size m × n with m > n (the QR and SVD factors of a LeastSquaresProblem), r has m entries, d has n, and d is the least-squares solution of A d = r, the minimum-norm one where an SVD factor has numerical rank below n.\n\nFor a static factor (R1), d is nothing and the result is a new SVector. For a dense factor (R3), d is the vector buffer written in place and returned. Where the element type of r is the element type of the factors, the solve runs directly in it. There a finite, nonzero r whose largest |re| or |im| is within a few bits of floatmax (2 + ⌈log2 N⌉ bits for N entries) is divided by the smallest power of two 2^k that leaves that headroom; any other r is solved as it is. This keeps Qᴴ r and Uᴴ r, which the QR and SVD solves form first, finite. The SVD, whose factors are those of J / 2^e, then divides the coordinates Uᴴ r by its singular values times the smallest power of two 2^t that keeps the coordinates the same headroom below floatmax; t is 0 wherever they are already below it, and for LU and QR. The step is multiplied by 2^(k + t - e), in one rounding, with e the exponent of factorize!!, so that the SVD step does not overflow where it is representable. The LU and QR solves can still overflow in the triangular solve for such an r. An entry of r within k bits of the subnormal range can lose those bits to the division, and a coordinate within t bits of it to the SVD's. The step still overflows on every route where the solution itself exceeds floatmax. Where the element types differ, a finite, nonzero r is scaled by s = maximum(abs, r), converted to the factor type, solved, converted back and multiplied by s (Kelley 2023, Newton's Method in Three Precisions, Pacific J. Optim., doi:10.61208/pjo-2023-030, eqs. 2.17–2.18 of arXiv:2307.16051v4). Normalisation prevents overflow from the overall right-hand-side scale in a lower factor precision; entries small relative to the largest entry can still underflow. The scale takes no squares and overflows only where an entry's absolute value does. In the mixed-type path, a zero r returns a zero step without solving, and an Inf or NaN in r gives a non-finite step. In both paths the step is multiplied by 2^-e, the power of two that factorize!! divided J by, in the element type of r. The SVD's solve leaves out the directions that its rank cutoff counted as zero, so that a rank-deficient system gives a finite, truncated step rather than an enormous one; an SVD factor with info ≠ 0 has rank 0 and gives the zero step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ldiv!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.factor_eltype","page":"Linear solvers","text":"factor_eltype(method, T)\n\nThe element type of the factors of a working element type T: T when the method's factorisation precision is Nothing, the precision TF for a real T, and complex(TF) for a complex T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.factor_eltype"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.StaticLU","page":"Linear solvers","text":"StaticLU{N, TF, L}\n\nThe LU factors with partial pivoting of an N × N static matrix, in element type TF: factors holds U on and above the diagonal and the multipliers of the unit lower L below it, ipiv the row exchanged with row k at step k, info the index of the first zero pivot, or 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by. L = N * N makes the SMatrix field concrete, so the factor is isbits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StaticLU"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.StaticLDLT","page":"Linear solvers","text":"StaticLDLT{N, TF, L}\n\nThe Bunch–Kaufman L D Lᵀ / L D Lᴴ factors of an N × N static matrix, in element type TF: factors holds the 1 × 1 and 2 × 2 diagonal blocks of D on the diagonal and first subdiagonal and the unit lower-triangular multipliers of L below them, ipiv encodes the symmetric row/column swaps (ipiv[k] = p > 0 for a 1 × 1 pivot swapping k ↔ p, and ipiv[k] = ipiv[k + 1] = -p < 0 for a 2 × 2 pivot swapping k + 1 ↔ p), info is the index of the first zero pivot block or 1 for a non-finite matrix, or 0, scale_exponent is the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by, and hermitian is true for a real symmetric or complex Hermitian factorisation (L D Lᴴ) and false for a complex symmetric factorisation (L D Lᵀ). L = N * N makes the SMatrix field concrete, so the factor is isbits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StaticLDLT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.StaticCholesky","page":"Linear solvers","text":"StaticCholesky{N, TF, L}\n\nThe Cholesky factor L of A = L Lᴴ for an N × N static Hermitian positive definite matrix, in element type TF: factors holds L, with a real positive diagonal, on and below the diagonal and zeros above it, info is the order of the first leading minor that is not positive or 1 for a non-finite matrix, or 0, and scale_exponent is the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by. A non-finite matrix is not factorised, and factors then holds it as it was converted. L = N * N makes the SMatrix field concrete, so the factor is isbits for an isbits TF; with BigFloat entries it is not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StaticCholesky"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.StaticQR","page":"Linear solvers","text":"StaticQR{N, TF, L}\n\nThe Householder QR factors of an N × N static matrix, in element type TF: factors holds R on and above the diagonal and below it the reflector vectors w, scaled to w[k] = 1; the reflector of step k is I - τ[k] w wᴴ. info is the index of the first zero diagonal entry of R, or 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StaticQR"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.StaticRectQR","page":"Linear solvers","text":"StaticRectQR{M, N, TF, L}\n\nThe Householder QR factors of a rectangular M × N static matrix (M ≥ N), in element type TF: factors holds R on and above the diagonal of the leading N × N triangle and below it the reflector tails w[(k + 1):M], scaled to w[k] = 1; the reflector of step k is I - τ[k] w wᴴ. info is the index of the first zero diagonal entry of R, or 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by. L = M * N makes the SMatrix field concrete, so the factor is isbits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StaticRectQR"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.StaticSVD","page":"Linear solvers","text":"StaticSVD{N, TF, R, L}\n\nThe singular value decomposition of an N × N static matrix, in element type TF, computed by the one-sided Jacobi iteration of src/linear/static/svd.jl. factors holds U, V holds the right singular vectors as columns, and σ̂ holds the N singular values of the matrix divided by 2^e, the ones the sweep computed and the solve divides by, in non-increasing order in R = real(TF); singular_values gives those of the matrix as it was passed. rank is the number of singular values above the rank cutoff rtol · σ̂₁ of SVDFactorization, by default √N · eps(real(TF)) · σ̂₁; the directions past it are zeroed in U, and the solve of ldiv!! leaves their coordinates out. info is 1 where the sweep reached its cap of 30 sweeps before the columns were orthogonal or a singular value is not finite, and rank is then 0; it is 0 otherwise, also for a rank-deficient matrix, so that returncode reports a failed factorisation alone. scale_exponent is the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by.\n\nL = N * N makes the SMatrix fields concrete, so the factor is isbits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StaticSVD"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.StaticRectSVD","page":"Linear solvers","text":"StaticRectSVD{M, N, TF, L, LN, R}\n\nThe thin singular value decomposition U * Diagonal(S) * V' of a rectangular M × N static matrix (M ≥ N), in element type TF, computed by one-sided Jacobi sweeps. U holds the M × N left singular vectors, V the N × N right singular vectors, S the N singular values of the matrix in R = real(TF), σ̂ the N singular values of the matrix divided by 2^e, info is 0 on convergence and 1 otherwise, rank is the numerical rank above rtol · σ̂₁ of SVDFactorization, by default √M · eps(R) · σ̂₁, and scale_exponent is e.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StaticRectSVD"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.DenseLU","page":"Linear solvers","text":"DenseLU{A, P, V}\n\nThe LU factors with partial pivoting of a dense n × n host matrix, with factors the n × n buffer in the factor element type, ipiv the BlasInt pivot vector of length n, rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type when that differs from the working element type and nothing otherwise, info the index of the first zero pivot, or 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DenseLU"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.DeviceLU","page":"Linear solvers","text":"DeviceLU{A, P, V, S}\n\nThe LU factors with partial pivoting of a dense n × n device matrix, with factors the n × n device buffer in the factor element type, ipiv the Int32 pivot vector of length n, rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type when that differs from the working element type and nothing otherwise, info the index of the first zero pivot, or 0, scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by, and status the 1-element Int32 device status buffer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DeviceLU"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.DenseLDLT","page":"Linear solvers","text":"DenseLDLT{A, P, W, V}\n\nThe Bunch–Kaufman L D Lᵀ / L D Lᴴ factors of a dense n × n host matrix, with factors the n × n buffer in the factor element type, ipiv the BlasInt pivot vector of length n, work the LAPACK workspace buffer, rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type when that differs from the working element type and nothing otherwise, info the index of the first zero pivot block or 1 for a non-finite entry, or 0, scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by, and hermitian true for a real symmetric or complex Hermitian factorisation (L D Lᴴ) and false for a complex symmetric factorisation (L D Lᵀ). A BlasFloat matrix is factorised by hetrf! where hermitian and the element type is complex, and by sytrf! otherwise; another element type by generic_ldlt!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DenseLDLT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.DeviceLDLT","page":"Linear solvers","text":"DeviceLDLT{A, P, V, S}\n\nThe Bunch–Kaufman L D Lᵀ / L D Lᴴ factors of a dense n × n device matrix, with factors the n × n device buffer in the factor element type, ipiv the Int32 pivot vector of length n, rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type when that differs from the working element type and nothing otherwise, info the index of the first zero pivot block or 1 for a non-finite entry, or 0, scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by, status the 1-element Int32 device status buffer, and hermitian true for a real symmetric or complex Hermitian (L D Lᴴ) factorisation and false for a complex symmetric (L D Lᵀ) factorisation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DeviceLDLT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.DenseCholesky","page":"Linear solvers","text":"DenseCholesky{A, V}\n\nThe Cholesky factor L of A = L Lᴴ for a dense n × n host Hermitian positive definite matrix, with factors the n × n buffer in the factor element type, which holds L on and below the diagonal and zeros above it, rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type when that differs from the working element type and nothing otherwise, info the order of the first leading minor that is not positive or 1 for a non-finite entry, or 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by. A non-finite matrix is not factorised, and factors then holds it as it was loaded. A BlasFloat matrix is factorised by LAPACK potrf, another element type by generic_cholesky!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DenseCholesky"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.DenseQR","page":"Linear solvers","text":"DenseQR{A, T, W, V}\n\nThe Householder QR factors of a dense m × n host matrix (m ≥ n), with factors the m × n buffer in the factor element type (R on and above the diagonal of its leading n × n block, reflectors below it), τ the reflector scale vector of length n, work the LAPACK workspace buffer, rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type, which is nothing only where the working element type is the factor's and the problem is not a LeastSquaresProblem, info the index of the first zero diagonal entry of R, or 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DenseQR"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.DeviceQR","page":"Linear solvers","text":"DeviceQR{A, T, V, S}\n\nThe Householder QR factors of a dense m × n device matrix (m ≥ n), with factors the m × n device buffer in the factor element type (R on and above the diagonal of its leading n × n block, reflectors below it), τ the reflector scale vector of length n, rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type, which is nothing only where the working element type is the factor's and the problem is not a LeastSquaresProblem, info the index of the first zero diagonal entry of R, or 0, scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by, and status the 1-element Int32 device status buffer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DeviceQR"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.DenseSVD","page":"Linear solvers","text":"DenseSVD{A, S, W, RW, T, V}\n\nThe singular value decomposition factors U * Diagonal(S) * VT of a dense m × n host matrix (m ≥ n), with factors the m × n Jacobian buffer in the factor element type, U the thin m × n and VT the n × n unitary factors, S the vector of the singular values σ̂ · 2^e of the matrix in the real factor type, which overflow to Inf where they exceed floatmax, work the LAPACK workspace buffer, rwork the real LAPACK workspace during gesvd and the singular values σ̂ of the matrix divided by 2^e in its first n entries after gesvd, tmp the vector workspace buffer, rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type, which is nothing only where the working element type is the factor's and the problem is not a LeastSquaresProblem, info 1 where the matrix has a non-finite entry, which is refused before gesvd, gesvd's non-convergence code, 1 where a σ̂ is not finite, or 0, rank the number of singular values above the cutoff rtol · σ̂₁ of SVDFactorization, by default √m · eps(real(TF)) · σ̂₁, and 0 where info ≠ 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DenseSVD"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.DeviceSVD","page":"Linear solvers","text":"DeviceSVD{A, S, F, T, V}\n\nThe singular value decomposition factors U * Diagonal(S) * VT of a dense m × n device matrix (m ≥ n), computed by the parallel one-sided Jacobi iteration of src/linear/ka/svd.jl, with factors the m × n Jacobian buffer in the factor element type, U the thin m × n and VT the n × n unitary factors, S the vector of the singular values σ̂ · 2^e of the matrix in the real factor type, V the n × n right-singular-vector workspace during the Jacobi sweep, σ̂ the vector of the singular values of the matrix divided by 2^e (and the squared column norms during the sweep), flag the 1-element Int32 rotation-flag buffer, tmp the vector workspace buffer, rhs the right-hand-side buffer of the factor element type, which is nothing only where the working element type is the factor's and the problem is not a LeastSquaresProblem, info 1 where the Jacobi sweep reaches its sweep cap without converging or meets a non-finite entry, or 0, rank the number of singular values above the cutoff rtol · σ̂₁ of SVDFactorization, by default √m · eps(real(TF)) · σ̂₁, and 0 where info ≠ 0, and scale_exponent the exponent e of the power of two 2^e that the matrix was divided by.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DeviceSVD"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.UMFPACKLU","page":"Linear solvers","text":"UMFPACKLU{T}\n\nThe sparse LU factor of UMFPACKFactorization in element type T, Float64 or ComplexF64. factors is the SparseMatrixCSC{T, Int} buffer with the pattern of the Jacobian prototype, into which factorize!! writes the Jacobian; colptr and rowval are copies of the prototype's column pointers and row indices, against which factorize!! checks the pattern of each Jacobian; lu is the UMFPACK factor SparseArrays.UMFPACK.UmfpackLU{T, Int} of that pattern; info is 1 for a singular Jacobian or one with a non-finite entry, and 0 otherwise.\n\ninit_linear builds lu with no analysis and no factorisation. The first factorize!! runs UMFPACK's symbolic analysis and its numeric factorisation, and every later factorize!! the numeric factorisation alone, on the symbolic object lu.symbolic of the first. Every factor that factorize!! returns from one init_linear shares factors, colptr, rowval and lu.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.UMFPACKLU"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.returncode","page":"Linear solvers","text":"returncode(fac)\n\nSUCCESS for a factor with info == 0, and SINGULAR otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.returncode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.exact_solve","page":"Linear solvers","text":"exact_solve(fac)\n\ntrue where the solve of ldiv!! with the factor fac is the exact solve of the factored matrix, and false for an SVD factor of numerical rank below n, whose solve is the truncated minimum-norm step. Newton measures the slope of a truncated step, because −2φ(0) is the slope of an exact Newton step alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.exact_solve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.singular_values","page":"Linear solvers","text":"singular_values(fac::Union{StaticSVD, StaticRectSVD})\n\nThe singular values of the matrix that factorize!! factorised, in non-increasing order, as an SVector in real(TF): the stored σ̂ of the matrix divided by 2^e, multiplied by 2^e. A singular value above floatmax(real(TF)) overflows to Inf, although every entry of the matrix is finite; the solve divides by σ̂ and stays finite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.singular_values"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.GMRES","page":"Linear solvers","text":"GMRES(; maxiter = 30, restart = nothing, forcing = EisenstatWalkerChoice2(),\n        preconditioner = nothing, jvp_eltype = Nothing, mgs = false)\n\nThe R5 matrix-free Newton–Krylov linear solver of Newton: right-preconditioned GMRES on the Jacobian–vector product of the problem, with the forcing term forcing (EisenstatWalkerChoice2 by default, EisenstatWalkerChoice1 or FixedForcing), a right preconditioner preconditioner, the element type jvp_eltype of the product, and the orthogonalisation of the Arnoldi process that mgs selects.\n\nFields\n\nmaxiter::Int32: the cap on the inner GMRES iterations of one Newton step, ≥ 1. Under a DogLeg the cap is min(maxiter, n), one Arnoldi cycle, because its model reads the Arnoldi relation of that cycle. A step that reaches the cap without meeting the tolerance of its forcing term counts a step-rule failure.\nrestart::Int32: the number of inner iterations between restarts, 1 ≤ restart ≤ maxiter. restart = nothing stores maxiter, which is unrestarted GMRES, and which DogLeg needs. The Krylov basis holds min(restart, n) + 1 columns.\nforcing::F: the rule that sets the relative tolerance eta_k of step k.\npreconditioner::P: the right preconditioner, nothing for none. refresh!(P, prob, x, Fx, p) is called once per Newton step, before GMRES starts, and apply_preconditioner!(z, P, v) once per inner iteration; the solve forms J M^-1 u = -F and returns d = M^-1 u. A type that carries a preconditioner extends those two functions, for example a stored factorisation that ldiv! applies, which is the frozen-Jacobian preconditioner of a Newton–Krylov method.\njvp_eltype::Type{TJ}: Nothing evaluates the Jacobian–vector product in the working type of the iterate; a real type TJ evaluates it in TJ and promotes the product to the working type, which is a defect-correction solve: a product in a lower precision, a residual in the working one. The iterate, the residual and every norm stay in the working type.\nmgs::Bool: false, the default, orthogonalises each new Krylov vector by classical Gram–Schmidt with a second pass (CGS2), which takes two reductions over the basis per inner iteration: h_1 = V_j^H w and w_1 = w - V_j h_1, then V_j w_1^H w_1 for the correction h_2 and for s_1 = w_1^2, and w_2 = w_1 - V_j h_2. The norm of w_2 is sqrts_1 - h_2^2 where s_1 is finite, at least n * floatmin and h_2^2 le s_1  2, and one more reduction, rnorm of w_2, elsewhere. true orthogonalises by modified Gram–Schmidt, which takes j + 1 reductions at inner iteration j.\n\nA Newton with GMRES allocates no n × n Jacobian, and its iterate must be an AbstractVector: a Number and a StaticArray have no Krylov basis here, and a LeastSquaresProblem has no square system. The solve calls no Jacobian function of the problem: the product comes from the jacobian of the Newton, a JVPFunction or an AD back end, AutoForwardDiff() by default. A residual that the back end cannot differentiate therefore needs a JVPFunction, even where the problem carries a Jacobian. For the same reason solve refuses a ForwardDiff.Dual in x₀ or p for a Newton with GMRES with an ArgumentError, before the solve: implicit differentiation factorises the Jacobian at the root. A constructor refuses maxiter < 1 and a restart outside 1:maxiter with an ArgumentError, and so does a Newton with a DogLeg globalization and a preconditioner or a restart < maxiter; a kernel cannot format a message, so each message is constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.GMRES"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.AbstractForcingTerm","page":"Linear solvers","text":"AbstractForcingTerm\n\nThe supertype of the forcing terms of GMRES: the rule that chooses the relative tolerance eta_k of the linear solve of Newton step k. Each term is isbits and its constants are in the real type R of the iterate, and compute_forcing is the one function that evaluates it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.AbstractForcingTerm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.EisenstatWalkerChoice2","page":"Linear solvers","text":"EisenstatWalkerChoice2(T = Float64; η₀ = 0.5, γ = 0.9, α = 2, η_max = 0.9)\n\nEisenstat and Walker's Choice 2 forcing term (Eisenstat and Walker 1996, (2.6), pp. 20–24) with the safeguard of that choice and the floor 2varepsilon(R):\n\neta_k^mathrmraw = gamma Bigl(fracF_kF_k-1Bigr)^alpha\nqquad\neta_k = maxBigl(minbigl(eta_k^mathrmsafe eta_maxbigr) 2varepsilon(R)Bigr)\n\nwith eta_k^mathrmsafe = max(eta_k^mathrmraw gammaeta_k-1^alpha) where gammaeta_k-1^alpha  01 and eta_k^mathrmraw elsewhere. The first step returns eta_0, floored at 2varepsilon(R) like every other value of the rule. The defaults are gamma = 09, alpha = 2, eta_0 = 05 and eta_max = 09; eta_max is 0.8 in Chen et al. (2011) and 0.2 in Chen et al. (2014).\n\nT is the real type of the constants. A term with 0 ≤ η₀ < 1, 0 < γ ≤ 1, α ≥ 1 and 0 < η_max < 1 is built, and any other raises an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.EisenstatWalkerChoice2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.EisenstatWalkerChoice1","page":"Linear solvers","text":"EisenstatWalkerChoice1(T = Float64; η₀ = 0.5, η_max = 0.9)\n\nEisenstat and Walker's Choice 1 forcing term (Eisenstat and Walker 1996, §2.2), which reads the linear residual of the last step s_{k-1}. After a line search on an unrestarted inner solve, s_{k-1} is the step that the search accepted, whose linear residual the Arnoldi relation gives without a product; after a restarted inner solve or a DogLeg step, it is the direction GMRES returned:\n\neta_k^mathrmraw =\n  fracbiglF_k - F_k-1 + J_k-1 s_k-1bigrF_k-1\nqquad\neta_k = maxBigl(minbigl(eta_k^mathrmsafe eta_maxbigr) 2varepsilon(R)Bigr)\n\nwith eta_k^mathrmsafe = max(eta_k^mathrmraw eta_k-1^phi) where eta_k-1^phi  01 and phi = (1 + sqrt5)2, and eta_k^mathrmraw elsewhere. The first step returns eta_0, floored at 2varepsilon(R).\n\nT is the real type of the constants, and the constructor validates 0 ≤ η₀ < 1 and 0 < η_max < 1 as EisenstatWalkerChoice2 does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.EisenstatWalkerChoice1"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.FixedForcing","page":"Linear solvers","text":"FixedForcing(η)\n\nThe constant forcing term eta_k = max(eta 2varepsilon(R)): every Newton step solves its linear system to the same relative tolerance, which is the choice of an inexact Newton method without an adaptive rule (Eisenstat and Walker 1996, §1). η must be finite and in [0, 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.FixedForcing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.JVPFunction","page":"Linear solvers","text":"JVPFunction(jvp!)\n\nThe Jacobian–vector product of a matrix-free problem, as the jacobian field of a Newton with GMRES takes it: jvp!(Jv, x, v, p) writes J v into Jv, output first, as jvp!! takes it. A problem that no array can hold the Jacobian of — an expensive residual whose every column costs a full evaluation, or a device array — solves through it:\n\njvp!(Jv, x, v, p) = (Jv .= (3 .* x .^ 2 .+ 2) .* v; Jv)\nmethod = Newton(; linsolve = GMRES(; maxiter = 10), jacobian = JVPFunction(jvp!))\n\nA callback that returns nothing is accepted, as are the vectors of a device backend: the call is jvp!(Jv, x, v, p) on the storage of the iterate, and the product is counted by the solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.JVPFunction"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.refresh!","page":"Linear solvers","text":"refresh!(P, prob, x, Fx, p)\n\nRefresh the right preconditioner P of GMRES at the start of a Newton step, from the iterate x, its residual Fx and the parameters p. Newton calls it once per step, before the inner solve, so that the preconditioner of step k sees the data of the last residual evaluation (Chen et al. 2014, pp. 558–560). The method for nothing does nothing; a preconditioner that holds data extends this function, and one that is constant across the solve extends it with a method that does nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.refresh!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.apply_preconditioner!","page":"Linear solvers","text":"apply_preconditioner!(z, P, v)\n\nThe right-preconditioned vector z = M⁻¹ v of GMRES, in the storage of z and return it, for the preconditioner P and the vector v. The method for nothing copies v, so a GMRES without a preconditioner solves J d = -F itself; any other type extends this function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.apply_preconditioner!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.f_calls","page":"Linear solvers","text":"f_calls(solver)\n\nThe number of evaluations of the residual F that the last solve of solver made, for a NonlinearSolver whose method is a Newton with GMRES: the count that SolverStatus records, saturated there at typemax(Int16) and exact here below it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.f_calls"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.jvp_calls","page":"Linear solvers","text":"jvp_calls(solver)\n\nThe number of Jacobian–vector products that the last solve of solver made, for a NonlinearSolver whose method is a Newton with GMRES: the count that SolverStatus records, saturated there at typemax(Int16) and exact here below it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.jvp_calls"},{"category":"function","location":"api/linear/#GeometricSolvers.jacobi_eigen","page":"Linear solvers","text":"jacobi_eigen(A)\n\nThe eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the Hermitian matrix A, or of a real symmetric one, by cyclic two-sided Jacobi: the rotations and the round-robin ordering of the Jacobi SVD of SVDFactorization, applied to the rows and the columns of A at once. The eigenvectors are unitary to working precision, which is what the ProjectedSkew exponential algorithm of GeometricOptimizers needs of them. A pair whose off-diagonal entry is at or below eps(real(eltype(A))) * ‖A‖_F is skipped, which is what ends the sweep on a rank-deficient or low-rank matrix, as the SVD's negligible-column guard ends its own.\n\nThe call returns (values, vectors, status): values holds the N eigenvalues in real(eltype(A)), vectors the columns of the unitary V with A = V * Diagonal(values) * V', and status is SUCCESS where the sweep reached its fixed point, or MAXITERS where it reached its cap of 30 sweeps first. All three are isbits, so the call runs inside a kernel.\n\nThe matrix is assumed Hermitian: the call reads its lower triangle and sets the upper triangle to the conjugate of the lower one on entry, so a matrix that is not Hermitian gives the eigensystem of that Hermitian part and not the one of A. A NaN or an Inf entry is not checked: nothing throws, and the sweep reaches its cap for N ≥ 2; at N = 1 there is no pair, so the call returns SUCCESS.\n\nFor a real skew matrix M, the Hermitian matrix is im * M, with the eigenvalues values of im * M and\n\nexp(M) ≈ real(V * Diagonal(exp.(-im .* values)) * V')\n\nto working precision — the ProjectedSkew case, which is what jacobi_eigen exists for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.jacobi_eigen"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"A solve in GeometricSolvers has four parts:\n\na problem, which holds the residual function and, optionally, its Jacobian,\na method, which holds the algorithmic configuration, such as Newton with a linear solver and a globalization,\nan initial iterate x₀, whose storage selects the call form of the functions and the regime of the solve, and\nthe parameters p, which arrive at solve time.\n\nThe problem holds no iterate and no parameters. One problem object therefore serves a whole time-stepping loop, and a batch of systems shares one problem.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#A-first-solve","page":"Getting started","text":"solve takes the problem, the initial iterate, the method and the parameters, and returns the solution and a SolverStatus:\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x^2 - p);\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, 1.0, Newton(), 2.0);\n\njulia> x ≈ sqrt(2)\ntrue\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS\ntrue\n\nThe residual of a scalar or static problem has the form F(x, p) and returns a value. Without a Jacobian on the problem, Newton differentiates F with ForwardDiff.","title":"A first solve"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Call-forms","page":"Getting started","text":"The storage of the initial iterate selects the call form of the residual and of the Jacobian. The problem has no flag for it.\n\niterate x₀ residual Jacobian J\nNumber F(x, p) returns a number J(x, p) returns a number\nSVector (a StaticVector) F(x, p) returns an SVector J(x, p) returns an SMatrix\nAbstractVector that is not static (Vector, CuArray, MtlArray, …) F!(y, x, p) writes into y J!(J, x, p) writes into J\n\nA static system:\n\njulia> using StaticArrays\n\njulia> F(x, p) = SVector(x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 - p, x[1] - x[2]);\n\njulia> x, status = solve(NonlinearProblem(F), SVector(1.0, 0.5), Newton(), 2.0);\n\njulia> x ≈ SVector(1.0, 1.0)\ntrue\n\nThe same system with in-place functions on a Vector, here with an analytic Jacobian:\n\njulia> function F!(y, x, p)\n           y[1] = x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 - p\n           y[2] = x[1] - x[2]\n           return y\n       end;\n\njulia> function J!(J, x, p)\n           J[1, 1] = 2x[1]; J[1, 2] = 2x[2]\n           J[2, 1] = 1;     J[2, 2] = -1\n           return J\n       end;\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem(F!, J!);\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, [1.0, 0.5], Newton(), 2.0);\n\njulia> x ≈ [1.0, 1.0]\ntrue\n\nsolve copies an array x₀, so the caller's initial guess stays unchanged.","title":"Call forms"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Parameters-at-solve-time","page":"Getting started","text":"The parameters p are the fourth positional argument of solve, init and solve_batch!(X, prob, method, p), the fifth of solve_batch!(X, S, prob, method, p) with a status vector S, and the last argument of every function of the problem. They can be any value: a number, a tuple, a named tuple, an array or a struct. The default is nothing. Because p is not part of the problem, the same problem solves for other parameters without a rebuild:\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x^2 - p);\n\njulia> [solve(prob, 1.0, Newton(), p)[1] for p in (4.0, 9.0)] ≈ [2.0, 3.0]\ntrue","title":"Parameters at solve time"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Preallocated-solves:-init-and-solve!","page":"Getting started","text":"init, solve! and solve are the functions of CommonSolve.jl. init takes the arguments of solve and returns a NonlinearSolver that holds all buffers, the factorisation workspace and the automatic-differentiation preparation. solve!(solver) solves from the iterate that the solver holds, and solve!(solver, x, p) solves from another iterate with other parameters of the same types, in place into an array x:\n\njulia> solver = init(NonlinearProblem(F!), [1.0, 0.5], Newton(), 2.0);\n\njulia> x, status = solve!(solver);\n\njulia> x ≈ [1.0, 1.0]\ntrue\n\njulia> x, status = solve!(solver, [3.0, 1.0], 8.0);\n\njulia> x ≈ [2.0, 2.0]\ntrue\n\nOn primal inputs, solve!(init(args...; kwargs...)) returns what solve(args...; kwargs...) returns. The preallocated form is the one for a loop that solves many times, such as the stages of an integrator: an R3 solve with solve! on an Array reuses its buffers.","title":"Preallocated solves: init and solve!"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Options","page":"Getting started","text":"The keywords of solve and init build the Options of the solve, typed in R = real(eltype(x₀)):\n\nkeyword default meaning\nf_abstol 0 absolute residual tolerance\nf_reltol √eps(R) residual tolerance relative to F(x_0)\nx_abstol 0 absolute step tolerance\nx_reltol 2 eps(R) step tolerance relative to x\nmin_iterations 0 lower bound on the number of steps\nmax_iterations 100 upper bound on the number of steps\nmax_stalls 2 consecutive stalled steps before the solve stops\ng_tol √eps(R) stationarity tolerance of a LeastSquaresProblem\n\nThe residual test is F(x) le max(f_abstol f_reltol F(x_0)). It returns SUCCESS, and so does the stationarity test of a LeastSquaresProblem; the step test of x_abstol and x_reltol returns STALLED. The default test is relative, because no default can know the scale of F; a caller who knows the scale sets f_abstol.\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, 1.0, Newton(), 2.0; f_abstol = 1e-12, max_iterations = 20);\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS\ntrue\n\nAn Options object can also be passed as options. It must have the real type of the iterate: Options(T; kwargs...) builds one for the element type T. A Float32 solve therefore takes Options(Float32; …); an Options{Float64} raises an ArgumentError there.\n\njulia> opt = Options(Float32; f_reltol = 1e-5);\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, 1.0f0, Newton(), 2.0f0; options = opt);\n\njulia> x isa Float32 && x ≈ sqrt(2.0f0)\ntrue\n\nPassing both options and keyword tolerances raises an ArgumentError.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Status-and-return-codes","page":"Getting started","text":"Every solve returns a SolverStatus. It is isbits, so a kernel can write one per system. Its fields are:\n\ncode: the ReturnCode, why the solve ended,\niterations: the number of steps,\nfnorm: F(x) at return, and stepnorm: the norm of the last step,\nstep_failures: the count of step-rule failures, such as failed line searches, restarts or refinement failures,\npromoted: a factorisation ran in a higher precision than the method's factorisation precision,\nstationary: the solve converged by the stationarity test of a LeastSquaresProblem,\nf_calls and jvp_calls: the residual and Jacobian–vector product counts of a matrix-free solve,\nrank, nullnorm and gradratio: range and spectrum diagnostics of the last fresh factorisation.\n\nThe return codes are:\n\ncode meaning\nSUCCESS the residual test (or the stationarity test of a least-squares problem) holds\nSTALLED the iterate or the residual stopped improving before the test held\nMAXITERS the iteration cap was reached\nSINGULAR a factorisation failed; the solve does not throw\nNONFINITE a residual, a step or a norm is not finite\nLINESEARCH_FAILED the globalization found no acceptable step\n\nA solve does not throw for a failure that it detects. It returns the code instead:\n\njulia> x, status = solve(NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x^2 + 1), 0.0, Newton());\n\njulia> status.code === SINGULAR\ntrue\n\nAn exception that a function of the problem raises, such as a DomainError of sqrt at a negative trial iterate, propagates to the caller.\n\nstopping_test names the test that ended a converged solve, and GeometricSolvers.isconverged and GeometricSolvers.isstalled read the code:\n\njulia> stopping_test(solve(prob, 1.0, Newton(), 2.0)[2])\n:residual\n\nThe section Status and stopping test of the API explains how rank, nullnorm and gradratio classify a STALLED or MAXITERS return.","title":"Status and return codes"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#The-SciML-NonlinearProblem-extension","page":"Getting started","text":"When SciMLBase is loaded, the package extension GeometricSolversSciMLBaseExt adds methods of solve and init for a SciMLBase.NonlinearProblem with a Newton or Picard method. The extension translates the problem and calls the native solve, so the result is the native (x, status):\n\nusing GeometricSolvers, SciMLBase\n\nf(u, p) = u .^ 2 .- p\nprob = SciMLBase.NonlinearProblem(f, [1.0, 1.0], 2.0)\n\nx, status = solve(prob, Newton(); abstol = 1e-12)\nGeometricSolvers.sciml_retcode(status.code)   # SciMLBase.ReturnCode.Success\n\nThe call form follows SciMLBase.isinplace(prob) and the type of prob.u0. The keywords abstol, reltol, maxiters and options map to the native Options, and sciml_retcode maps a ReturnCode to a SciMLBase.ReturnCode.T. The section SciMLBase extension of the API gives the complete rules.","title":"The SciML NonlinearProblem extension"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/getting_started/#Where-to-go-next","page":"Getting started","text":"The three regimes explains how the storage of the iterate selects the code path, and how to choose between value, batched and in-place solves.\nMethods by regime lists the methods that run in each regime, and the conditions under which each one applies.\nProblem types describes the least-squares, stationarity, reparametrized, deflated and eliminated problems.\nThe pages under Algorithms describe each nonlinear method, globalization, linear solver and differentiation path, and the composition of methods.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"stability/#stability","page":"Stability","text":"This page states the backward-error and spectral-accuracy bounds satisfied by the linear and spectral kernels in GeometricSolvers, together with their literature sources and the verification domain covered by the test suite (test/linear/stability.jl).\n\nThroughout this page:\n\nT in textttFloat32 textttFloat64 textttComplexF32 textttComplexF64 denotes the problem element type and R = textttreal(T) its real precision, with machine epsilon varepsilon_R = texttteps(R).\nn denotes the matrix dimension and kappa_2(A) = sigma_1(A)  sigma_r(A) the 2-norm condition number of a matrix A in T^n times n of numerical rank r.\nThe cited theorems bound each error by a low-degree polynomial in n times varepsilon_R, with the growth factor or the condition number where stated. The test suite asserts each bound with the explicit constant\nc = 16\na test threshold with a margin above the measured errors, not a constant derived from the theorems.\n\n","title":"Numerical Stability and Backward-Error Bounds"},{"category":"section","location":"stability/#Kernel-Summary","page":"Stability","text":"Kernel family Factor / function types Bound verified (c = 16) Literature source\nLU with partial pivoting StaticLU, DenseLU, DeviceLU dfraclVert A hatx - b rVert_2lVert A rVert_2 lVert hatx rVert_2 + lVert b rVert_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R Higham (2002), §9.3–9.4\nHouseholder QR StaticQR, DenseQR, DeviceQR dfraclVert A hatx - b rVert_2lVert A rVert_2 lVert hatx rVert_2 + lVert b rVert_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R Higham (2002), §19.3\nSVD (one-sided Jacobi and LAPACK) StaticSVD, DenseSVD, DeviceSVD dfraclVert A hatx - b rVert_2lVert A rVert_2 lVert hatx rVert_2 + lVert b rVert_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R for A of numerical rank n, dfracmax_i lvert hatsigma_i - sigma_i rvertsigma_1 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R Demmel and Veselić (1992); Higham (2002)\nHermitian Jacobi eigensolver jacobi_eigen dfraclVert H hatV - hatV operatornameDiagonal(hatlambda) rVert_2lVert H rVert_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R, lVert hatV^* hatV - I rVert_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R, dfracmax_i lvert hatlambda_i - lambda_i rvertlambda_1 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R Demmel and Veselić (1992)\nRank-deficient SVD minimum-norm solve (TF = T) StaticSVD, DenseSVD, DeviceSVD dfraclVert hatx - x_min rVert_2lVert x_min rVert_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R cdot bigl(kappa_2(A) + kappa_2(A)^2 tanthetabigr), tantheta = dfraclVert b - A x_min rVert_2lVert A x_min rVert_2 Demmel and Veselić (1992); Higham (2002), §20.4\nMixed-precision scaled solve (TF ≠ T) ldiv!! on LUFactorization, QRFactorization, SVDFactorization of numerical rank n dfraclVert A hatx - b rVert_2lVert A rVert_2 lVert hatx rVert_2 + lVert b rVert_2 le c cdot n cdot max(varepsilon_R varepsilon_operatornamereal(F)) in the tested cases Kelley (2023), eqs. 2.17–2.18\n\n","title":"Kernel Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"stability/#LU-with-Partial-Pivoting-(StaticLU,-DenseLU,-DeviceLU)","page":"Stability","text":"LUFactorization dispatches to:\n\nStaticLU on SMatrix{n, n, T} (register-resident Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting),\nDenseLU on host Array{T, 2} (LAPACK getrf! / getrs!), and\nDeviceLU on GPU AbstractGPUArray{T, 2} backends such as JLArray and MtlArray (KernelAbstractions panel pivoting and trailing-submatrix update).\n\nBy Higham (2002, §9.3–9.4), Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting produces a computed solution hatx to A x = b that is the exact solution of (A + Delta A)hatx = b, where Delta A_2  A_2 is bounded by O(n  rho_n  varepsilon_R) for growth factor rho_n. On systems with moderate pivot growth, the test suite verifies the normwise backward error bound\n\nfracA hatx - b_2A_2 hatx_2 + b_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R qquad c = 16\n\n","title":"LU with Partial Pivoting (StaticLU, DenseLU, DeviceLU)"},{"category":"section","location":"stability/#Householder-QR-(StaticQR,-DenseQR,-DeviceQR)","page":"Stability","text":"QRFactorization dispatches to:\n\nStaticQR on SMatrix{n, n, T} (register-resident Householder reflections with standard complex sign selection),\nDenseQR on host Array{T, 2} (LAPACK geqrf! / ormqr! / unmqr! and BLAS.trsv!), and\nDeviceQR on GPU AbstractGPUArray{T, 2} backends (KernelAbstractions column-norm reduction and reflector application).\n\nBy Higham (2002, Theorem 19.4, §19.3), Householder QR factorization and triangular back-substitution are unconditionally backward stable, with a normwise backward error of order n  varepsilon_R. For a nonsingular square system A x = b, the test suite verifies the normwise backward error bound for the computed solution hatx\n\nfracA hatx - b_2A_2 hatx_2 + b_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R qquad c = 16\n\n","title":"Householder QR (StaticQR, DenseQR, DeviceQR)"},{"category":"section","location":"stability/#SVD-and-Hermitian-Jacobi-Eigensolver-(StaticSVD,-DenseSVD,-DeviceSVD,-jacobi_eigen)","page":"Stability","text":"SVDFactorization dispatches to:\n\nStaticSVD on SMatrix{n, n, T} (one-sided Jacobi orthogonalization of A followed by column normalization and descending sort),\nDenseSVD on host Array{T, 2} (LAPACK gesvd), and\nDeviceSVD on GPU AbstractGPUArray{T, 2} backends (KernelAbstractions one-sided Jacobi sweeps with complex phase annihilation).\n\nFor Hermitian matrices H::SMatrix{n, n, T}, jacobi_eigen computes the spectral decomposition H = V operatornameDiagonal(lambda) V^* by two-sided cyclic Jacobi sweeps.\n\nBy Demmel and Veselić (1992) and Higham (2002), these algorithms are backward stable, with errors of order n  varepsilon_R. The test suite verifies:\n\nFull-rank linear solve (ldiv!!): For A of numerical rank n (sigma_n  sigma_1  sqrtn  varepsilon_R), the pseudoinverse backsolve hatx = hatV hatSigma^-1 hatU^* b satisfies the normwise backward error bound\nfracA hatx - b_2A_2 hatx_2 + b_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R qquad c = 16\nSingular values (singular_values on StaticSVD, fac.S on DenseSVD and DeviceSVD): The computed singular values hatsigma_1 ge dots ge hatsigma_n satisfy the Weyl relative bound\nfracmax_1 le i le n hatsigma_i - sigma_isigma_1 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R qquad c = 16\nHermitian eigendecomposition (jacobi_eigen): On a Hermitian positive-definite matrix H with exact eigenvalues lambda_1 ge dots ge lambda_n  0, the computed eigenpairs (hatlambda hatV) satisfy\nfracH hatV - hatV operatornameDiagonal(hatlambda)_2H_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R qquad\nhatV^* hatV - I_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R qquad\nfracmax_1 le i le n hatlambda_i - lambda_ilambda_1 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R\nwith c = 16.\n\n","title":"SVD and Hermitian Jacobi Eigensolver (StaticSVD, DenseSVD, DeviceSVD, jacobi_eigen)"},{"category":"section","location":"stability/#Rank-Deficient-SVD-Minimum-Norm-ldiv!!","page":"Stability","text":"When A in T^n times n has exact rank r  n with singular values sigma_1 ge dots ge sigma_r  0 = sigma_r+1 = dots = sigma_n and sigma_r  sigma_1 gg sqrtn  varepsilon_R, SVDFactorization with factor precision T (SVDFactorization()) across StaticSVD, DenseSVD, and DeviceSVD detects the numerical rank fac.rank == r by truncating singular values satisfying hatsigma_i le sqrtn  varepsilon_R  hatsigma_1.\n\nWith a reduced factor precision F, the cutoff is sqrtn  varepsilon_operatornamereal(F)  hatsigma_1 instead, and a singular value at or below it counts as zero even where sigma_r  sigma_1 gg sqrtn  varepsilon_R holds. For example, with T = textttFloat64, SVDFactorization(Float32) gives fac.rank == 2 for A = operatornameDiagonal(1 1 10^-9 0), whose rank is 3. The rank detection above and the bound below hold for the factor precision T only.\n\nSVDFactorization(; rtol) replaces the cutoff by mathrmrtol cdot hatsigma_1, in each factor precision, and a singular value at or below it counts as zero. A cutoff above the default truncates singular values that are not rounding noise, and a cutoff below it keeps singular values that are.\n\nBy Demmel and Veselić (1992) and Higham (2002, §20.4), the truncated pseudoinverse solve ldiv!! approximates the exact minimum-norm least-squares solution x_min = V_1r Sigma_1r^-1 U_1r^* b with relative forward error bounded by\n\nfrachatx - x_min_2x_min_2 le c cdot n cdot varepsilon_R cdot bigl(kappa_2(A) + kappa_2(A)^2 tanthetabigr) qquad\ntantheta = fracb - A x_min_2A x_min_2 qquad c = 16\n\nwhere kappa_2(A) = sigma_1  sigma_r is the 2-norm condition number of the nonzero singular spectrum and theta is the angle between b and the range of A. The term kappa_2(A)^2 tantheta is the residual term of least-squares perturbation theory; for a consistent system, tantheta = 0. The test suite draws b with a component outside the range of A and asserts the stronger bound c cdot n cdot kappa_2(A) cdot varepsilon_R cdot b_2  A x_min_2, which implies the one above because b_2  A x_min_2 = sqrt1 + tan^2theta le 1 + kappa_2(A) tantheta.\n\n","title":"Rank-Deficient SVD Minimum-Norm ldiv!!"},{"category":"section","location":"stability/#Mixed-Precision-Factorization-and-Scaled-Solve-(TF-T)","page":"Stability","text":"When a factorization method is constructed with reduced factor precision TF (for example LUFactorization(Float32), QRFactorization(Float32), or SVDFactorization(Float32) applied to a problem with T in textttFloat64 textttComplexF64 and factor storage type F = textttFloat32 or textttComplexF32), factorize!! scales A by a power of two 2^-e, converts the scaled matrix into precision F, and computes the factorization in F.\n\nDuring ldiv!!, GeometricSolvers implements the scaled mixed-precision solve of Kelley (2023, eqs. 2.17–2.18):\n\nCompute the right-hand side scale s = max_i b_i = b_infty in working precision T (returning 0 immediately if s = 0).\nScale and downcast tildeb = F(b  s) into factor precision F, preventing exponent underflow or overflow in reduced precision.\nSolve the factored system for tildex in precision F.\nRescale by s cdot 2^-e and convert back to working precision T.\n\nFor T in textttFloat64 textttComplexF64, textttTF = textttFloat32, n in 4 16 64 and kappa_2(A) in 10 10^4, the cases that the test suite covers, the resulting solution hatx in T^n satisfies the normwise backward error bound at the coarser of the working precision varepsilon_R and the factor precision varepsilon_operatornamereal(F) = texttteps(operatornamereal(F)):\n\nfracA hatx - b_2A_2 hatx_2 + b_2 le c cdot n cdot max(varepsilon_R varepsilon_operatornamereal(F)) qquad c = 16\n\nFor a reduced factor precision, as in these cases, the maximum is varepsilon_operatornamereal(F). For a wider factor precision, the rounding of hatx to T limits the attainable backward error to the order of varepsilon_R; the test suite does not cover this case.\n\nThe bound does not cover a truncated SVD solve. The SVD factor counts every singular value hatsigma_i le sqrtn  varepsilon_operatornamereal(F)  hatsigma_1 as zero, and ldiv!! leaves out its direction. For example, with T = textttFloat64, F = textttFloat32, A = operatornameDiagonal(1 1 1 10^-9) and b = e_4, the factor has fac.rank == 3, the solve gives hatx = 0, and the backward error is 1. In the tested cases, kappa_2(A) le 10^4  1  (sqrtn  varepsilon_textttFloat32), so every SVD factor there has numerical rank n.\n\n","title":"Mixed-Precision Factorization and Scaled Solve (TF ≠ T)"},{"category":"section","location":"stability/#Verification-Matrix-(test/linear/stability.jl)","page":"Stability","text":"Every bound above is verified in test/linear/stability.jl using random matrices with Haar-distributed orthogonal or unitary factors (test/helpers/randsvd.jl) and geometrically distributed singular values or eigenvalues:\n\nElement types: T in textttFloat32 textttFloat64 textttComplexF32 textttComplexF64 (ELTYPES).\nCondition numbers: kappa_2 in 10 10^4 for 32-bit real precision (R = textttFloat32) and kappa_2 in 10 10^4 10^8 for 64-bit real precision (R = textttFloat64); kappa_2 = 100 with rank r = n - 2 for rank-deficient SVD; kappa_2 in 10 10^4 with T in textttFloat64 textttComplexF64 and textttTF = textttFloat32 for mixed-precision solves.\nSystem dimensions: n in 4 16 64 for the full-rank and mixed-precision solves across dense (Array) and device (JLArray under allowscalar(false)) backends, n in 4 16 for the rank-deficient SVD solve across dense and device backends, and n in 4 16 across static (SMatrix{n, n, T}) kernels, which include jacobi_eigen.\n\n","title":"Verification Matrix (test/linear/stability.jl)"},{"category":"section","location":"stability/#References","page":"Stability","text":"Demmel, J. and Veselić, K. (1992). Jacobi's Method is More Accurate than QR. SIAM Journal on Matrix Analysis and Applications, 13(4), 1204–1245. doi:10.1137/0613074\nHigham, N. J. (2002). Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms, 2nd ed. Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics (SIAM), Philadelphia. (§9.3–9.4 for LU with partial pivoting; §19.3 for Householder QR; §20.4 for rank-deficient least squares.) doi:10.1137/1.9780898718027\nKelley, C. T. (2023). Newton's Method in Three Precisions. Pacific Journal of Optimization. (Eqs. 2.17–2.18 of arXiv:2307.16051v4 for scaled mixed-precision linear solves.) doi:10.61208/pjo-2023-030, arXiv:2307.16051","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api/ad/#Automatic-differentiation","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"In R1, prepare_ad returns the AutoForwardDiff() back end itself for a StaticVector or a Number iterate. jacobian!!, gradient_hessian!! and jvp!! run tagged ForwardDiff directly with that source. The source nothing calls the problem's own J; a JacobianFunction calls a method's operator for jacobian!! only. A back end other than AutoForwardDiff has no R1 method, and the call is a host MethodError.\n\nIn R3 the AD path is chosen once, by prepare_ad, from the back end and the storage of the iterate: AutoForwardDiff() goes through an own chunked forward mode on every array type (ChunkedForwardDiff for NonlinearProblem and ChunkedHessianForwardDiff for StationarityProblem), and any other back end goes through DifferentiationInterface on a CPU iterate (DIJacobian and DIStationarity), so every ADTypes back end works with no line of source here. A device array iterate takes AutoForwardDiff() only, because DifferentiationInterface's jacobian! raises a scalar-indexing error on an MtlArray, a CuArray and a JLArray. When the problem supplies an analytic 3-argument J(Jbuf, x, p), JacobianMatrixJVP evaluates prob.J into its pre-allocated matrix buffer and multiplies by the direction in place. Only a LineSearch other than Static and a Refinement along a reused factor take a JVP, so under any other configuration the analytic J gets no JVP preparation and no second m × n buffer. A Refinement on a step that factorises at x multiplies by the regularised Jacobian that the step holds, in a buffer of its own beside the factor, which the factorisation overwrites. jacobian!!, gradient_hessian!! and jvp!! then dispatch on the object the preparation returned, so the solver makes no runtime choice and carries no ad keyword.\n\nFor a sparse R3 Jacobian on a host Array iterate, an AutoSparse back end takes either an explicit jacobian_prototype on the NonlinearProblem (which prepare_ad wraps in KnownJacobianSparsityDetector) or, when SparseMatrixColorings is loaded and jacobian_prototype === nothing, a sparsity detector such as TracerSparsityDetector() of SparseConnectivityTracer alongside a colouring algorithm such as GreedyColoringAlgorithm(). The GeometricSolversSparseADExt package extension then extracts the detected pattern from the prepared DIJacobian via jacobian_pattern as a SparseMatrixCSC{<:Any, Int} — a SparseMatrixCSC{Bool, Int} for TracerSparsityDetector — so init_linear allocates the sparse factor and Jacobian buffer without a user-supplied prototype where has_sparse_factor holds, and the dense factor elsewhere; when an explicit jacobian_prototype is also given, the explicit prototype takes precedence.\n\nIn R3 the iterate is a vector; another shape raises ArgumentError where the preparation is built. The parameters may be replaced on every call, which is what lets one preparation serve a whole time-stepping loop.\n\nAutoEnzyme reaches the Array path through DifferentiationInterface like any other back end, but it needs runtime activity for the parameter context — AutoEnzyme(; mode = Enzyme.set_runtime_activity(Enzyme.Forward)). A plain AutoEnzyme() raises EnzymeRuntimeActivityError on a residual that broadcasts constant parameters into its result; see KNOWN_ISSUES.md. The mode is the caller's choice, so the package sets none.\n\nThese names are not exported. Reach them as GeometricSolvers.name.","title":"Automatic differentiation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/ad/#GeometricSolvers.prepare_ad","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"prepare_ad(backend, prob, r, x, p)\n\nBuild the AD preparation for the residual of prob at an iterate like x, with a residual buffer like r and a representative parameter set p, and return the object that jacobian!! and jvp!! dispatch on. The values of p, and the parameter object itself, may be replaced on every call.\n\nAutoForwardDiff() gives a ChunkedForwardDiff on every array type. Any other back end gives a DIJacobian on a CPU iterate, and raises ArgumentError on a device array iterate: DifferentiationInterface's jacobian! raises \"Scalar indexing is disallowed\" on an MtlArray, a CuArray and a JLArray. The iterate must be a vector.\n\nWhere prob carries a jacobian_prototype, a back end other than AutoForwardDiff() raises ArgumentError unless the prototype has the size of the Jacobian, and an AutoSparse back end takes that prototype as its sparsity pattern, whatever detector it carries. AutoForwardDiff() does not read the prototype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.prepare_ad"},{"category":"function","location":"api/ad/#GeometricSolvers.prepare_jvp","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"prepare_jvp(backend, prob, r, x, p)\n\nBuild the Jacobian–vector preparation of a solve that forms no Jacobian and reads the product alone, such as a Newton with GMRES: the same DIJacobian that prepare_ad returns, without the prepared jacobian! it would never call. AutoForwardDiff takes the own chunked forward mode of src/ad/chunked.jl on every array type; any other back end takes DifferentiationInterface's pushforward! on a CPU iterate, and raises ArgumentError on a device array iterate, as prepare_ad does. r is a residual buffer like a residual of the problem at x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.prepare_jvp"},{"category":"type","location":"api/ad/#GeometricSolvers.DIJacobian","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"DIJacobian\n\nThe AD preparation of the R3 path through DifferentiationInterface: the back end, DI's prepared jacobian! and pushforward!, and a residual buffer. prepare_ad builds it. The parameters are a context and not a differentiated argument, so each call wraps them in DI's Constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DIJacobian"},{"category":"type","location":"api/ad/#GeometricSolvers.DIStationarity","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"DIStationarity\n\nThe R3 AD preparation for StationarityProblem with any AbstractADType back end other than AutoForwardDiff, on a CPU Array: the gradient of S gives the residual, and the (E, U) block of the Hessian of S gives the Jacobian.\n\nThe Jacobian and the Hessian-vector product are the (E, U) block of one Hessian of S, not the derivative of a gradient map: a back end of finite differences would differentiate an already finite-difference gradient, whose noise then swamps the outer difference. The Hessian is taken over the whole state, so a state far longer than the unknown set costs more here than under AutoForwardDiff(), which sweeps E × U only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DIStationarity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/ad/#GeometricSolvers.ChunkedForwardDiff","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"ChunkedForwardDiff{N, Tg}\n\nThe AD preparation of the R3 path of AutoForwardDiff: N Jacobian columns per residual evaluation, in one pass of ForwardDiff.Dual{Tg, T, N} arithmetic. Tg is the caller's tag, or this solver's own where the caller named none, and the same tag serves the Jacobian and the JVP.\n\nThe four fields are the dual buffers: xdual and rdual carry N partials for jacobian!!, xdual1 and rdual1 one partial for jvp!!. A complex iterate gives Complex{ForwardDiff.Dual{Tg, real(T), N}} buffers, so that a holomorphic residual is differentiated in its complex argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ChunkedForwardDiff"},{"category":"type","location":"api/ad/#GeometricSolvers.ChunkedHessianForwardDiff","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"ChunkedHessianForwardDiff{N, TgIn, TgOut}\n\nThe R3 AD preparation of AutoForwardDiff for StationarityProblem: chunked nested ForwardDiff.Dual workspaces and device-resident index maps e_pos and u_pos so that residual!!, jacobian!!, gradient_hessian!!, and jvp!! allocate zero bytes on CPU Array and perform zero scalar indexing on AbstractGPUArray.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ChunkedHessianForwardDiff"},{"category":"type","location":"api/ad/#GeometricSolvers.JacobianFunction","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"JacobianFunction(f)\n\nThe Jacobian source of a method that carries an operator of its own: f(x, p) is that operator, and jacobian!! calls it in place of the problem's J. The method's operator is not the derivative of the residual, so it serves jacobian!! and never jvp!!.\n\nInternal: wrap the operator in this type to pass it as the source of the R1 jacobian!! call. It holds a function and nothing else, so it is isbits exactly when that function is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.JacobianFunction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/ad/#GeometricSolvers.JacobianMatrixJVP","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"JacobianMatrixJVP(J)\n\nThe R3 JVP preparation when the problem provides an analytic Jacobian prob.J(J, x, p): a preallocated m × n matrix buffer J (m = n unless the problem has more residuals than unknowns) into which jvp!! evaluates prob.J(J, x, p) and multiplies by v via mul!!. Because this buffer is distinct from Newton's Jacobian buffer, evaluating a line-search slope under Jacobian reuse (reuse > 1) leaves the held Newton Jacobian untouched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.JacobianMatrixJVP"},{"category":"function","location":"api/ad/#GeometricSolvers.jacobian!!","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"jacobian!!(J, prep, prob, x, p)\n\nThe Jacobian of the residual of prob at the iterate x, for the parameters p, in the storage of J, and return it.\n\nprep is the Jacobian source. In R1 the output slot J is nothing: nothing calls the problem's J(x, p), an AutoForwardDiff runs tagged ForwardDiff and returns an SMatrix in the element type of a vector iterate or a Number for a Number iterate, and a JacobianFunction calls the method's own function. factorize!! converts the result to the factorisation precision TF. In R3 J is an m × n buffer in the element type of x, m = n unless the problem has more residuals than unknowns: nothing calls prob.J(J, x, p), a JacobianFunction calls s.f(J, x, p), a DIJacobian runs DifferentiationInterface's prepared jacobian!, and a ChunkedForwardDiff runs the own chunked forward mode. The preparation selects the code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.jacobian!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/ad/#GeometricSolvers.gradient_hessian!!","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"gradient_hessian!!(F, J, prep, prob::StationarityProblem, x, p)\n\nEvaluate both the stationarity residual F = (∇S(x, p))_E + b and its Jacobian J = (∇²S(x, p))_{E, U} at full state x and parameters p. In R1 (F === nothing and J === nothing, prep the back end AutoForwardDiff() that prepare_ad returns), one nested ForwardDiff.Dual evaluation of prob.S returns (F, J) in a single call to prob.S. In R3, F and J are written in place and returned as (F, J). The seven-argument form gradient_hessian!!(F, J, prep, backend, prob, x, p) takes prep === nothing and uses backend in R1, and uses prep and ignores backend in R3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.gradient_hessian!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/ad/#GeometricSolvers.jvp!!","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"jvp!!(Jv, prep, prob, x, v, p)\n\nThe Jacobian–vector product J(x, p) * v of the residual of prob, in the storage of Jv, and return it.\n\nWith an AD source, one pushforward and exactly one evaluation of the residual make the merit derivative φ′(α) = F(x + αd)ᵀ J(x + αd) d of a line search cost one directional derivative rather than n. With the source nothing or a JacobianMatrixJVP, the call evaluates the problem's J instead of F.\n\nprep is the JVP source from the problem, never from a method's operator, and it selects the code exactly as in jacobian!!. In R1 the output slot Jv is nothing and the call returns a new value: nothing gives J(x, p) * v from the problem's J, and an AutoForwardDiff one dual evaluation of F. The R1 direction has the element type and the length of the iterate, as the line-search slope does: a direction of another element type is a host MethodError. In R3 Jv is a buffer and prep is a JacobianMatrixJVP, a DIJacobian or a ChunkedForwardDiff.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.jvp!!"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#guide","page":"Methods by regime","text":"This page lists, for each regime, the methods that run in it and the conditions under which each one applies. It names no method as the right one: the choice depends on the problem, its size, the cost of its residual and its Jacobian, and the start. The pages under Algorithms describe each method in full.\n\nThe storage of the iterate selects the regime (see The three regimes). R5 is an R3 iterate with a matrix-free linear solver:\n\nregime iterate entry point linear solver\nR1 value semantics Number or SVector solve a factorisation of a static matrix\nR2 batched an array of Number or SVector, one entry per system solve_batch! a factorisation of a static matrix, per system\nR3 in place an AbstractVector that is not static solve, init, solve! a factorisation of a dense or sparse matrix\nR5 matrix-free an AbstractVector that is not static solve, init, solve! GMRES, with no Jacobian matrix\n\nNewton() takes linsolve = LUFactorization() and globalization = Static() unless the caller gives others (see Nonlinear methods).","title":"Methods by regime"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#The-method-of-a-call-that-names-none","page":"Methods by regime","text":"init(prob, x₀) and solve(prob, x₀) on a NonlinearProblem, a StationarityProblem or a LeastSquaresProblem take the method default_method(prob, x₀) and the parameters p = nothing. The method follows from the problem type, its jacobian_prototype and the storage of the iterate:\n\nproblem iterate method\nNonlinearProblem with a SparseMatrixCSC jacobian_prototype a Vector{Float64} or a Vector{ComplexF64} Newton(; linsolve = UMFPACKFactorization())\nNonlinearProblem, every other prototype and iterate any other Newton()\nStationarityProblem any Newton()\nLeastSquaresProblem any GaussNewton()\n\nThe rules read no size. The LUFactorization() of Newton() selects its factor from the storage of the Jacobian: a static factor in R1, and on a dense array the factor that What runs where lists for its array type. A SparseMatrixCSC prototype on any other iterate takes Newton() as well: a Vector{Float32} gets the Sparspak factor where Sparspak is loaded, and init raises otherwise, as it does when the caller names Newton(). A CuSparseMatrixCSR prototype takes Newton(), which has the cuDSS factor where CUDA and CUDSS are loaded (see Sparse direct solves). The default method is never matrix-free and never a combinator: the problem carries no information that selects either.","title":"The method of a call that names none"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#R1:-one-small-system-as-a-value","page":"Methods by regime","text":"Methods.\n\nmethod condition\nNewton with LUFactorization a square Jacobian\nNewton with LDLTFactorization a symmetric or Hermitian Jacobian; a real Jacobian is read from the lower triangle only, a complex one must be given in full, because both triangles decide between L D L^mathsfT and L D L^mathsfH\nNewton with CholeskyFactorization a Hermitian (real symmetric) positive definite Jacobian at every iterate, read from the lower triangle; a Jacobian that is not positive definite gives SINGULAR; in R3 a host Array only, a device array raises an ArgumentError\nNewton with QRFactorization a square Jacobian, or a LeastSquaresProblem\nNewton with SVDFactorization a rank-deficient or nearly singular Jacobian, or a LeastSquaresProblem; the status reports the numerical rank\nNewton with regularization = μ a singular or nearly singular Jacobian, where the step of J + mu I is acceptable\nQuasiNewton(k), or Newton with reuse = k the Jacobian and its factorisation dominate the cost of a step; the rate is then linear\nRefinement, GMRESRefinement a factorisation precision below the working type, and not a LeastSquaresProblem; in R1 a GMRESRefinement runs the LU-IR passes alone\nGaussNewton a LeastSquaresProblem\nPicard I - omega J is a contraction; it needs no Jacobian, and the rate is linear\nAnderson, Broyden a fixed-point iteration whose map is cheap and whose Jacobian is expensive or unavailable; in R1 the depth is 1 ≤ m ≤ 4\nAdditive, Multiplicative, Repeat, Switch members that run in R1, and a problem that is not a LeastSquaresProblem\nFallback members that run in R1, and a problem that is not a LeastSquaresProblem; each member solves from x_0 in turn until one returns SUCCESS\n\nGlobalizations. Static applies close to the solution. Backtracking, Bisection and StrongWolfe search along the direction from a distant start. DogLeg and LevenbergMarquardt change the direction and continue where the Jacobian is singular or ill-conditioned; they take no factor reuse (see Globalization).\n\nConditions of the regime. The static factorisations unroll over the size of the system, so the compile time and the register use grow with it. A GMRES solve and LeftPreconditioned need an array iterate and raise an ArgumentError here. A factorisation precision below the working type saves registers and memory, not operations. solve differentiates through the solve when x₀ or p carries a ForwardDiff.Dual (see Implicit differentiation); a LeastSquaresProblem raises an ArgumentError there.\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers, StaticArrays\n\njulia> F(x, p) = SVector(x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 - p, x[1] - x[2]);\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem(F);\n\njulia> candidates = (Newton(), Newton(; linsolve = SVDFactorization()), QuasiNewton(3),\n                     Newton(; globalization = DogLeg()));\n\njulia> all(candidates) do m\n           x, status = solve(prob, SVector(1.0, 0.5), m, 2.0)\n           status.code === SUCCESS && x ≈ SVector(1.0, 1.0)\n       end\ntrue","title":"R1: one small system as a value"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#R2:-many-small-systems-in-one-launch","page":"Methods by regime","text":"Methods. solve_batch! runs the R1 loop in one work-item per system, so a batch takes each method of R1 above, with the same conditions. A LeastSquaresProblem has no batch form. PerSystem and BatchParams give each system its own parameters, and batch_summary reduces the status vector.\n\nConditions of the regime. The systems are independent and of the same form. On a Vector the kernel runs on the KernelAbstractions CPU() backend. On a GPU backend the problem enters the kernel by value, so each part of it is isbits: a residual that captures a host array, or a sparse jacobian_prototype, cannot enter a device kernel. On Metal the element type is Float32. This page runs no device example.\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers, StaticArrays\n\njulia> F(x, p) = SVector(x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 - p, x[1] - x[2]);\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem(F);\n\njulia> X = [SVector(1.0, 0.5), SVector(2.0, 1.0), SVector(0.5, 0.1)];\n\njulia> S = Vector{SolverStatus{Float64}}(undef, length(X));\n\njulia> solve_batch!(X, S, prob, Newton(; globalization = Backtracking()), PerSystem([2.0, 8.0, 0.5]));\n\njulia> batch_summary(S).all_converged && X ≈ [SVector(1.0, 1.0), SVector(2.0, 2.0), SVector(0.5, 0.5)]\ntrue","title":"R2: many small systems in one launch"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#R3:-one-system-in-place","page":"Methods by regime","text":"Methods. Each method of R1 runs in R3, with no limit on the depth of Anderson and Broyden, and a GMRESRefinement runs its GMRES-IR stage. R3 adds:\n\nmethod condition\nLUFactorization on a sparse jacobian_prototype a host Vector iterate of a real element type, Sparspak loaded, the element type of the iterate as the factorisation precision, and a Jacobian from an analytic J! that writes the nonzeros of the prototype or from jacobian = AutoSparse(...) (see Sparse direct solves)\nRAS local nonlinearities that a BlockPartition isolates, and one local residual per block\nLeftPreconditioned an inner step N whose fixed points are the wanted roots; each trial point of the outer globalization costs one inner step, and with RAS it needs converged local solves\n\nRefinement converges while the error of the solve in the factorisation precision, roughly the condition number of J times its unit round-off, stays below one, and GMRESRefinement extends that range of condition numbers; where both fail, the step refactorises in the working type. Mixed precision gives the conditions in full.\n\nConditions of the regime. init allocates each buffer once, so a system that is solved more than once goes through init and solve!. On a device array the factorisation methods run on device kernels. On CUDA, LUFactorization, QRFactorization and SVDFactorization call cuSOLVER for BLAS element types, except an SVDFactorization whose rtol lies below its default cutoff √m · eps(R), with m the row count and R the real type of the factor, which runs on the own kernel; on Metal, LUFactorization calls Metal Performance Shaders for Float32 (see What runs where). The residual of a device solve indexes no scalars. On a device array, LevenbergMarquardt runs with SVDFactorization and scaling = :none only, and the sparse LU does not run. This page runs no device example.\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers\n\njulia> F!(y, x, p) = (y .= x .^ 2 .- p; y);\n\njulia> method = Newton(; linsolve = LUFactorization(Float32), refinement = Refinement());\n\njulia> solver = init(NonlinearProblem(F!), ones(4), method, [1.0, 4.0, 9.0, 16.0]);\n\njulia> x, status = solve!(solver);\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && x ≈ [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]\ntrue","title":"R3: one system in place"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#R5:-one-system-without-a-Jacobian-matrix","page":"Methods by regime","text":"Methods. Newton with linsolve = GMRES() holds no n times n Jacobian. Each inner iteration of GMRES takes one Jacobian–vector product, from automatic differentiation or from a JVPFunction.\n\nmethod or option condition\nGMRES an AbstractVector iterate, on the host or on a device, and the square system of a NonlinearProblem\nEisenstatWalkerChoice2 (the default), EisenstatWalkerChoice1 an inner tolerance that is loose far from the root and tight near it\nFixedForcing(η) a constant inner tolerance η\na right preconditioner with methods of refresh! and apply_preconditioner! an operator that is refreshed once per Newton step and applied before every product\nStatic, Backtracking, Bisection, StrongWolfe as in R1\nDogLeg as in R1; it runs on the Arnoldi relation of one unrestarted cycle without a preconditioner\n\nConditions of the regime. R5 applies to a large system whose Jacobian no array can hold. A GMRES solve takes no factor reuse, no Refinement, no LevenbergMarquardt and no LeastSquaresProblem, and it has no implicit differentiation; each of them raises an ArgumentError. A Newton with GMRES is not the outer method of a LeftPreconditioned, and raises an ArgumentError there; it can be the inner method. On a device array no factor is formed. f_calls and jvp_calls count the evaluations of the last solve.\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers\n\njulia> F!(y, x, p) = (y .= x .^ 2 .- p; y);\n\njulia> method = Newton(; linsolve = GMRES(; forcing = FixedForcing(1e-6)));\n\njulia> solver = init(NonlinearProblem(F!), ones(4), method, [1.0, 4.0, 9.0, 16.0]);\n\njulia> x, status = solve!(solver);\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && x ≈ [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0] && jvp_calls(solver) > 0\ntrue","title":"R5: one system without a Jacobian matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#Problems-and-solves","page":"Problems and solves","text":"","title":"Problems and solves"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#The-problem","page":"Problems and solves","text":"A problem holds functions and, for a NonlinearProblem, an optional Jacobian prototype: the residual, optionally its derivative, and optionally jacobian_prototype for a sparse R3 Jacobian in NonlinearProblem; the residual, optionally its derivative, and its length in LeastSquaresProblem; or the scalar action, its equation and unknown index sets, and an optional datum in StationarityProblem. Reparametrized wraps a NonlinearProblem or a StationarityProblem in a coordinate chart x = g(y, p), and Deflated wraps either in a deflation of the roots it is given, so that a solve from the same start can converge to another root. BlockEliminated partitions the unknowns of a NonlinearProblem or a StationarityProblem into inner unknowns y = x[Y] and outer unknowns z = x[Z], eliminates y at each outer iterate by solving the inner equations, and steps on the outer equations with the Schur complement J_zz - J_zy J_yy⁺ J_yz; VariableProjection does the same for a LeastSquaresProblem across all m residual rows by Golub–Pereyra variable projection or Kaufman's reduced Jacobian (BlockEliminated on a LeastSquaresProblem forwards to VariableProjection). The storage of the iterate selects the call form of the functions, so one problem serves every regime. NonlinearProblem, StationarityProblem, LeastSquaresProblem, Reparametrized, Deflated, BlockEliminated and VariableProjection are exported; GeometricSolvers.jacobian_prototype and GeometricSolvers.jacobian_pattern are internal accessors.\n\nA NonlinearProblem also carries the local residuals of a BlockPartition when it is built with the block_residuals keyword: its F is then a BlockResidual that forwards every call to the global residual, and the block functions are read with block_residuals, block_jacobians, block_residual and block_jacobian. J stays the caller's own Jacobian, so every method that reads it is unchanged.","title":"The problem"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#Reduced-space-elimination-with-a-batch-inside-the-residual","page":"Problems and solves","text":"BatchEliminated eliminates a whole batch of independent inner systems from the residual of an outer matrix-free Newton, rather than a block of one unknown vector: the outer solver steps on the m-vector alone, and every evaluation of that residual — the Krylov products of the outer step included — solves the batch and deposits its moments. It is the pattern of the implicit PIC method (Chen2011, pp. 7025 and 7033; LanzkronRoseWilkes:1996), where the outer Newton works on the field and the per-particle orbit solves are the eliminated unknowns. The inner tolerance of each evaluation is the adaptive quadratic max(2varepsilon(R_textin) R_textin(tfrac12min(c g_2(textprev)^2)sqrtN_p)), the batch is warm-started from the previous evaluation — so a second evaluation at the same iterate and tolerance takes 0 inner iterations — and the outer Jacobian–vector product is a one-sided difference at the step varepsilon max(1 x)v, with the relative step varepsilon = sqrtoperatornameeps(R_texteff) of the coarser of the two precisions, so that a Float32 batch inside a Float64 outer solve converges at the tolerance its own accuracy supports and, below it, ends on a stall rather than on a factorisation failure — on the problem of make_pic_elimination_problem, the solve at 2e-4 returns SUCCESS and the one at 1e-10 STALLED. The batch's BatchSummary reports its global residual norm, whether every system converged, and how many did not; deposited_moments returns what the deposit wrote, and reset_batch! returns the problem to its initial state. make_pic_elimination_problem builds the one-dimensional implicit electrostatic PIC test problem that the elimination checks solve.","title":"Reduced-space elimination with a batch inside the residual"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#The-solve-interface","page":"Problems and solves","text":"solve adapts the method to R = real(eltype(x₀)), calls init_state and solve!! on a copy of x₀ when x₀ is an array, and returns (x, status); when x₀ or p carries a ForwardDiff.Dual leaf, solve on a NonlinearProblem strips duals via StripDual, runs the primal solve, and on SUCCESS differentiates the root implicitly at x*, while on a StationarityProblem it raises an ArgumentError. With the same arguments, solver = init(prob, x₀, method, p; options, kwargs...) pre-allocates a NonlinearSolver (or a ReparametrizedSolver for a Reparametrized problem) that holds a copy of an array x₀ and p, solve!(solver) solves into solver.x and returns (solver.x, status), and solve!(solver, x, p) solves from another x with other p of the same types, in place into an array x. init, solve! and solve are the functions of CommonSolve.jl, which the package extends on its own types, and solve!(init(args...)) returns what solve(args...) returns on primal inputs. solve!! takes a solver state from init_state(method_R, prob, x₀, p) and runs the loop in R1 and R3 alike. On a batch X of independent small systems, solve_batch! solves each in place on get_backend(X) with one work-item per system, and PerSystem wraps an indexable collection of per-system parameters so system i receives p.data[i].","title":"The solve interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#Status-and-stopping-test","page":"Problems and solves","text":"Every type in this section is isbits, so a kernel can hold it, and every real field is in R = real(T) for the element type T of the iterate. SolverStatus also carries the range and spectrum diagnostics (rank, nullnorm and gradratio) recorded at the iterate x_k of the last fresh Newton factorisation, not at the iterate x at return; a solve of a LeastSquaresProblem that ends on the stationarity test has factorised afresh at the iterate it returns. With regularization = μ ≠ 0, rank and nullnorm describe J(x_k) + μ I and gradratio describes J(x_k), so the classification below holds for μ = 0 only. For n ≥ 2 (for n = 1, a nonzero scalar Jacobian always gives gradratio = 1, and rank = 1 with an SVD factor), they classify the outcome and a STALLED or MAXITERS return: a consistent rank-deficient system that converged has a small residual (fnorm small) and nothing outside the range (rank < n, nullnorm ≈ 0); a least-squares point with no root has gradratio ≈ 0 (fnorm > 0) and the residual outside the range (textnullnorm approx F(x_k)  0, and rank < n for a square system; a LeastSquaresProblem with more residuals than unknowns can have full rank there); a stall in nearly singular directions has the residual inside the range (nullnorm ≈ 0) at a fine SVD cutoff and outside it (rank < n, textnullnorm approx F(x_k)) at a coarse one; and an in-range stall from conditioning or step acceptance has gradratio bounded away from zero with nullnorm ≈ 0.","title":"Status and stopping test"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#SciMLBase-extension","page":"Problems and solves","text":"When SciMLBase is loaded, the package extension GeometricSolversSciMLBaseExt adds methods of solve and init for a SciMLBase.NonlinearProblem and a method Newton or Picard. Both translate the problem and call the native solve or init, so solve returns (x, status), init returns a NonlinearSolver that holds a copy of an array u0, and solve!(init(prob, method; kwargs...)) returns what solve(prob, method; kwargs...) returns; the native solve! serves the solver. A SciML caller maps status.code to a SciMLBase.ReturnCode.T with sciml_retcode (GeometricSolvers.sciml_retcode(status.code)), which is public and not exported.\n\nThe call form follows SciMLBase.isinplace(prob) and u0 = prob.u0: an out-of-place f(u, p) with a StaticArray or a Number u0 solves in R1 and returns a value of the type of u0 (with an SVector u0, an out-of-place jac(u, p) must return an SMatrix, as the native R1 J does); an in-place f(du, u, p) with an array u0 that is not a StaticArray solves in R3 on a copy of u0; an out-of-place f(u, p) with such an array u0 solves in R3 through a closure wrapper (y, x, p) -> (y .= f(x, p)) (and (A, x, p) -> (A .= jac(x, p)) for prob.f.jac), which allocates as f and jac do; and an in-place f with a StaticArray or a Number u0 raises an ArgumentError. prob.f.jac becomes the J of the native problem (overridden by a method's own jacobian field), and prob.p is passed unchanged, SciMLBase.NullParameters() included. A problem with jac_prototype, sparsity, lb or ub set raises an ArgumentError.\n\nThe extension merges prob.kwargs with the call's keywords (the call's winning) and accepts the closed set abstol, reltol, maxiters and options: abstol maps to f_abstol (and sets f_reltol = 0 when reltol is not given), reltol maps to f_reltol, maxiters maps to max_iterations, and options passes an Options alone; any other keyword, or options together with another keyword, raises an ArgumentError.","title":"SciMLBase extension"},{"category":"section","location":"api/problems/#IntervalArithmetic-extension","page":"Problems and solves","text":"When IntervalArithmetic is loaded, the package extension GeometricSolversIntervalArithmeticExt adds a method of certify for a NonlinearProblem and an iterate x that is a real floating-point Number, a StaticVector or a host Vector. It runs Krawczyk's test in intervals of the element type of x and returns (certified, enclosure, guaranteed): whether a box around x holds exactly one root, an enclosure of that root, and whether IntervalArithmetic guarantees the intervals of F(x) and J(X). certify is public and not exported (GeometricSolvers.certify); without the extension it has no method.","title":"IntervalArithmetic extension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.AbstractNonlinearProblem","page":"Problems and solves","text":"AbstractNonlinearProblem\n\nThe supertype of the problems a NonlinearSolver solves: NonlinearProblem, whose residual has as many entries as the iterate has variables, StationarityProblem, whose residual is a part of the gradient of a scalar action, and LeastSquaresProblem, whose residual may have more entries than the iterate has variables. init, solve, solve! and solve!! take it, and so does Newton with each of its globalizations, so one solver loop serves every problem type; Picard, the combinators Additive, Multiplicative, Repeat and Switch, and solve_batch! take a NonlinearProblem or a StationarityProblem, and a combinator raises an ArgumentError for a LeastSquaresProblem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.AbstractNonlinearProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.NonlinearProblem","page":"Problems and solves","text":"NonlinearProblem(F; jacobian_prototype = nothing)\nNonlinearProblem(F, J; jacobian_prototype = nothing)\nNonlinearProblem(F, J; jacobian_prototype = nothing,\n    block_residuals = nothing, block_jacobians = nothing)\n\nThe residual F of a nonlinear system, optionally its derivative J, and optionally a matrix jacobian_prototype giving the storage type and sparsity pattern of the Jacobian. With no prototype (jacobian_prototype = nothing), the problem holds functions only — no parameters, no iterate and no flag — so it is isbits when F and J are and a kernel can hold it.\n\nWith block_residuals given, the problem also carries one local residual F_i!(f_i, x_i, x, p) per block of a BlockPartition — writing the residual of block i on its own index set into f_i, reading external couplings from the frozen full iterate x — and optionally one block Jacobian J_i!(J_i, x_i, x, p) per block, and its F is the BlockResidual that holds them. J stays as the caller gives it; RAS reads the block functions and every other method the global ones. block_jacobians without block_residuals, and the two of different lengths, raise an ArgumentError.\n\nThe call form follows the storage of the iterate, and residual!! selects it on the storage alone: with no buffer slot the call is F(x, p), for a Number, an MVector — which counts as a value — and an Array alike. J is the derivative of F, which jvp!! and jacobian!! read for the source nothing; AutoForwardDiff instead differentiates F. A method's own operator is a separate Jacobian source. When jacobian_prototype is a SparseMatrixCSC, Newton allocates its Jacobian buffer and linear factorisation from that pattern and AutoSparse uses its sparsity pattern directly. Its Jacobian then comes from an analytic J or an AutoSparse jacobian: AutoForwardDiff(), which is the source of Newton(; jacobian = nothing) without J, writes a dense Jacobian only, so Newton with it and a sparse prototype raises ArgumentError when it initialises. A linear solver exists for a host Vector iterate with a SparseMatrixCSC{<:Any, Int} prototype in two configurations: a real iterate with Sparspak loaded and an LUFactorization in the iterate's element type, and a Float64 or ComplexF64 iterate with UMFPACKFactorization; a QRFactorization, an SVDFactorization and a dense prototype raise MethodError when Newton initialises, and ArgumentError with UMFPACKFactorization. With CUDA and CUDSS loaded, a CuVector iterate with a CuSparseMatrixCSR prototype and an LUFactorization in its element type has the cuDSS factor. On a device iterate with a SparseMatrixCSC prototype, Newton(; jacobian = nothing) with an analytic J raises MethodError from the factor, or ArgumentError with UMFPACKFactorization, and an AutoSparse jacobian raises ArgumentError in prepare_ad first, as any back end other than AutoForwardDiff() does. Picard and a static or batched iterate ignore the prototype.\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x .* x .- 4);\n\njulia> prob.F(3.0, nothing)\n5.0\n\njulia> prob.J === nothing && prob.jacobian_prototype === nothing\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.NonlinearProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.StationarityProblem","page":"Problems and solves","text":"StationarityProblem(S; E = :, U = :, b = nothing)\nStationarityProblem(S, E, U, b = nothing)\n\nA stationarity problem (∇S(x, p))_E + b = 0 for the unknown entries x[U] of a state x, with scalar action S(x, p), stationarity-equation index set E, unknown index set U, and optional datum b.\n\nThe residual and its Jacobian are\n\nF_a(u p) = fracpartial Spartial x_E_a(x(u) p) + b_a qquad\nJ_a c(u p) = fracpartial^2 Spartial x_E_apartial x_U_c(x(u) p)\nqquad a c in 1 dots E\n\nwhere x(u)_i = u_c for i = U_c and x(u)_i = (x_0)_i for i notin U. The action S(x, p) is never used as a line-search or trust-region merit function: the merit is always F_2^2 = (nabla S)_E + b_2^2, because stationarity points of physical actions include saddle points where S is indefinite.\n\nWhere E == U, J is a block of the Hessian of S and is symmetric. For other index sets it is not symmetric in general, so LDLTFactorization, which reads only the lower triangle, does not apply, and LUFactorization or QRFactorization does.\n\nThe state x must be real. For a complex state the default automatic differentiation raises an error: an ArgumentError from ForwardDiff for an array iterate, and a MethodError for an R1 iterate. A StationarityProblem has no implicit differentiation: init, solve, solve! and solve_batch! raise an ArgumentError for a ForwardDiff.Dual in the iterate or in p.\n\nArguments\n\nS: a scalar callable S(x, p) returning a scalar in the element type of x.\nE: : (all indices 1:length(x₀)) or a duplicate-free collection of positive integer indices (Tuple, SVector, AbstractUnitRange, or AbstractVector{<:Integer}). A Tuple of integers is converted to SVector{N, Int} so an R1 problem is isbits. An R1 iterate (a StaticVector or a Number) takes :, a Tuple or an SVector, and any other index set raises an ArgumentError at the solve.\nU: : or a duplicate-free collection of positive integer indices of the same length as E. E and U are both : or both index sets.\nb: nothing (zero datum), a vector of length |E|, a Number, or a callable b(p) returning a vector of length |E| or a Number. The keyword b on init, solve, solve! and solve_batch! overrides prob.b for that solve; in solve_batch! a PerSystem b gives system i the datum b.data[i].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StationarityProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.LeastSquaresProblem","page":"Problems and solves","text":"LeastSquaresProblem(F)\nLeastSquaresProblem(F, J)\nLeastSquaresProblem(F, m::Integer)\nLeastSquaresProblem(F, J, m::Integer)\nLeastSquaresProblem(F, J = nothing; output_length = nothing)\n\nA nonlinear least-squares problem min_x tfrac12F(x p)_2^2 with residual F of length m in n variables (m ge n) and optional Jacobian J.\n\nFor out-of-place problems (SVector or Number), m is inferred from F(x, p) and the field output_length may be nothing. For in-place problems (F(r, x, p) on mutable arrays), output_length is required and must satisfy m >= 1 and m >= length(x).\n\nA LeastSquaresProblem holds no Jacobian prototype, so its Jacobian is dense. It has no implicit differentiation: solve with a ForwardDiff.Dual in x₀ or p raises an ArgumentError, as init and solve! do for every problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LeastSquaresProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.Reparametrized","page":"Problems and solves","text":"Reparametrized(prob, g, ∂g = nothing;\n               covariant = prob isa StationarityProblem, curvature = true)\n\nCoordinate-chart wrapper x = g(y, p) around an AbstractNonlinearProblem prob (NonlinearProblem or StationarityProblem).\n\nSolving a Reparametrized problem iterates in the chart coordinates y. solve and init / solve! return (x*, status) and solve!! returns (x*, status, state), where x* = g(y*, p) is evaluated at the returned chart iterate y*; solve_batch! writes each system's x* into the batch. For a StationarityProblem, the keyword b of init and solve sets the datum of the inner problem, as it does without the chart; with any other inner problem, b raises an ArgumentError.\n\nArguments and keywords\n\nprob: the inner NonlinearProblem or StationarityProblem in x-coordinates. A StationarityProblem needs U = : or U = 1:n in order, for the chart dimension n, and E = : or a permutation of 1:n; init, solve, solve_batch! and prepare_ad raise an ArgumentError for any other index set. For an R1 iterate the index sets are :, a tuple or an SVector, as for a StationarityProblem without a chart, so a range such as 1:n raises an ArgumentError there.\ng: the chart map x = g(y, p) in R1 (StaticVector or Number) or g!(x, y, p) in R3 (AbstractVector).\n∂g = nothing: optional chart Jacobian G = ∂g(y, p) in R1 or ∂g!(G, y, p) in R3. When ∂g === nothing, G(y, p) = ∂g/∂y is computed by forward-mode automatic differentiation.\ncovariant::Bool: when false (default for NonlinearProblem), the transformed residual and Jacobian are tildeF(y p) = F(g(y p) p) and tildeJ(y p) = J(g(y p) p) G(y p). When true (default for StationarityProblem), the residual is pulled back covariantly as tildeF(y p) = G(y p)^top F(g(y p) p).\ncurvature::Bool = true: when covariant == true, includes the chart-curvature term (partial G^top  partial y) F(g(y p) p) in tildeJ(y p) = G^top J G + (partial G^top  partial y) F when true, or uses the pull-back approximation tildeJ(y p) = G^top J G when false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Reparametrized"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.Deflated","page":"Problems and solves","text":"Deflated(prob, roots; power = 2, shift = 1)\nDeflated(prob, roots, power, shift)\n\nShifted norm deflation of the known roots roots around NonlinearProblem or StationarityProblem prob: the problem whose residual is\n\nF_mathrmdef(x) = M(x) F(x) qquad M(x) = prod_i=1^k left( x - r_i_2^-p + sigma right)\n\nand whose Jacobian is the product rule of that product,\n\nJ_mathrmdef(x) = M(x) J(x) + F(x) nabla M(x)^top\n\nso that, for p > 1/2, the known simple roots r₁, …, r_k repel the full steps of Newton started from the same x₀ (Farrell, Birkisson and Funke 2015, Deflation techniques for finding distinct solutions of nonlinear partial differential equations). Near a simple root rᵢ, a Newton step on the deflated residual multiplies x - rᵢ by about -p/(1-p) for p ≠ 1, whose modulus exceeds one for p > 1/2, and leaves the neighbourhood of rᵢ for p = 1, which is what pushes the iterate away from it. A known root that is not simple can still attract the solve, and so can a simple one under a line search, which shortens the step that overshoots it: with p = 3/4, F(x) = x^2 deflated at 0 from x₀ = 1/2, and F(x) = x deflated at 0 from x₀ = 0.1 with Backtracking, both return SUCCESS near 0. Deflation does not guarantee that another root exists or that the solve reaches one either: F(x) = x - 1 deflated at 1 from x₀ = 0.6 ends with MAXITERS. The caller reads the returned status and compares the solution with the known roots.\n\nThe steps and the line search take the deflated residual, but the stopping test of Options reads the inner one: status.fnorm is ‖F(x)‖, f_abstol bounds it, and f_reltol is relative to ‖F(x₀)‖, so a deflated root meets the tolerance of a solve of prob.\n\npower (p > 1/2; p ≤ 1/2 raises an ArgumentError, because a solve with it can return SUCCESS even at a known simple root under full steps) and shift (σ > 0) are stored in T = float(real(eltype(first(roots)))), so Float32 roots give power::Float32 and a Float32 product; a solve needs an iterate of that element type and a real one.\n\nRoots\n\nroots is a non-empty Tuple of roots — a Number for a Number iterate, an SVector for an R1 iterate, an array of the iterate's own back end for an R3 iterate (a Vector for a Vector, a JLArray or an MtlArray for one on that device) — or an AbstractVector of real vectors. Every root has the length of the iterate and finite real entries, and the roots of one set share their element type and are all numbers or all vectors. A solve on an array iterate raises an ArgumentError for a root on another back end or a static root, because the deflation broadcasts x .- r on the iterate's back end. The AbstractVector form is not isbits, so solve_batch! refuses it: the batch kernel takes the problem by value.\n\nFor a wrapped StationarityProblem a root is a full state, as solve returns it, and the distance is taken on the unknowns x_U: M and ∇M are functions of the |U| entries the solve steps on, so ∇M has length |U| whatever the length of the state.\n\nEvaluation\n\nAt a deflated root (x = rᵢ) the factor mᵢ is Inf, so a solve from x₀ = rᵢ returns NONFINITE at 0 iterations, and a trial step onto rᵢ has a non-finite merit, which Backtracking shrinks away from by the step min(0.1α, √α √αmin). A factor that overflows (‖x - rᵢ‖^{-p} = Inf, as at 10^{-200} in Float64) does the same. Every coordinate of ∇mᵢ is evaluated unit-scaled, -(p ((uⱼ/d) ϕ)/d) with d = ‖u‖ and ϕ = d^{-p}, which forms no ϕ/d² and so leaves no NaN where uⱼ is zero: the unscaled -(p ϕ/d²) uⱼ overflows to Inf at d = 10^{-100} and gives Inf * 0.0 = NaN there. Where ‖u‖^{-p-1} overflows although ‖u‖^{-p} does not (between about 10^{-155} and 10^{-103} in Float64 for p = 2), ∇mᵢ has an infinite coordinate and the Jacobian is not finite.\n\nA Newton with a callable jacobian raises an ArgumentError, because Newton would take it as the deflated Jacobian. Give an analytic Jacobian of F to the wrapped NonlinearProblem instead.\n\nImplicit differentiation\n\nsolve on a Deflated(NonlinearProblem) with a ForwardDiff.Dual in x₀ or p runs the primal deflated solve and differentiates the undeflated prob implicitly at the root it found, as solve does on the problem itself. A Deflated(StationarityProblem) refuses a dual with an ArgumentError, as a StationarityProblem does.\n\nR2\n\nsolve_batch! solves every system of a batch under one Deflated, so each system is deflated at the same roots. The batch of a wrapped StationarityProblem takes the datum of its solves from the keyword b, shared or PerSystem, or from the problem's own b.\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x^3 - x);\n\njulia> def = Deflated(prob, (0.0,));\n\njulia> solve(def, 0.1, Newton())[1] ≈ 1 || solve(def, 0.1, Newton())[1] ≈ -1\ntrue\n\njulia> solve(Deflated(prob, (0.0,)), 0.0, Newton())[2].code === NONFINITE\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Deflated"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.BlockEliminated","page":"Problems and solves","text":"BlockEliminated(prob, inner; outer = nothing, inner_eqs = inner, outer_eqs = outer,\n                inner_method = Newton(; linsolve = SVDFactorization()),\n                linear_inner = false, inner_options = nothing)\n\nPartition the unknowns x of a NonlinearProblem or StationarityProblem prob into inner indices Y = inner (y = x[Y]) and outer indices Z = outer (z = x[Z], defaulting to the complement of inner in 1:n), and the residual equations into inner equations E_y = inner_eqs and outer equations E_z = outer_eqs (|E_y| == |Y|, |E_z| == |Z|). The default outer_eqs = outer takes the outer unknown indices as equation indices, so give outer_eqs whenever inner_eqs differs from inner. On a LeastSquaresProblem, BlockEliminated(prob, inner; kwargs...) forwards to VariableProjection.\n\nAt each outer iterate z, the inner equations F_E_y(y z) = 0 are solved for y^*(z): in one linear solve y^*(z) = -J_yy(0 z)^+ F_E_y(0 z) when linear_inner = true, or from the previous inner solution y_warm when linear_inner = false, with the adaptive tolerance tau_textinner = max(10varepsilonmax(1 F_E_y^0_2) min(c 05 F_E_z(textprev)_2^2)), where c is the tolerance cap of inner_options (unbounded when they are nothing) and F_E_y^0_2 is the inner residual at the warm start. The cap stands alone before the first outer residual exists, so the tolerance is max(10varepsilonmax(1 F_E_y^0_2) c) with inner_options and the floor alone without them. The inner iteration is Newton's method with step halving (at most 8 halvings per step) and at most max_iterations steps of inner_options (50 without them); inner_method supplies only the linear solver of these steps and of the Schur complement (inner_method.linsolve for a Newton, or a linear method itself). The outer solver then steps on the reduced residual F_textred(z) = F_E_z(tilde y(z) z), where for linear_inner = false and an inner residual above the floor tilde y(z) = y - J_yy(y z)^+ F_E_y(y z) is the inner iterate y refined by one Newton correction (it agrees with F_E_z(y^*(z) z) to first order in F_E_y), and otherwise tilde y = y; the outer Jacobian is the Schur complement\n\nS(z) = J_zz(y^*(z) z) - J_zy(y^*(z) z) J_yy(y^*(z) z)^+ J_yz(y^*(z) z)\n\nAt return, x^*Y = y^*(z^*) and x^*Z = z^*. Any inner solver failure or rank-deficient inner Jacobian (fac_yy.rank < |Y| under SVD) increments status.step_failures. The linear solve of linear_inner = true solves the inner equations only when they are affine in y: an inner residual F_E_y(y^*(z) z)_2 above sqrtvarepsilonmax(1 F_E_y(0 z)_2) also counts as an inner failure, and the solve goes on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.BlockEliminated"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.VariableProjection","page":"Problems and solves","text":"VariableProjection(prob::LeastSquaresProblem, inner; outer = nothing,\n                   linsolve = SVDFactorization(), jacobian_mode = :kaufman,\n                   linear_inner = true,\n                   inner_method = GaussNewton(; linsolve = SVDFactorization()),\n                   inner_options = nothing)\n\nGolub–Pereyra variable projection for a LeastSquaresProblem prob, partitioning the unknowns x into inner (linear or fast) parameters Y = inner (y = x[Y]) and outer parameters Z = outer (z = x[Z], defaulting to the complement of inner in 1:n) across all m residual rows.\n\nFor each outer iterate z, the inner subproblem min_y tfrac12r(y z)_2^2 is solved for y^*(z) (in one linear least-squares solve y^*(z) = -Phi(z)^+ r(0 z) with Phi(z) = partial r  partial y when linear_inner = true, or by a Gauss–Newton iteration with step halving, warm-started from y_warm, when linear_inner = false). linsolve factorises Phi(z) when linear_inner = true; with linear_inner = false the linear solver of inner_method (inner_method.linsolve for a Newton, or a linear method itself) does, in the iteration and in the reduced Jacobian. The outer solver steps on the reduced residual r_textred(z) = r(y^*(z) z) with reduced Jacobian (m × |Z|):\n\njacobian_mode = :kaufman (default): Kaufman's first-term reduced Jacobian J_textred(z) = (I - Phi(z)Phi(z)^+) D(z), where D(z) = partial r(y^*(z) z)partial z.\njacobian_mode = :golub_pereyra: the full Golub–Pereyra reduced Jacobian J_textred(z) = (I - Phi(z)Phi(z)^+) D(z) - (Phi(z)^+)^H (partialPhipartial z)^H r_textred(z).\n\nAny inner solver failure or rank-deficient Phi increments status.step_failures. The linear solve of linear_inner = true minimises over y only when r is affine in y: an inner gradient Phi(y^*)^H r(y^* z)_2 above sqrtvarepsilonmax(1 r(0 z)_2)max(1 Phi(y^*)_F) also counts as an inner failure, and the solve goes on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.VariableProjection"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.jacobian_prototype","page":"Problems and solves","text":"jacobian_prototype(prob)\n\nReturn the Jacobian prototype matrix stored on prob, or nothing when none is supplied. A StationarityProblem and a LeastSquaresProblem hold no prototype, so their Jacobians are dense.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.jacobian_prototype"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.jacobian_pattern","page":"Problems and solves","text":"jacobian_pattern(prob, jacprep)\n\nReturn the Jacobian sparsity prototype for prob and jacprep: prob.jacobian_prototype for a NonlinearProblem, and nothing for any other problem and for nothing. The GeometricSolversSparseADExt extension adds a method for a NonlinearProblem with no prototype and a DIJacobian of an AutoSparse back end, which returns the pattern that back end's sparsity detector found; a back end with no detector finds only a pattern that is true everywhere, so that method returns nothing and the dense path runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.jacobian_pattern"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.BlockResidual","page":"Problems and solves","text":"BlockResidual(F, block_residuals, block_jacobians)\n\nThe residual of a NonlinearProblem that also carries the local residuals of a BlockPartition: the global residual F, the tuple of block residuals, and the tuple of block Jacobians or nothing. A call forwards every argument to the global F unchanged, so every method that calls the problem's residual reads one residual per entry of the iterate; RAS reads the block functions through block_residuals and block_jacobians instead, and calls the global F no times during a sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.BlockResidual"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.block_residuals","page":"Problems and solves","text":"block_residuals(prob)\n\nThe tuple of block residuals of prob, each of the form F_i!(f_i, x_i, x, p), or nothing when prob carries none. A problem built without the block_residuals keyword, and any problem that is not a NonlinearProblem, gives nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.block_residuals"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.block_jacobians","page":"Problems and solves","text":"block_jacobians(prob)\n\nThe tuple of block Jacobians of prob, each of the form J_i!(J_i, x_i, x, p), or nothing when prob carries none. A block without a Jacobian of its own is differentiated by RAS through AutoForwardDiff() on its block residual.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.block_jacobians"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.block_residual","page":"Problems and solves","text":"block_residual(prob, i)\n\nThe block residual of block i of prob, in the form F_i!(f_i, x_i, x, p).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.block_residual"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.block_jacobian","page":"Problems and solves","text":"block_jacobian(prob, i)\n\nThe block Jacobian of block i of prob, in the form J_i!(J_i, x_i, x, p), or nothing when block i has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.block_jacobian"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.BatchEliminated","page":"Problems and solves","text":"BatchEliminated(outer_F!, inner_prob, Y₀, deposit!, moments; per_system = nothing,\n                inner_method = Newton(), inner_options = nothing,\n                inner_atol_cap = 1e-2, eliminated_indices = nothing,\n                workgroupsize = 64)\n\nReduced-space nonlinear elimination with an R2 batch inside the outer residual: the outer solver steps on the m-vector that outer_F! returns, and every evaluation of that residual — the Jacobian–vector products of a matrix-free outer Newton included — solves the whole batch of length(Y₀) independent inner systems and deposits its moments.\n\ninner_prob is the NonlinearProblem of one inner system, solved for every system by solve_batch! from the batch's warm start Y, which the problem keeps in place across outer evaluations; Y₀ is the initial batch state and is never mutated, and Y is a host Vector copy of it, so the batch runs on the CPU backend whatever the array type of Y₀; per_system is the indexable per-system data of the batch, or nothing for a batch whose systems share all their parameters. deposit!(moments, Y, shared, per_system) fills moments from the batch Y and the shared parameter, and outer_F!(F_out, x, moments, shared) writes the outer residual at x — where shared is the outer iterate converted to real(eltype(eltype(Y₀))), which is what every system of the batch gathers. eliminated_indices are the indices of the eliminated unknowns in the stacked unknown vector, one for each entry of every system, 1:sum(length, Y₀) by default; a set of another length is a DimensionMismatch.\n\nThe inner tolerance of the batch at one outer evaluation is the adaptive quadratic one of LanzkronRoseWilkes:1996 (Thm 5.4 and Cor 5.5),\n\nf_textabstolp = maxleft(2varepsilon(R_textin)\nR_textinleft(tfrac12min(textttinner_atol_cap\ng_2(textprev)^2)  sqrtN_pright)right)\n\nso that the batch residual satisfies g_1  g_2(textprev)^2 wherever g_2(textprev)^2  4varepsilon(R_textin)sqrtN_p. Below that bound the floor binds: no arithmetic in R_in reaches the quadratic, and the batch residual stops at the floor of the batch's own precision instead of following the schedule. Each evaluation records the pair (‖g₁‖, ‖g₂(prev)‖) in the fields g₁_history and g₂_prev_history, with Inf before the first outer residual exists, the index set it eliminated in eliminated_index_log, and accumulates its inner counts in statuses. A batch whose systems did not all converge, with a residual above the coarse ceiling 32varepsilon(R_textin)sqrtN_p, has no value to deposit: the residual of that evaluation is NaN and the outer solve cannot step on it. Under the ceiling the batch has stopped at the floor of its own arithmetic instead, and its deposit stands. The next evaluation takes the cap as its tolerance when the previous residual is not finite, since such a residual narrows nothing. reset_batch! returns the batch to Y₀ and the records to empty.\n\nA second evaluation at the same (x, tolerance) reports 0 inner iterations for every system: the batch state is the root of the first one, and residual!!(F_out, prob, x, p; repeat_same_tol = true) re-uses the tolerance of the previous evaluation instead of narrowing it.\n\nThe outer Jacobian–vector product is a one-sided difference with the step h = varepsilon max(1 x)v, scaled by the iterate and the direction, and the relative step varepsilon = sqrtoperatornameeps(R_texteff), R_eff the coarser of the batch's and the outer iterate's element type (ChenChaconBarnes:2012, p. 5384): a Float32 batch inside a Float64 outer solve converges at the outer tolerance that the batch's accuracy supports and stalls, rather than failing, below it. Both evaluations of the difference are solved at the batch's own floor 2 eps(R_in), so the difference is the field's response and not the tolerance's, and the batch returns to prob.Y_anchor afterwards. The outer Newton takes jacobian = nothing or AutoForwardDiff() for this difference, or a JVPFunction of its own, and a GMRES without a jvp_eltype; any other back end, and a jvp_eltype, is an ArgumentError.\n\nprob.Y, prob.Y_anchor, prob.statuses, prob.moments, the two history vectors and prob.eliminated_index_log are the state of the elimination, and a Jacobian–vector product writes its per-system statuses to prob.probe_statuses rather than to prob.statuses; eliminated_indices, deposited_moments, batch_summary, reset_batch! and make_pic_elimination_problem read and reset it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.BatchEliminated"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.BatchSummary","page":"Problems and solves","text":"BatchSummary{R <: Real}\n\nThe reduction of a batch's per-system SolverStatus vector to one report.\n\nFields\n\nfnorm::R: sqrtsum_i g_i_2^2, the norm of the concatenated per-system residuals, from the fnorm of each system's status, taken in the batch's real element type R.\nall_converged::Bool: every system ended with SUCCESS.\nfailure_count::Int: the number of systems that did not.\nmax_iterations::Int32, total_iterations::Int: the largest and the sum of the per-system iteration counts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.BatchSummary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.batch_summary","page":"Problems and solves","text":"batch_summary(S::AbstractVector{SolverStatus{R}}) -> BatchSummary{R}\n\nReduce the statuses S of a batch to a BatchSummary, on the backend of S: the global residual norm is the 2-norm of the per-system norms through the rnorm seam, and the counts are the mapreduce forms of count, maximum and sum, so no entry of S is indexed by the host and the reduction runs on a device array as it does on an Array. An empty batch converged, with fnorm = 0 and both iteration counts 0.\n\n\n\n\n\nbatch_summary(prob::BatchEliminated)\n\nThe BatchSummary of the inner batch that prob's last outer evaluation solved. A Jacobian–vector product solves the batch into a status slot of its own, so it leaves this summary as the anchor evaluation recorded it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.batch_summary"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.BatchParams","page":"Problems and solves","text":"BatchParams(shared, per_system)\n\nWrap a parameter value that every system of a batch shares together with an indexable collection of per-system values, so solve_batch! passes (p.shared, p.per_system[i]) to system i. It is what a batch whose systems differ in one field and agree in another takes, such as the particles of an implicit PIC step, which each carry their own previous position and velocity and share the field they gather. On a device backend the kernel launch converts both parts with Adapt, as it converts every argument: an array of that backend becomes its device form, and a host Array stays on the host, so shared there is an array of that backend or an isbits value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.BatchParams"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.DepositedMoments","page":"Problems and solves","text":"DepositedMoments(current, density, flux, temperature)\n\nThe four moment vectors a batch deposits on the outer grid, each of the outer grid's length and element type: the current density current, the number density density, the momentum flux flux, and the temperature temperature. deposit_pic_moments! fills one in place, a BatchEliminated holds the one its deposit! wrote at the last outer evaluation, and deposited_moments returns it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.DepositedMoments"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.deposited_moments","page":"Problems and solves","text":"deposited_moments(prob::BatchEliminated)\n\nThe DepositedMoments that prob.deposit! wrote at the last outer evaluation of prob, in place. A Jacobian–vector product deposits into its own slot, so the moments here are those of the residual, not of a perturbation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.deposited_moments"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.reset_batch!","page":"Problems and solves","text":"reset_batch!(prob::BatchEliminated) -> prob\n\nReturn prob to its initial batch state prob.Y_init and clear the records of its outer evaluations: prob.g₁_history, prob.g₂_prev_history, prob.eliminated_index_log, the previous outer residual prob.g₂_prev (Inf again) and the tolerance of the previous evaluation (inner_atol_cap again). The statuses become the zero-iteration SUCCESS of a batch that has not run, and the deposited moments are left as they are, since they belong to the batch state the reset discards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.reset_batch!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.eliminated_indices","page":"Problems and solves","text":"eliminated_indices(prob::BatchEliminated)\n\nThe indices, in the stacked unknown vector of the un-eliminated problem, of the unknowns this reduced-space elimination removes: one for each of the batch's sum(length, prob.Y) unknowns, the entries of every system stacked in turn, 1:sum(length, prob.Y) unless the constructor was given another set. They are fixed for the life of the problem, and prob.eliminated_index_log records the set at every outer evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.eliminated_indices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.make_pic_elimination_problem","page":"Problems and solves","text":"make_pic_elimination_problem(T_in, T_out; N_x = 16, N_p = 64, Δt = 1 // 10) -> (prob, E0)\n\nBuild the one-dimensional implicit electrostatic PIC test problem of a BatchEliminated after Chen2011, in the batch's element type T_in and the outer field's T_out, and return it with the initial field E0.\n\nThe problem is one implicit Vlasov–Ampère step on the periodic domain 0 2pi): Crank–Nicolson in the particle orbits, and backward Euler in the field, whose Ampère residual reads the current deposited at the new time level rather than the time-centred current of Chen2011. It has N_x field nodes, N_p particles, each an SVector{2, T_in} of position and velocity, and the time step Δt. The outer unknown is the field at the new time level; the inner unknown is the whole batch, which is solved per particle by solve_batch! from the PIC1DParams of each particle and the shared field. The particle distribution and the previous field are fixed by the constructor, so two calls with the same element types build the same problem, and one outer evaluation deposits one set of moments bit for bit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.make_pic_elimination_problem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.NonlinearSolver","page":"Problems and solves","text":"NonlinearSolver(prob, x, method, options, state, p)\n\nA preallocated nonlinear solver for NonlinearProblem, StationarityProblem or LeastSquaresProblem prob with method and Options options, holding the workspace and AD preparation in state, the iterate x and the parameters p, in the fields x::X and p::Q. It keeps the element type T and the length n of x and the types X of x and Q of p, which solve! checks. Build it with init(prob, x₀, method, p; options = nothing, kwargs...) and solve with solve!(solver) or solve!(solver, x, p).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.NonlinearSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.ReparametrizedSolver","page":"Problems and solves","text":"ReparametrizedSolver(prob, y, x, method, options, state, p)\n\nA preallocated nonlinear solver for a Reparametrized problem prob, holding both the chart iterate y and the primal coordinate x = g(y, p) alongside method, Options options, the solver workspace state, and the parameters p.\n\nBuild it with init(reparam, y₀, method = Newton(), p = nothing; kwargs...) and solve with solve!(solver) or solve!(solver, y, p), which updates solver.y to y*, evaluates x* = g(y*, p) into solver.x, and returns (solver.x, status).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ReparametrizedSolver"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#CommonSolve.init","page":"Problems and solves","text":"init(prob::AbstractNonlinearProblem, x₀, method, p = nothing; options = nothing, kwargs...)\n\nAllocate and prepare a NonlinearSolver for prob and method from the initial iterate x₀ and the parameters p, with the arguments of solve, so that solve!(init(args...; kwargs...)) returns what solve(args...; kwargs...) returns on primal inputs. The solver holds a copy of an array x₀, so init and solve!(solver) do not mutate x₀; it holds a StaticArray or a Number x₀ and p themselves. Passing both options and keyword tolerances raises an ArgumentError, and so does an options that is not an Options{real(eltype(x₀))}. Any ForwardDiff.Dual in x₀ or p raises an ArgumentError; use solve for implicit differentiation. init does not check prob, which may capture an object whose type holds a ForwardDiff.Dual; a residual that contains a ForwardDiff.Dual on primal x and p raises an ArgumentError in solve!, from solve!!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.init"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#CommonSolve.solve","page":"Problems and solves","text":"solve(prob::AbstractNonlinearProblem, x₀, method, p = nothing; options = nothing, kwargs...)\n\nSolve AbstractNonlinearProblem prob from x₀ with method and parameters p, and return (x, status) where status is a SolverStatus. For an R3 array iterate x₀, solve copies x₀ so the caller's initial guess is not mutated. On primal inputs, solve(args...; kwargs...) returns what solve!(init(args...; kwargs...)) returns, without building a NonlinearSolver.\n\nsolve adapts method to real(eltype(x₀)) with ToReal, builds Options in eltype(x₀) from kwargs... or takes options, calls init_state, and delegates to solve!!. Passing both options and keyword tolerances raises an ArgumentError, and so does an options that is not an Options{real(eltype(x₀))}: a Float32 iterate takes Options(Float32; …), because the default tolerances of Options(Float64) are below what a Float32 solve reaches. The residual norm rnorm is finite up to floatmax, but the merit ‖F‖² of a line search overflows to Inf above ‖F‖ = sqrt(floatmax), and a solve with a line search then returns NONFINITE.\n\nWhen x₀ or p carries a ForwardDiff.Dual (or Complex{<:ForwardDiff.Dual}) leaf, solve strips duals via StripDual, runs the primal nonlinear solve to (x*, status), and on status.code === SUCCESS solves the implicit-function-theorem tangent equation J(x^* p)dotx_k = -(partial F  partial p_k)(x^* p) at x* from a single unregularised factorisation of J(x^* p) and one dual residual evaluation F(hatx p) seeded with zero partials on hatx. Each tangent is one back-substitution with that factor, without refinement, so its accuracy is that of the factor element type of linsolve: with LUFactorization(Float32) on a Float64 iterate, x* is accurate to Float64 and the tangent to Float32. A non-SUCCESS primal solve returns primal values at x* with NaN partials and the primal status; a SINGULAR tangent factorisation at x* performs no back-substitution and returns primal values at x* with NaN partials and status.code === SINGULAR. Nested duals, distinct dual tags or partial counts across x₀ and p, and custom structs not registered with Adapt.@adapt_structure raise an ArgumentError. A combinator method, Additive, Multiplicative, Repeat, Switch, Fallback, Anderson or Broyden, and a preconditioning method, LeftPreconditioned or RAS, has no implicit differentiation: with a ForwardDiff.Dual in x₀ or p, solve raises an ArgumentError before the primal solve.\n\nprob may capture an object whose type holds a ForwardDiff.Dual, such as a ForwardDiff.GradientConfig or a dual work buffer, and solve does not check it. A residual that contains a ForwardDiff.Dual on the primal x and p of the solve raises an ArgumentError from solve!!; an in-place residual that writes a ForwardDiff.Dual into its primal buffer raises the MethodError of that conversion instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.solve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#CommonSolve.solve!","page":"Problems and solves","text":"solve!(solver::NonlinearSolver; b = nothing)\nsolve!(solver::NonlinearSolver, x, p = solver.p; b = nothing)\n\nSolve solver.prob, using the preallocated workspace and AD preparation in solver, store the solution in solver.x, and return (solver.x, status) where status is a SolverStatus. solve!(solver) solves from solver.x with solver.p, the iterate and the parameters that init or the last solve!(solver, x, p) stored, and writes the solution into solver.x when it is an array. solve!(solver, x, p) stores x in solver.x and p in solver.p, then solves from x with p; an array x receives the solution in place and stays in solver.x. Without p, it solves with the parameters that the solver holds. For a StationarityProblem, or a BlockEliminated of one, the keyword b overrides the datum of the solve without reallocating. An x whose element type or length differs from the iterate of init raises an ArgumentError, and so do an x of another type, a p of another type than those of init, and any ForwardDiff.Dual in x or p. A refused call changes neither solver nor x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.solve!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.solve_batch!","page":"Problems and solves","text":"solve_batch!(X::AbstractVector{<:Union{SVector, Number}},\n             S::AbstractVector{SolverStatus{R}},\n             prob::NonlinearProblem,\n             method,\n             p = nothing;\n             order = nothing,\n             options = nothing,\n             workgroupsize = nothing,\n             kwargs...) -> S\n\nsolve_batch!(X::AbstractVector{<:Union{SVector, Number}},\n             prob::NonlinearProblem,\n             method,\n             p = nothing;\n             order = nothing,\n             options = nothing,\n             workgroupsize = nothing,\n             kwargs...) -> NamedTuple{(:SUCCESS, :STALLED, :MAXITERS, :SINGULAR, :NONFINITE, :LINESEARCH_FAILED), NTuple{6, Int}}\n\nSolve a batch of independent small nonlinear systems in place on get_backend(X), assigning one work-item per system.\n\nEach element X[idx] is the initial guess for system idx and is overwritten with its final iterate. Wrap an indexable per-system parameter collection in PerSystem so system idx receives p.data[idx], or in BatchParams so it receives (p.shared, p.per_system[idx]); any other p is shared across all systems. When order is a permutation of 1:length(X), work-item i solves system idx = order[i]; any other order throws. An order that is not isbits needs a method of get_backend, and one that is not an array of the backend of X, such as an array of another backend or a host view, is copied into one. Any ForwardDiff.Dual in X or p raises an ArgumentError. The kernel takes prob by value, so a jacobian_prototype that is not isbits, such as a SparseMatrixCSC, raises an ArgumentError. solve_batch! does not look for a ForwardDiff.Dual captured in prob, so one is not detected in a batch. A workgroupsize of nothing leaves the launch at the backend's default; any other positive value is the workgroup size of the single launch. The Options of the solve may also be passed as the sixth positional argument, solve_batch!(X, S, prob, method, p, options), instead of the options keyword. The forms of a StationarityProblem and of a Reparametrized problem take the same order, options and workgroupsize keywords, and the StationarityProblem form the datum b as well.\n\nThe first form writes each system's SolverStatus into S[idx] and returns S (requiring R === real(eltype(eltype(X))) and get_backend(S) == get_backend(X)). The second form allocates a 1-byte-per-system ReturnCode buffer on get_backend(X), copies the codes once to the host after kernel completion, and returns the six outcome counts as a NamedTuple.\n\n\n\n\n\nsolve_batch!(X, S, def::Deflated, method, p = nothing; order, options, workgroupsize, kwargs...)\nsolve_batch!(X, def::Deflated, method, p = nothing; order, options, workgroupsize, kwargs...)\n\nSolve a batch of independent small systems under one Deflated problem, as solve_batch! does on a NonlinearProblem: every system is deflated at the same roots, and, as for one solve, no system is guaranteed to reach another root. The batch of a wrapped StationarityProblem is deflated on the unknowns of the same full-state roots, and its datum comes from the keyword b, shared or PerSystem, or from the problem's own b. The kernel takes the problem by value, so a Deflated whose roots or inner problem are not isbits — the AbstractVector root storage of an R3 iterate, for one — raises an ArgumentError; a root of another length than the iterate, or an iterate of another element type than the roots, raises one too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.solve_batch!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.solve!!","page":"Problems and solves","text":"solve!!(state, prob::AbstractNonlinearProblem, x₀, method, p = nothing, options = Options(eltype(x₀)))\n\nRun the nonlinear solver loop from x₀ using state = init_state(method, prob, x₀, p) and return (x, status, state). solve!! is the in-place and kernel entry point: inside a KernelAbstractions.@kernel, a caller prepares state₀ = init_state(m, prob, x₀, p) on the host and calls solve!!(state₀, prob, x_i, m, p_i, opt) in each work-item.\n\nAt the start of each call, solve!! evaluates Fx = residual!!(residual_buffer(state), prob, x₀, p) and fnorm₀ = rnorm(Fx), and calls start!! once, which resets the per-solve state of the method (the reuse age and the stalled flag under factor reuse, the radius under DogLeg, the damping parameter under LevenbergMarquardt, and the anchor merit of a line search). Every return goes through finish!!, and solve!! returns the triple that finish!! returns.\n\nWhen fnorm₀ is not finite — an entry of Fx is not finite, or ‖F‖ is above floatmax — it returns NONFINITE with 0 iterations. When the residual test of Options already holds at 0 iterations, it returns SUCCESS. When options.max_iterations == 0, it returns MAXITERS with 0 iterations. options must be an Options{real(eltype(x₀))}; any other raises an ArgumentError, and so does a residual that contains a ForwardDiff.Dual, at x₀ or at any later evaluation inside a step: solve takes dual inputs.\n\nInside the loop, each trip calls step!! and updates status via record. SINGULAR and NONFINITE return immediately, and so does MAXITERS, the code of a step whose inner iteration reached its own cap, such as a RAS step whose local solve did not converge. A LINESEARCH_FAILED step whose stepnorm is zero (meaning the failed line-search trial did not decrease the merit) stops immediately with LINESEARCH_FAILED, unless the stationarity test below holds, which a nonzero-residual least-squares minimum reaches with every step a line search can try; one that decreased the merit keeps its new iterate, increments status.step_failures, and continues. The residual test returns SUCCESS with status.stationary == false; on a LeastSquaresProblem where the residual test does not hold, the stationarity test ‖Jᴴ F‖ / (‖J‖_F ‖F‖) ≤ options.g_tol (with ‖J‖_F > 0 and ‖F‖ > 0) returns SUCCESS with status.stationary == true. The state's diagnostics are those of its last fresh factorisation, at the start of the step, so the ratio is re-measured at the iterate the solve returns before it ends on this test. When neither test holds after a step that is neither STALLED nor limited, a step test that holds returns STALLED at once, and so does a SUCCESS step of zero norm, which leaves the iterate where the next step would start — unless the stationarity test, re-measured at that iterate, holds, which a nonzero-residual least-squares minimum reaches with a step of any length, a zero one included; min_iterations bounds both. After a STALLED step only the residual and stationarity tests apply, and options.max_stalls consecutive STALLED steps return STALLED. After a step whose limited is set only the residual and stationarity tests apply too, because a radius or a damping, not the distance to a root, sets its norm; such a step is not a stall. Reaching options.max_iterations without convergence returns MAXITERS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.solve!!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.PerSystem","page":"Problems and solves","text":"PerSystem(data)\n\nWrap an indexable collection of per-system parameters so solve_batch! passes data[i] to system i. Any parameter value not wrapped in PerSystem — a number, a tuple, an SVector, or a plain AbstractVector — is shared unchanged across every system in the batch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.PerSystem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.ReturnCode","page":"Problems and solves","text":"ReturnCode\n\nWhy a solve or a step ended, stored in one byte so that a status fits a GPU register budget:\n\nSUCCESS: the residual test of Options holds.\nSTALLED: the iterate or the residual stopped improving before the test held.\nMAXITERS: the iteration cap was reached: the cap of the solve, or the cap of an inner iteration inside a step, such as a RAS local solve that did not converge.\nSINGULAR: a factorisation failed, as factorize!! states; it does not throw.\nNONFINITE: a residual, a step or a norm is not finite.\nLINESEARCH_FAILED: the globalisation found no acceptable step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ReturnCode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.sciml_retcode","page":"Problems and solves","text":"sciml_retcode(code::ReturnCode) -> SciMLBase.ReturnCode.T\n\nMap a ReturnCode to a SciMLBase.ReturnCode.T when SciMLBase is loaded. A SciML caller reads sciml_retcode(status.code):\n\nSUCCESS → SciMLBase.ReturnCode.Success\nSTALLED → SciMLBase.ReturnCode.Stalled\nMAXITERS → SciMLBase.ReturnCode.MaxIters\nSINGULAR → SciMLBase.ReturnCode.InternalLinearSolveFailed\nNONFINITE → SciMLBase.ReturnCode.Unstable\nLINESEARCH_FAILED → SciMLBase.ReturnCode.InternalLineSearchFailed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.sciml_retcode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.SolverStatus","page":"Problems and solves","text":"SolverStatus{R <: Real}\n\nThe result of a solve. It is isbits, so a kernel can write one per system, and every real field is in R = real(T) for the element type T of the iterate.\n\nFields\n\ncode::ReturnCode: why the solve ended.\nf_calls::Int16: the evaluations of the residual F of the solve, saturated at typemax(Int16); 0 for a method that keeps no count. A Newton with a GMRES keeps the count, and so does a Repeat of it. The other combinators keep none and report 0: the members of a step-level combinator count only their own steps, and Fallback does not add the counts of the solves of its members.\niterations::Int32: the number of steps taken.\nfnorm::R: F(x) at return.\nstepnorm::R: Δx of the last step.\nstep_failures::Int32: every step-rule failure of the solve, such as a failed line search, in one count.\npromoted::Bool: a factorisation ran in a higher precision than the factorisation precision of its method, after a stall or as a vendor fallback.\nstationary::Bool: the solve converged by the stationarity test rather than the residual test.\njvp_calls::Int16: the Jacobian–vector products of the solve, saturated at typemax(Int16); 0 for a method that keeps no count, the same methods as for f_calls.\nrank::Int32: the numerical rank of the last fresh factorisation where it is an SVD factor with info == 0, else Int32(-1): no factorisation, an LU or QR factor, or a failed SVD factor.\nnullnorm::R: (I - U_r U_r^H) F(x_k) at the iterate x_k of the last fresh factorisation, with U_r the first rank columns of U, where rank is not Int32(-1), else R(NaN).\ngradratio::R: J(x_k)^H F(x_k)  (J(x_k)_F F(x_k)) at the iterate x_k of the last fresh factorisation, zero(R) when J(x_k)_F = 0 or F(x_k) = 0, or R(NaN) when no Jacobian was factorised.\n\nThe three numbers are those of the last fresh factorisation (x_k), not of the iterate x at return; a solve of a LeastSquaresProblem that ends on the stationarity test has factorised afresh at the iterate it returns, so its numbers are that iterate's. With regularization = μ ≠ 0, rank and nullnorm are those of the factored matrix J(x_k) + μ I and gradratio is that of J(x_k), so the classification below holds for μ = 0 only. For a Newton solve with n ≥ 2 (for n = 1, any nonzero scalar Jacobian has gradratio == 1, and rank == 1 with an SVD factor), they classify the outcome and a STALLED or MAXITERS return: a consistent rank-deficient system that converged has a small residual (fnorm small) and nothing outside the range (rank < n, nullnorm ≈ 0); a least-squares point with no root has a ratio near zero (gradratio ≈ 0, fnorm > 0) and the residual outside the range (textnullnorm approx F(x_k)  0, and rank < n for a square system; a LeastSquaresProblem with more residuals than unknowns can have full rank there); a stall in nearly singular directions has the residual inside the range (nullnorm ≈ 0) at a fine SVD cutoff and outside it (rank < n, textnullnorm approx F(x_k)) at a coarse cutoff; and an in-range stall from conditioning or step acceptance has gradratio bounded away from zero and nullnorm ≈ 0.\n\nThe predicates GeometricSolvers.isconverged and GeometricSolvers.isstalled read the code. They are not exported, because they are generic names that a package doing using GeometricSolvers may want for itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.SolverStatus"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.stopping_test","page":"Problems and solves","text":"stopping_test(status::SolverStatus)\n\nName the stopping test that ended a converged solve: :stationarity when status.stationary is set, :residual when the solve converged by the residual test, and :none when the solve did not converge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.stopping_test"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.StepInfo","page":"Problems and solves","text":"StepInfo{R <: Real}\n\nThe result of one step, which record folds into a SolverStatus.\n\nFields\n\ncode::ReturnCode: the outcome of the step.\nfnorm::R: F(x) at the new iterate, so that the loop needs no second reduction and, on a device, no second synchronisation.\nstepnorm::R: x - x.\nfailures::Int32: the step-rule failures inside this step, those of its members included.\npromoted::Bool: the step factorised in a higher precision than its method's.\nlimited::Bool: the step was accepted, but a trust-region radius truncated it or a damping above its initial value shortened it, so its norm is set by the radius or the damping and does not measure the distance to a root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StepInfo"},{"category":"type","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.Options","page":"Problems and solves","text":"Options{R <: Real}\n\nThe stopping test of the loop, and nothing else. It is isbits, and every tolerance is in R = real(T) for the element type T of the iterate, so a Float64 tolerance never reaches a Float32 kernel. The iteration cap of a line search is a field of the line search.\n\nFields\n\nf_abstol::R, f_reltol::R: the residual test F(x)  max(f_abstol f_reltol F(x₀)).\nx_abstol::R, x_reltol::R: the step test Δx  max(x_abstol x_reltol x).\nmin_iterations::Int32, max_iterations::Int32: the bounds on the number of steps.\nmax_stalls::Int32: the number of consecutive stalled steps after which the solve stops.\ng_tol::R: the stationarity tolerance J^top F_2  (J_F F_2) le g_texttol on a LeastSquaresProblem.\n\nOptions(T; kwargs...) builds one from the element type T of the iterate, so the caller never names R. A solve takes only an Options{R} for R = real(T) and raises an ArgumentError for any other: the tolerances of another precision do not carry over, because the Float64 default f_reltol = √eps(Float64) lies below eps(Float32) and a Float32 solve cannot reach it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Options"},{"category":"method","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.Options-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:Number","page":"Problems and solves","text":"Options(T = Float64; f_abstol, f_reltol, x_abstol, x_reltol, min_iterations, max_iterations, max_stalls, g_tol)\n\nBuild the Options of a solve whose iterate has element type T, as Options{real(T)}. The relative tolerances scale with eps(R):\n\nkeyword default\nf_abstol 0\nf_reltol √eps(R)\nx_abstol 0\nx_reltol 2 eps(R)\nmin_iterations 0\nmax_iterations 100\nmax_stalls 2\ng_tol √eps(R)\n\nThe default residual test is relative. The residual cannot fall below about mathrmeps(T) J x, whatever the precision of the residual (Tisseur 2001, Cor. 2.5), and a default cannot know J x. So the default f_abstol is 0, which turns the absolute test off, and the relative test f_reltol F(x₀) decides.\n\nThe stopping test runs before the first step, and min_iterations is 0. From a start at the limiting accuracy one Newton step makes the result worse (Wilkinson, quoted by Tisseur 2001). The relative test cannot see such a start: before the first step it compares F(x₀) with a fraction of itself. So a start at the limiting accuracy returns unchanged after 0 iterations only when the caller sets f_abstol at or above its residual floor, as an integrator that knows the scale of F can.\n\nA negative or NaN tolerance, a negative min_iterations, a max_iterations below min_iterations and a max_stalls below 1 raise an ArgumentError.\n\njulia> opt = Options(ComplexF32; f_reltol = 1e-4);\n\njulia> typeof(opt)\nOptions{Float32}\n\njulia> opt.f_reltol\n0.0001f0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Options"},{"category":"function","location":"api/problems/#GeometricSolvers.certify","page":"Problems and solves","text":"certify(prob::NonlinearProblem, x, p = nothing) -> (; certified, enclosure, guaranteed)\n\nTest with interval arithmetic whether prob has a root near the iterate x, for the parameters p, when IntervalArithmetic is loaded. The package extension GeometricSolversIntervalArithmeticExt adds the method; without it, certify has no method.\n\nThe test is Krawczyk's: with Y the inverse of the Jacobian of F at x, and a box X around x, the operator\n\nK(X) = x - Y F(x) + (I - Y J(X)) (X - x)\n\nis evaluated in intervals. When K(X) lies in the interior of X, the box X holds exactly one root of F, and K(X) encloses it. The first box has the radius of one eps of x, and each failed test widens the box to twice the distance of K(X) from x, plus one eps of x, up to 10 boxes.\n\nThe result is a NamedTuple of one concrete type for both outcomes:\n\ncertified: whether the test holds.\nenclosure: K(X) when certified, in the storage of x with entries Interval{T} for the element type T of x: an Interval{T} for a Number, an SVector or an MVector of them for an SVector or an MVector, and a Vector of them for a Vector. With no certificate, every entry is the whole real line.\nguaranteed: whether every interval of F(x) and J(X) is guaranteed, that is, carries no NG flag of IntervalArithmetic. A number that is not an interval, such as the 1 of 1 - x[1], sets the flag where it meets an interval, and the certificate holds without the guarantee. With no certificate, guaranteed is false.\n\nEvery hypothesis of the test is read from F: ForwardDiff differentiates F for Y and for J(X), and the J of the problem is not read. The call form of F follows the storage of x, as for a solve: F(x, p) for a real floating-point Number and a StaticVector, and F(y, x, p) for a host Vector. F must accept floats, intervals and ForwardDiff duals of both. An error of F is not caught: log of a negative float x throws a DomainError, and an F that branches on a comparison of intervals, such as x[1] < 0, or that calls max or min on arguments whose order the box does not fix, throws IntervalArithmetic's InconclusiveBooleanOperation where the comparison has no single answer.\n\nA singular Jacobian at x, a non-finite x or Y, a box radius that is not finite, an unbounded box X, an unbounded x - Y F(x) or I - Y J(X), and a test that fails on the tenth box each give certified == false. So does an interval of F(x), F(X) or J(X) that is unbounded, or that IntervalArithmetic decorates below dac (defined and continuous): F can then be undefined, not continuous or not continuously differentiable on part of the box, such as log or sqrt across 0, 1/x at 0, floor across an integer, or a kink of abs. The decoration follows the functions that F calls, so abs across 0 refuses also where F is continuously differentiable, such as x * abs(x) + x at 0. None of these throws. A complex iterate, a batch, a device array, a StationarityProblem and a LeastSquaresProblem have no method.\n\ncertify is public and not exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.certify"},{"category":"section","location":"api/composition/#Combinators-and-preconditioning","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"","title":"Combinators and preconditioning"},{"category":"section","location":"api/composition/#Combinators","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"Additive, Multiplicative, Repeat, Switch, Anderson and Broyden compose nonlinear methods through the same init_state, start!!, step!! and finish!! interface as Newton and Picard, so a combinator runs in R1, R2 and R3 alike. Additive combines member steps from the same iterate with OptimalWeights or FixedWeights, Multiplicative and Repeat thread (x, F(x)) sequentially across sub-steps while sync_merit updates any active line-search baseline merit, Switch transitions permanently from first to second once F(x) le textthreshold cdot F(x_0), Anderson accelerates its member with filtered Type-II Anderson mixing, Tikhonov regularisation, restarts and best-iterate restoration, and Broyden accelerates its member over the same shared AndersonHistory, filters and safeguards by solving the lower-triangular Type-II Broyden multisecant equations by forward substitution. A combinator has no implicit differentiation: solve with a combinator method raises an ArgumentError for a ForwardDiff.Dual in x₀ or p.\n\nFallback composes whole solves instead of steps: it runs solve!! with each member in turn, each from x₀, and stops at the first member that returns SUCCESS; fallback_member reads the index of the member whose result a solve returned.","title":"Combinators"},{"category":"section","location":"api/composition/#Nonlinear-preconditioning","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"LeftPreconditioned(outer, inner) runs outer on the residual r^ell(y) = y - N(y) of one step of inner instead of on F, so every trial point of its globalization costs one inner step and every merit value it reads is r^ell_2^2. With the inner method a one-level RAS map over a BlockPartition it is RASPEN, LeftPreconditioned(Newton(), RAS(partition)), whose Jacobian is the exact form from the block factors of the last sweep; RASPEN needs converged local solves. Multiplicative(RAS(partition), Newton()) is NKS-RAS, the same map followed by one Newton step. A RAS sweep restricts each subdomain, solves each block with the package's own factor, and prolongates through the owner sets only, so each index of 1:n is written once and the sweep calls the global residual no times; ras_counts reports the counters of its last solve. A problem with block residuals supplies the block functions, and a block without a Jacobian is differentiated by AutoForwardDiff().","title":"Nonlinear preconditioning"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.Additive","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"Additive(m₁, m₂, ...; weights = OptimalWeights())\nAdditive((m₁, m₂, ...), weights::Union{OptimalWeights, FixedWeights} = OptimalWeights())\n\nParallel nonlinear combinator that runs one step of each member from the same iterate (x, F(x)) to obtain candidates (x_i F(x_i)) and forms x_+ = x + sum_i=1^m w_i (x_i - x) using OptimalWeights or FixedWeights. Under OptimalWeights, a rank-deficient Gram matrix drops the collinear candidate directions and solves the weights over the ones kept, a one-weight secant step when one survives; a trial iterate that violates the PETSc safeguard is replaced by the candidate with the smallest residual norm. Each rule counts one failure in step_failures, and a step on which both fire counts one.\n\nA member whose step fails hard (SINGULAR, NONFINITE, or LINESEARCH_FAILED with a zero step) is left out of the combination: OptimalWeights solves for the weights of the members kept, and FixedWeights rescales the weights of the members kept to the sum of all weights. The step fails, with the code of the first member that failed, when every member fails, or when the fixed weights of the members kept sum to zero. The step is STALLED when every member step is.\n\nThe tuple form takes the weight rule as its second positional argument: a weights keyword after a tuple makes the tuple a single member.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Additive"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.OptimalWeights","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"OptimalWeights(ρ = 1.0)\nOptimalWeights(; ρ = 1.0)\n\nSecant least-squares weight rule for Additive. Minimises the linearised model residual F(x) + sum_i w_i (F(x_i) - F(x))_2 over real weights w_i in mathbbR, drops rank-deficient candidate directions in favour of the candidate with the smaller residual norm, and falls back to the best candidate when sum_i w_i  01, when F(x_+)  rho min_i F(x_i), or when F(x_+) is not finite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.OptimalWeights"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.FixedWeights","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"FixedWeights(w₁, w₂, ...)\nFixedWeights((w₁, w₂, ...))\n\nFixed real combination weights for Additive, forming x_+ = x + sum_i=1^m w_i (x_i - x) without a Gram solve or safeguard evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.FixedWeights"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.Multiplicative","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"Multiplicative(m₁, m₂, ...)\nMultiplicative((m₁, m₂, ...))\n\nSequential nonlinear combinator (composite step G_m circ cdots circ G_1) that threads (x, F(x)) through m₁, m₂, ... in order within a single outer solver iteration. Before each member steps, sync_merit updates its line-search baseline merit to the incoming F(x)_2^2 when the member state has been started. The outer step is STALLED when every member step is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Multiplicative"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.Repeat","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"Repeat(method, count)\n\nSequential nonlinear combinator that runs count sub-steps of method (count >= 1) per outer solver iteration, synchronising any line-search baseline merit between sub-steps and reporting the combined outer step norm x^(textcount) - x^(0). The outer step is STALLED when every sub-step is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Repeat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.Switch","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"Switch(first, second; threshold = 1e-2)\nSwitch(first, second, threshold)\n\nTwo-stage nonlinear combinator that runs first while F(x)  textthreshold cdot F(x_0) and latches permanently to second for the remainder of the solve once F(x) le textthreshold cdot F(x_0). Calling step!! on a state that has not been initialised by start!! (!isfinite(state.fnorm₀)) fails closed with NONFINITE and a zero step norm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Switch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.Fallback","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"Fallback(m₁, m₂, ...)\nFallback((m₁, m₂, ...))\n\nWhole-solve combinator that runs solve!! with each member in turn and stops at the first member that returns SUCCESS. Every member starts from x₀, with the full Options of the solve and its own state, which start!! resets as for a solve with the member alone; solve!! copies an array x₀ into the state before the first member and back into the iterate before each later one, so each member's result is the result of its own solve from x₀. Every code but SUCCESS moves on to the next member, and an exception that a member throws is not caught.\n\nThe SolverStatus is that of the member that succeeded, or of the last member when none did, with iterations and step_failures summed over the members that ran (each saturated at typemax(Int32)), promoted set when any of them set it, and the call counts f_calls and jvp_calls 0. The state that solve!! returns holds the index of that member, which GeometricSolvers.fallback_member reads.\n\nFallback needs at least one member, and raises an ArgumentError with none. It has no step!!, so it is a method of its own and not a member of a step-level combinator such as Switch. Like every combinator, it refuses a ForwardDiff.Dual in x₀ or p in solve and a LeastSquaresProblem in init_state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Fallback"},{"category":"function","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.fallback_member","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"fallback_member(state::FallbackState)\n\nThe index of the member of a Fallback whose result the last solve!! on state returned: the member that succeeded, or the last member when none did; 0 for a state that no solve returned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.fallback_member"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.Anderson","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"Anderson(member, ::Val{m};\n         c_s = 0.2, κ_bar = nothing, restart_factor = 10.0,\n         M_alpha = 25.0, safeguard_cos = nothing, grow_depth = false)\nAnderson(::Val{m}; damping = 1, kwargs...)\nAnderson(member, m::Integer = 4; kwargs...)\nAnderson(m::Integer = 4; damping = 1, kwargs...)\n\nType-II Anderson acceleration of history depth m wrapping an inner nonlinear method member (Picard, Newton, or a combinator). At each outer iteration, Anderson runs one step of member from (x, Fx) to obtain the candidate x_m and correction w_k = x_m - x, updates the history of consecutive iterate differences Δx_j and correction differences Δw_j, filters the history by the length and angle filters of Pollock and Rebholz (2023) from the Gram matrix G_{ij} = Re⟨Δw_i, Δw_j⟩, solves the eps(R)-regularised normal equations (G_K + λI) γ = b_K with b_i = Re⟨Δw_i, w_k⟩, and forms the accelerated iterate x₊ = x_m - ∑_j γ_j (Δx_{K[j]} + Δw_{K[j]}).\n\nIn R1 (StaticVector or Number), m must satisfy 1 <= m <= 4, and the state is isbits for an SVector or Number iterate. In R3 (Array or AbstractGPUArray), any m >= 1 is supported with preallocated n × m difference matrices, and step!! on an Array allocates nothing. When a solve ends with a code other than SUCCESS, finish!! restores the iterate with the smallest residual norm ‖F(x)‖ seen during the solve. A step is STALLED when the step of member is.\n\nThe constructors without a member wrap Picard(; damping), so Anderson() wraps the undamped Picard().\n\nKeywords:\n\nc_s: angle-filter threshold in (0, 1); a history column is kept only if its component orthogonal to the kept columns has squared norm ≥ c_s² G_jj.\nκ_bar: bound of the length filter; nothing selects 1 / sqrt(eps(R)) for the real element type R of the iterate.\nrestart_factor (≥ 3): the history is cleared when ‖F‖ exceeds restart_factor times the smallest residual norm seen.\nM_alpha (≥ 1): bound on ∑_j |α_j| for the affine weights α_j of the member candidates g_{k-j} that x₊ combines, also after a filter has dropped a history column; a step whose weights exceed it, are not finite, or increase the residual of the linear model takes the step of member and counts one failure.\nsafeguard_cos: when set, the history is cleared if the cosine between consecutive corrections w_k exceeds it in modulus.\ngrow_depth: when true, the depth is capped at ceil(-log10(‖w_k‖)) clamped to 1:m, so the cap grows as the corrections shrink.\n\nReferences\n\nS. Pollock and L. G. Rebholz (2023). Filtering for Anderson acceleration. SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 45(4), A1571–A1590. doi:10.1137/22M1536741\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Anderson"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.Broyden","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"Broyden(member, ::Val{m};\n        c_s = 0.2, κ_bar = nothing, restart_factor = 10.0,\n        M_alpha = 25.0, safeguard_cos = nothing, grow_depth = false)\nBroyden(::Val{m}; damping = 0.5, kwargs...)\nBroyden(member, m::Integer = 4; kwargs...)\nBroyden(m::Integer = 4; damping = 0.5, kwargs...)\n\nType-II Broyden (bad Broyden) multisecant acceleration of history depth m wrapping an inner nonlinear method member (Picard, Newton, or a combinator). Shares the difference history, length and angle filters of Pollock and Rebholz (2023), extrapolation safeguards, restarts, and best-iterate restoration of Anderson, differing in the weight solve: instead of the full Gram normal equations, Broyden solves the lower-triangular multisecant system (L_G + λI) γ = b_K by forward substitution, where (L_G)_{c,r} = Re⟨Δw_{K[c]}, Δw_{K[r]}⟩ for r <= c and λ = eps(R) * tr(G_K) / p. The kept columns K are in increasing age, newest first; in this order, and for λ = 0, the weights are those of the sequential rank-one Type-II Broyden updates of group size one (Fang and Saad, 2009), applied oldest column first.\n\nIn R1 (StaticVector or Number), m must satisfy 1 <= m <= 4, and the state is isbits for an SVector or Number iterate. In R3 (Array or AbstractGPUArray), any m >= 1 is supported with preallocated n × m difference matrices, and step!! on an Array allocates nothing.\n\nKeywords are identical to Anderson. The constructors without a member wrap Picard(; damping) with damping = 0.5 by default, so Broyden() wraps a damped Picard, while Anderson() wraps the undamped Picard().\n\nReferences\n\nS. Pollock and L. G. Rebholz (2023). Filtering for Anderson acceleration. SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 45(4), A1571–A1590. doi:10.1137/22M1536741\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.Broyden"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.AndersonHistory","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"AndersonHistory{m, X, DX, DW, A}\n\nImmutable view of the difference history stored in an AndersonState{m} for Anderson and Broyden, returned by state.history.\n\nFields:\n\nx_prev: previous outer iterate x_{k-1}.\nw_prev: previous member correction w_{k-1} = g(x_{k-1}) - x_{k-1}.\nΔX: stored iterate differences Δx_j = x_{k-a_j+1} - x_{k-a_j} (NTuple{m} in R1, n × m matrix in R3).\nΔW: stored correction differences Δw_j = w_{k-a_j+1} - w_{k-a_j} (NTuple{m} in R1, n × m matrix in R3).\nages: column ages (NTuple{m, Int32} in R1, Vector{Int32} in R3).\ndepth: number of active columns currently stored in ΔX and ΔW.\ncurrent_depth: active depth cap (1:m) after the most recent step.\nhas_prev: true once x_prev and w_prev hold a previous step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.AndersonHistory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.LeftPreconditioned","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"LeftPreconditioned(outer, inner)\n\nThe left-preconditioned method of a NonlinearProblem: the method outer runs on the residual r^ell(y) = y - N(y) of the inner map N, one step!! of inner from (y, F(y)), instead of on F itself. Every trial point of outer's globalization therefore costs one inner step, and every merit value it reads is r^ell_2^2. The zeros of r^ell are the fixed points of N.\n\nLeftPreconditioned(Newton(), RAS(partition)) is RASPEN: the merit is on the restricted additive Schwarz map, and the Jacobian of r^ell is the exact form of raspen_jvp! built from the block factors of the last sweep (Dolean:2016, eq. 3.17). RASPEN needs converged local solves: a RAS with a positive tolerance whose local solve does not converge returns MAXITERS, and a LeftPreconditioned step that meets it, at its iterate or at the trial point where its outer step reports NONFINITE, returns MAXITERS too, which ends the solve. A RAS with a balance_threshold is refused at init_state with an ArgumentError, because a skipped sweep returns its input and so reads as a zero of r^ell at a point that is not a root. A Newton with GMRES as outer is refused at init_state with an ArgumentError, because it solves a NonlinearProblem and outer solves r^ell; as inner it is taken. outer may be a combinator, such as Repeat or Multiplicative, whose members step on r^ell; with a RAS inner, every member of an Additive after the first costs one more sweep, at the iterate, because the RASPEN Jacobian reads the block factors of the last sweep. A Deflated problem is refused at init_state with an ArgumentError, because its stopping test reads the deflation factor from a Newton state. With an inner other than a RAS the derivative of r^ell is a central difference, one inner step at each of x pm h v, which prepare_ad prepares at init_state whatever its globalization reads. With a RAS inner that preparation is the RASPEN form, so every sweep forms the block factor at the local iterate it returns even for a globalization that reads no slope: measured on the 5-block P8 problem at one inner iteration per block, a LeftPreconditioned(Picard(), RAS) under Static makes 10 block-Jacobian calls per sweep against plain RAS's 5.\n\nThe solve!! status of a LeftPreconditioned solve is the one of the loop: its fnorm is F(x) of the global residual at the returned iterate, and its convergence tests apply to that residual. init_state raises the ArgumentError of an inner RAS for a problem without block residuals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LeftPreconditioned"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.LeftPreconditionedProblem","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"LeftPreconditionedProblem(prob, inner, state, work, code)\n\nThe problem that outer of a LeftPreconditioned method solves: the user's problem prob, the inner method inner, the inner method's state in the iterate's element type, the iterate buffer that one inner step runs on — a step of an array iterate writes into its x, so the residual of this problem steps a copy — and a Ref that holds the code of the last inner step. Its residual is r^ell(y) = y - N(y), one step!! of inner from (y, F(y)) subtracted from y, so residual!! evaluates it and a LineFunction merits it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LeftPreconditionedProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.LeftPreconditionedState","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"LeftPreconditionedState\n\nThe state of a LeftPreconditioned method: the LeftPreconditionedProblem it runs on, the outer method's state, the buffer of the global residual that a step returns, and the parameters p of the solve, which finish!! evaluates the residuals with. The outer state's residual workspace holds r^ell; the problem's inner state holds the workspace of the inner step, and its counters are the ones ras_counts reports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LeftPreconditionedState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.LeftPreconditionedAD","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"LeftPreconditionedAD\n\nThe AD preparation of a LeftPreconditioned method whose inner state is not a RASState: the iterate and residual buffers of the two points of a central difference, and the direction buffer that seeds one column at a time. jvp!! evaluates r^ell at x pm h v and forms (r^ell(x + hv) - r^ell(x - hv))  2h, and jacobian!! takes one column per call of it.\n\nA central difference, because one step of an inner method is not ForwardDiff.Dual-generic: a method carries the real type of the working precision in its type, so a dual iterate reaches the StepInfo of its globalization and the right-hand-side scaling of its linear factor in the wrong type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LeftPreconditionedAD"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.RASPenPreparation","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"RASPenPreparation\n\nThe AD preparation of a LeftPreconditioned method whose inner state is a RASState: the RAS state, whose block factors the RASPEN Jacobian product reads. jacobian!! and jvp!! apply the exact form of raspen_jacobian! and raspen_jvp!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.RASPenPreparation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.RAS","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"RAS(partition::BlockPartition; max_iterations = 1, rtol = 0, atol = 0,\n    balance_threshold = nothing)\n\nThe restricted additive Schwarz map of one-level nonlinear preconditioning, as a nonlinear solver method of NonlinearProblem with block residuals: one step!! restricts the iterate to each subdomain, takes up to max_iterations local Newton steps with that block's own factor, and prolongates through the owner sets only, so every index is written once. A sweep evaluates each block residual once per inner iteration and calls the global residual no times; the step then evaluates the global residual once at the swept iterate and returns it, so a member of Multiplicative passes the next member a residual of the iterate that the sweep reached. LeftPreconditioned(Newton(), RAS(partition)) is RASPEN and Multiplicative(RAS(partition), Newton()) is NKS-RAS.\n\nAn inner iteration of block i stops before its linear solve when the block residual norm is at most atol + rtol * ‖f_i⁰‖, with f_i⁰ the block's residual at its first inner iteration. With a positive tolerance (atol > 0 or rtol > 0), a local solve is also converged at its residual floor, the bound of the rounding error of its residual: a block residual norm of at most n_i varepsilon J_i_F x_i ends it, with n_i the block size, J_i the block Jacobian of its last Newton step, cdot_F the Frobenius norm and varepsilon the eps of the element type. A local solve that takes all max_iterations steps evaluates its block residual once more, and where that norm is above the tolerance and above the floor, the local solve has not converged: the step returns the iterate unchanged with MAXITERS, which ends the solve, and ras_counts names the block in failed_block. With atol = rtol = 0 every local solve takes max_iterations steps and makes no claim of convergence. With balance_threshold = ρ the step returns the iterate unchanged, as a SUCCESS of zero norm, when max_i R_i F(x) le rho F(x) (CaiLi:2011); the block residuals cost one call per block in that test, and the global residual costs none. LeftPreconditioned refuses a RAS with a balance_threshold.\n\nThe counters of the last start!! are read with ras_counts. A problem without block residuals, a partition whose block count or index count differs from the problem's, and an iterate that is not a vector raise an ArgumentError at init_state; the partition's validation raises one at construction. A jacobian_prototype sizes the global Jacobian only: the block factors are dense. Only a RASPEN product reads the global Jacobian, so on a sparse jacobian_prototype without an analytic J a sweep, and a Picard outer under Static, solve without one, and a reader of the RASPEN product — a Newton outer, or a Picard outer with a line search — raises an ArgumentError at init_state: the ForwardDiff Jacobian of the global residual writes a dense Jacobian only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.RAS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.RASState","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"RASState\n\nThe state of a RAS method: the workspace of every block, the factors of the block Jacobians, which raspen_jvp! and raspen_jacobian! read, the global Jacobian buffer and its preparation — nothing where the problem has its own J, and AutoForwardDiff() on F where it has none; both nothing on a sparse jacobian_prototype without J, where no global Jacobian is built — the composite iterate that a RASPEN Jacobian product forms per block, and the counters that ras_counts reports. Every block factor is re-formed in every inner iteration, and once more at the local iterate that the sweep returns where the block factors feed a RASPEN preparation, which is the factor at the local solution. A factor that no sweep has written fails closed: the product raises an ArgumentError before it reads one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.RASState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.BlockPartition","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"BlockPartition(subdomains, owners)\nBlockPartition(n, nblocks; overlap = 1)\n\nThe index sets of a one-level Schwarz method over 1:n: one subdomain S_i per block, which holds the index set of the local solve, and one owner set S̃_i ⊆ S_i per block, which holds the indices that the block writes back. The owner sets are pairwise disjoint and cover 1:n exactly, so a RAS sweep writes each index once and the map is the restricted form. A subdomain is non-empty and repeats no index, and every index is in 1:n, with n the largest index of any subdomain; any other input raises an ArgumentError. The field local_owners[i] holds the positions of owners[i] in subdomains[i], so that subdomains[i][local_owners[i]] == owners[i].\n\nBlockPartition(n, nblocks; overlap = 1) splits 1:n into nblocks contiguous owner sets of equal or nearly equal size, and extends each into its subdomain by overlap indices on each side, clipped to 1:n. overlap = 0 gives subdomains == owners, which is block Jacobi; n < 1, nblocks < 1, nblocks > n and overlap < 0 raise an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.BlockPartition"},{"category":"function","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.ras_sweep!!","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"ras_sweep!!(state, m::RAS, prob, x, p)\n\nRun one restricted additive Schwarz sweep from the iterate x, overwrite x with the swept iterate, and return it. The sweep restricts each subdomain, evaluates that block's residual once per inner iteration, solves the block system with the block's own factor, and prolongates through the owner sets. It calls the global residual no times. A block factor that reports a zero pivot, a block residual with a non-finite entry, and a local solve with a positive tolerance that does not converge leave x unchanged; the step!! of RAS reports them as SINGULAR, NONFINITE and MAXITERS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ras_sweep!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.ras_prolongate!","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"ras_prolongate!(dest, x_locals, partition)\n\nWrite each block's local iterate back into dest at that block's owner set, dest[owners[i][k]] = x_locals[i][local_owners[i][k]], for the host index sets of partition. Each index of 1:n is written once, because the owner sets of a BlockPartition are disjoint and cover 1:n. A RAS sweep calls the method that takes the device index arrays of its state, so a device iterate is never scattered element by element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ras_prolongate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.ras_counts","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"ras_counts(state)\n\nThe counters of the last solve of a RAS state, as a named tuple:\n\nsweeps: the sweeps run.\nparallel_block_solves, parallel_inner_iterations, inner_iterations: the block solves of the parallel schedule — one per inner iteration of the slowest block of each sweep, summed over the sweeps — and, where a RASPEN preparation reads the block factors of the state, one more per sweep for the block Jacobian that each block forms at the local iterate its sweep returns.\ntotal_inner_iterations, block_solves: every block solve, summed over the blocks and the sweeps, with the same one more per block on such a state.\nfailed_block: the block whose local solve did not converge in the last sweep, which made the step return MAXITERS, or 0.\n\nstart!! resets them, so a second solve! on one solver reports its own solve. A state of LeftPreconditioned with an inner RAS, and a state of Multiplicative with a RAS member, report that member's counters.\n\n\n\n\n\nras_counts(state::LeftPreconditionedState)\n\nThe counters of the RAS state of a LeftPreconditioned method's inner map, as ras_counts reports them. A method whose inner map is not a RAS raises an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\nras_counts(state::MultiplicativeState)\n\nThe counters of the RAS member of a Multiplicative method, as ras_counts reports them. A method with no RAS member raises an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ras_counts"},{"category":"function","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.raspen_jvp!","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"raspen_jvp!(Jv, state, prob, x, v, p)\n\nThe Jacobian-vector product of the left-preconditioned residual r^ell(y) = y - N(y) of the exact RASPEN Jacobian, J_r^ell(x) v = sum_i=1^M tildeR_i^top (R_i J(u^(i)) P_i)^-1 R_i J(u^(i)) v (Dolean:2016, eq. 3.17), with the block factors that the last sweep of state formed and u^(i) the iterate x with the local solve x_locals[i] of block i written over its subdomain S_i. Per block it evaluates the global Jacobian at u^(i), keeps the rows of S_i, multiplies them by v, solves with that block's factor, and prolongates through the owner set. The global Jacobian is the problem's own J where it has one, so a residual that takes no ForwardDiff.Dual serves RASPEN, and AutoForwardDiff() on F where it has none. Jv is overwritten and returned. A state that no complete sweep formed has no block factor to read, so it raises an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.raspen_jvp!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/composition/#GeometricSolvers.raspen_jacobian!","page":"Combinators and preconditioning","text":"raspen_jacobian!(J, state, prob, x, p)\n\nThe Jacobian of the left-preconditioned residual r^ell at x of the exact RASPEN form of raspen_jvp!. Per block it evaluates the global Jacobian once, at u^(i), and solves all columns of its subdomain rows with that block's one factor, so a call takes one global Jacobian per block. It overwrites J and returns it. A state that no complete sweep formed raises an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.raspen_jacobian!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"These names are not exported. Reach them as GeometricSolvers.name.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"section","location":"api/internals/#The-storage-seam","page":"Internals","text":"The storage seam lets an algorithm read each function's result rather than the storage itself, so the storage-specific operations stay at the leaves. Each function has a value method for a StaticArray or a Number iterate (R1) and an in-place method for an AbstractArray buffer slot (R3).","title":"The storage seam"},{"category":"section","location":"api/internals/#The-solver-loop-and-its-helpers","page":"Internals","text":"A nonlinear method enters the loop of solve!! through init_state, which builds its state, step!!, and the two hooks start!! and finish!!, which run once before the first step and at every return. residual_buffer names the slot of the state that holds F(x₀), and refine!! refines the linear direction d in working precision before globalization; gmres_ir!! is the GMRES-IR stage that follows a failed pass of a GMRESRefinement.","title":"The solver loop and its helpers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.buffer","page":"Internals","text":"buffer(x)\nbuffer(x, TF, dims...)\n\nOne storage slot for an iterate like x, in the element type TF and with the shape dims. A caller creates slots during initialization; an algorithm consumes their returned values.\n\nA value iterate — a StaticArray or a Number — has no slot: the call returns nothing, so the R1 state carries no buffer and spends no register on one. A static matrix is never an iterate and gets no slot either, which is why this method is written on StaticArray and not on StaticVector. An array iterate allocates a storage slot via similar.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.buffer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.residual!!","page":"Internals","text":"residual!!(y, prob, x, p)\n\nThe residual F(x, p) of prob at the iterate x, for the parameters p, in the storage of y, and return it.\n\ny is the storage slot the residual is to occupy, and it alone selects the call form of the problem's F: with no buffer slot (nothing) the call is F(x, p), for an SVector, an MVector, a Number and an Array alike, because the iterate's type is read nowhere; with an array buffer slot y the call is F(y, x, p). There is no inplace flag that could disagree with the iterate, and no hasmethod.\n\n\n\n\n\nresidual!!(F_out, prob::BatchEliminated, x, p; repeat_same_tol = false) -> F_out\n\nThe outer residual of the reduced-space elimination at the field x: solve the batch from the warm start prob.Y at the inner tolerance of this evaluation, deposit its moments, and write g_2(x). Every call records its (‖g₁‖, ‖g₂(prev)‖) in prob.g₁_history and prob.g₂_prev_history and its eliminated set in prob.eliminated_index_log, and leaves the batch at the root of the system it solved, which the next evaluation warm-starts from. A batch whose systems did not all converge, with a residual above 32 eps(R_in) √N_p, has no value to report: the residual it writes is NaN, so no outer step accepts it. With repeat_same_tol = true it re-uses the tolerance of the previous evaluation instead of the schedule of BatchEliminated, so a second evaluation at the same x stops every system at 0 iterations. The outer iterate's length must be the length of the problem's field: any other is a DimensionMismatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.residual!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.update_iterate!!","page":"Internals","text":"update_iterate!!(y, x, α, d)\n\nThe updated iterate x + α d, in the storage of y, and return it.\n\nThis is the one function that moves the iterate, and the one seam a manifold solver extends: a retraction is a second method of it, and no algorithm changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.update_iterate!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.map!!","page":"Internals","text":"map!!(f, y, xs...)\n\nmap(f, xs...) in the storage of y, and return it: the elementwise updates that are not an iterate update of update_iterate!! — a negation, a difference, a copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.map!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.mul!!","page":"Internals","text":"mul!!(C, A, B)\n\nThe matrix product A * B, in the storage of C, and return it: the Gram matrix of the Anderson and weighting histories, and a matrix–vector product where no jvp!! applies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.mul!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.copy!!","page":"Internals","text":"copy!!(dst, src)\n\nCopy src into the storage of dst, and return it. On a value slot (nothing, a StaticArray, or a Number) this returns src unchanged; on an array buffer slot it copies src into dst via copyto!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.copy!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.scale!!","page":"Internals","text":"scale!!(dst, α, x)\n\nThe scaled vector α * x, in the storage of dst, and return it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.scale!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.axpby!!","page":"Internals","text":"axpby!!(dst, α, x, β, y)\n\nThe linear combination α * x + β * y, in the storage of dst, and return it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.axpby!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.regularize!!","page":"Internals","text":"regularize!!(J, μ)\n\nAdd μ * I to the Jacobian J in its storage, and return it — on a rectangular J, to the min(M, N) entries of its leading diagonal. When iszero(μ) holds, J is returned untouched. On a Matrix the diagonal is shifted in a loop so bounds-checked runs allocate nothing; on any other AbstractMatrix the diagonal view view(J, diagind(J)) is updated by broadcast so device arrays perform no scalar indexing. An SMatrix has immutable entries, so its shifted diagonal is a rebuilt tuple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.regularize!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.init_state","page":"Internals","text":"init_state(method, prob::AbstractNonlinearProblem, x, p)\n\nBuild the state of method for AbstractNonlinearProblem prob at the initial iterate x and parameters p: its workspace, its AD preparation and its rule state, in one concrete type per method. In R1 (SVector, MVector or Number), init_state is allocation-free and returns an isbits state when prob and method are isbits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.init_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.residual_buffer","page":"Internals","text":"residual_buffer(state)\n\nThe slot that solve!! evaluates F(x₀) into: the residual workspace of state for an array iterate, and nothing for a value iterate. The default is nothing, which lets residual!! return a new value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.residual_buffer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.step!!","page":"Internals","text":"step!!(state, method, prob::AbstractNonlinearProblem, x, Fx, p)\n\nCompute one nonlinear solver step from (x, Fx) and return (x₊, F₊, state₊, info) where info is a StepInfo. A line-search step of Newton or Picard reads the anchor merit that the last start!! or step!! on its state set, so it takes only the (x, Fx) that they returned; on a state that no start!! saw, or that solve!! returned, it returns NONFINITE and keeps x and Fx.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.step!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.start!!","page":"Internals","text":"start!!(state, method, prob::AbstractNonlinearProblem, x, Fx, p, fnorm₀) -> state\n\nPrepare state for a solve from x, with residual Fx and its norm fnorm₀, and return the state that the first step reads. solve!! calls it once, before its stopping test at 0 iterations, so a state reused across solves starts each solve afresh. The default returns state. Newton resets its rule state, and Newton and Picard set the anchor merit norm2(Fx) of a line search, which init_state leaves at NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.start!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.finish!!","page":"Internals","text":"finish!!(state, method, prob::AbstractNonlinearProblem, x, Fx, status) -> (x, status, state)\n\nClose a solve that ends at x, with residual Fx and status, and return the triple that solve!! returns. solve!! calls it at every return. The default returns (x, status, state). A method may return another iterate; it then sets status.fnorm itself, and for an array iterate it writes that iterate into x and returns x. Newton and Picard set the anchor merit of a line search to NaN again, as init_state does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.finish!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.refine!!","page":"Internals","text":"refine!!(d, ref, fac, jvpprep, prob, x, Fx, fnorm, p, refbuf = nothing) -> (d, failed::Bool)\n\nRefine the initial linear direction d for J(x) d = -F(x) using the factor fac and the JVP preparation jvpprep. With NoRefinement, returns (d, false) and reads neither fnorm nor Fx. With Refinement or the LU-IR passes of GMRESRefinement, performs up to ref.maxiter defect-correction steps in working precision T and returns (d_best, failed), where failed is true if a step does not strictly reduce ‖F(x) + J(x) d‖ or if ref.maxiter iterations finish above max(ref.η, √eps(real(T))) * fnorm; the GMRES-IR stage of a GMRESRefinement follows a failed pass in the step, not here. fnorm is ‖F(x)‖ when the caller already holds it and nothing otherwise, in which case this method forms it from Fx.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.refine!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.sync_merit","page":"Internals","text":"sync_merit(state, method, merit::Real)\n\nUpdate a started line-search member state's stored baseline merit phi(x) = F(x)_2^2 to merit when another member has moved the iterate (x, Fx) since state's last step. Preserves NaN on an un-started state so calling step!! without start!! still fails closed with NONFINITE, and leaves the merit of a Static or DogLeg state (merit === nothing) as it is. A Newton state also sets the residual norm it carries to NaN, so that its next step measures F(x) of the residual it is given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.sync_merit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.init_globalization","page":"Internals","text":"init_globalization(g, x)\ninit_globalization(g, linsolve, x)\n\nAllocate the workspace named tuple for globalization method g at iterate x through buffer(x): (; xt, Ft) for Static, (; xt, Ft, Jv) for a LineSearch, (; g̃, ĝ, Jg, s, xt, Ft, u, b) for DogLeg, whose u and b share the storage of s and Jg for a square problem, and (; Jδ, xt, Ft, R₀, c₀, R, c, d_diag, w) for LevenbergMarquardt, whose stacked slots R₀ to w are nothing where the step reads the SVD factor and not the stacked solve. In R1 every slot is nothing; in R3 each slot is a preallocated array like x. The method with linsolve is the one Newton calls; it takes the linear method of the solve into account, and for any g but LevenbergMarquardt it is the method without it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.init_globalization"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.LineFunction","page":"Internals","text":"LineFunction(prob, x, Fx, d, p, jvpprep, xt = nothing, Ft = nothing, Jv = nothing)\n\nThe line function along the ray from x in the direction d for AbstractNonlinearProblem prob with parameters p, Jacobian-vector preparation jvpprep, and workspace slots xt, Ft and Jv; Fx is the residual at x. It is a callable struct, not a subtype of Function, so isbits holds when its fields are isbits.\n\nφ(lf, α) advances x along d by α through update_iterate!!, evaluates the residual through residual!!, and returns its merit norm2, F_2^2. φ′(lf, α) evaluates the derivative of the merit, 2mathrmRelangle F J drangle, through jvp!! and rdot. A line search along lf returns the residual at its step in its LineSearchResult, and Fx for a step it did not evaluate. Calling lf(α) delegates to φ(lf, α).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LineFunction"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.globalize!!","page":"Internals","text":"globalize!!(g, ls, prob, x, Fx, d, p, jvpprep, stepkind = MeasuredSlope())\n\nAdvance x along d under the globalization method ls using workspace g and return (x₊, F₊, g, info) where info is a StepInfo.\n\nFor Static, globalize!! takes the fixed step ls.α through update_iterate!! and evaluates residual!! once, without a line-search loop. When the step d or the new residual norm is not finite, it returns (x, Fx, g, StepInfo{R}(NONFINITE, rnorm(Fx), abs(ls.α) * rnorm(d), 0, false, false)) without advancing the iterate or evaluating F at a non-finite trial point.\n\nFor Backtracking, Bisection and StrongWolfe, globalize!! returns NONFINITE immediately when rnorm(d) is not finite; otherwise it builds a LineFunction and calls linesearch from α = 1. An accepted step returns the residual that the search evaluated at res.α, so it costs no further evaluation of F. A LINESEARCH_FAILED result whose φ is finite and strictly less than φ₀ = norm2(Fx) takes the step res.α and reports failures = 1; otherwise the iterate stays at x with stepnorm = 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.globalize!!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.LineSearch","page":"Internals","text":"LineSearch{R}\n\nThe supertype of the line searches Static, Backtracking, Bisection and StrongWolfe, whose numbers are of the real type R. A line search is a method: it is isbits, it holds numbers and, except Static, an Int32 cap and nothing else, and linesearch runs it.\n\nEvery line search keeps six contracts:\n\nIt never throws. A constructor checks its parameters on the host and raises an ArgumentError; linesearch raises nothing.\nIt returns α  0.\nIt reports through the code of its LineSearchResult and never logs.\nA non-finite or ascending anchor is reported, not searched: NONFINITE and LINESEARCH_FAILED. A stationary anchor, φ(0) = 0, is STALLED. Static is the exception: it reads no anchor and returns its step.\nIts number of evaluations does not depend on the scale of the merit.\nIt returns α  α_max, for the caller's αmax and for its own.\n\nIt never evaluates the merit at α = 0: the caller passes φ(0), which is the residual it already has.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LineSearch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.linesearch","page":"Internals","text":"linesearch(ls::LineSearch{R}, lf, step, φ₀::R, α::R, αmax::R = R(Inf))\n\nRun the line search ls along the line function lf from the trial step α, and return a LineSearchResult{R}. φ₀ is the merit at α = 0, which the caller has; step is an ExactStep, an InexactStep{R}, a MeasuredSlope or the slope φ(0)R itself; αmax is the caller's ceiling on the step. A trial step that is not positive or not finite is replaced by 1, and one below the step floor smallest_step is raised to it.\n\nA non-positive or NaN αmax is a caller error. The search then evaluates nothing and returns LINESEARCH_FAILED with the trial step, bounded by the ceiling of the method.\n\nThis is the one entry point: it applies the ceiling, the trial step and the anchor checks, then runs the loop of the method. Each loop runs while it has no result, for at most ls.maxiter trips. Static has no loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.linesearch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.φ","page":"Internals","text":"φ(lf, α)\n\nThe merit of the line function lf at the step α, φ(α) = r(x + α d)^2, in the real type of α. A line search sees a line function only through this and φ′, and through merit_and_residual and anchor_residual, whose fallbacks call φ and return nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.φ"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.φ′","page":"Internals","text":"φ′(lf, α)\n\nThe derivative dφdα of the merit of the line function lf at the step α: one Jacobian–vector product.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.φ′"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.ExactStep","page":"Internals","text":"ExactStep()\n\nThe step kind of a direction from a fresh Jacobian and an exact linear solve. For the merit φ = r^2 it gives φ(0) = -2φ(0), so no line search evaluates φ(0). For φ(0)  mathrmfloatmax(R)2 that slope overflows, and the search reports NONFINITE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ExactStep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.InexactStep","page":"Internals","text":"InexactStep(η)\n\nThe step kind of a direction d with the linear residual r + J d  η r, 0  η  1. It gives only φ(0)  -2(1 - η) φ(0) (Eisenstat and Walker 1996, (1.2)). Along α d the linear residual is (1 - α) r + α (r + J d), so its bound is η(α) = 1 - α + α η. Backtracking tests r(x + α d)  1 - c_1 (1 - η(α)) r with this η(α), which for α  1 is the update η  1 - θ (1 - η) of each backtrack by θ, and evaluates no φ. A step with η(α)  1 promises no decrease and is rejected. Bisection and StrongWolfe need the true slope and evaluate φ(0). The constructor checks nothing, so that solver code can build one in a kernel: a search given an η outside 0 1) evaluates nothing and returns LINESEARCH_FAILED. Static reads no step kind and returns its step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.InexactStep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.MeasuredSlope","page":"Internals","text":"MeasuredSlope()\n\nThe step kind of any other descent direction, such as one from a reused Jacobian: every line search except Static evaluates φ(0) once. A caller that already has φ(0) passes the number itself as the step kind, and no search evaluates it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.MeasuredSlope"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.LineSearchResult","page":"Internals","text":"LineSearchResult{R <: Real, Q}\n\nThe result of one linesearch. It is isbits when the residual is. LineSearchResult{R}(α, φ, code, evaluations) builds one with the residual nothing.\n\nFields\n\nα::R: the step, 0  α  α_max.\nφ::R: the merit at α, or NaN if the search did not evaluate it there.\ncode::ReturnCode: SUCCESS for an accepted step that decreases the merit by more than its round-off, and for the step of Static, which evaluates nothing; STALLED for a step that changes the merit by no more than its round-off, or a stationary anchor; NONFINITE for a non-finite anchor; LINESEARCH_FAILED for anything else: an ascending anchor, a spent cap, an increase of the merit, an invalid αmax, or a StrongWolfe step without the curvature condition. φ tells the caller whether the step of a failure still decreases the merit.\nevaluations::Int32: the evaluations of φ and φ′ together. A StrongWolfe search whose trials share one residual buffer evaluates F once more at a result step that is not its last trial, to restore residual; that evaluation is not counted.\nresidual::Q: the residual at α that gave φ; the residual at α = 0 where φ is NaN; nothing for a line function with no residual.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LineSearchResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.roundoff","page":"Internals","text":"roundoff(φ₀)\n\nThe round-off resolution τ = 4mathrmeps(R)φ(0) of the merit, four units of relative round-off. A step that changes the merit by no more than τ is at the round-off floor of the merit. It is proportional to φ(0), not a count of ulps of φ(0), so that it and the step floor smallest_step scale with the merit and do not jump by a factor 2 at a power of two. For a subnormal φ(0), where the product underflows, it is at least four of the smallest subnormals, so it is never 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.roundoff"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.smallest_step","page":"Internals","text":"smallest_step(d₀, τ)\n\nThe step floor τ  φ(0) of every search: below it the decrease that the slope predicts, α φ(0), is smaller than the round-off τ, so no trial can show it. It is at least floatmin(R), so that no search tries a step of 0 when the quotient underflows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.smallest_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.sufficient_decrease","page":"Internals","text":"sufficient_decrease(φα, φ₀, demand, τ)\n\nThe Armijo test on the difference, φ(α) - φ(0)  min(0 mathrmdemand + τ), where demand is the negative decrease the test demands and τ the round-off allowance. The difference keeps a demand below one ulp of φ(0), which φ(0) + mathrmdemand would round away; the min lets τ lower the demand, never accept an increase. StrongWolfe passes τ = 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.sufficient_decrease"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.classify","page":"Internals","text":"classify(φα, φ₀, τ)\n\nThe code of an accepted step: SUCCESS for a finite decrease by more than τ, STALLED for a change by no more than τ, and LINESEARCH_FAILED for an increase by more than τ or a merit that is not finite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.classify"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.backtrack_step","page":"Internals","text":"backtrack_step(φ₀, d₀, α, φα, αp, φp, p)\n\nThe next trial step after the rejected trial α with merit φα; αp and φp are the trial before it, and αp is NaN on the first backtrack. The model of φ is a quadratic through φ(0), φ(0) = d_0 and φ(α), then a cubic through φ(α_p) as well, both in the merit divided by d_0. The cubic minimiser is 1  (b + sqrtb^2 + 3a) for b  0, which does not cancel as a  0. The result is clamped to 01 α p α; a model without a minimiser gives p α, and a merit that is not finite at α gives 01 α.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.backtrack_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.zoom_step","page":"Internals","text":"zoom_step(a, φa, da, b, φb, db)\n\nThe minimiser of the cubic through the merit φa, φb and its derivative da, db at the two ends a and b of a bracket (Nocedal and Wright, (3.59)), clamped to the inner 80 % of the bracket. The discriminant is scaled by the largest of θ, d_a, d_b, as in Moré and Thuente, so it neither overflows nor depends on the scale of the merit. A cubic without a minimiser gives the middle of the bracket. Without a finite db it is the minimiser of the quadratic through φa, da and φb.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.zoom_step"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.LinearMethod","page":"Internals","text":"LinearMethod{TF}\n\nThe supertype of the linear-solver methods. TF is the factorisation precision: Nothing for the working type of the problem, a real float type, or a PrecisionMarker. It is a type parameter and not a field, because a type stored in a field is not isbits, and a method must enter a kernel.\n\nTF is a real type for a complex problem too: LUFactorization(Float32) factorises a ComplexF64 Jacobian in ComplexF32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.LinearMethod"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.compute_forcing","page":"Internals","text":"compute_forcing(forcing, k, fnorm, fnorm_prev, η_prev, linres_prev) -> η\n\nThe relative tolerance eta_k of the linear solve of Newton step k, in the real type of the forcing term's constants.\n\nk is the zero-based index of the step, so that k = 0 returns the initial tolerance eta_0 of the term. fnorm is F_k, fnorm_prev is F_k-1, η_prev is eta_k-1, and linres_prev is the linear residual F_k-1 + J_k-1 s_k-1 of the last step: the step that a line search accepted on an unrestarted inner solve, and the direction GMRES returned after a restarted one or a DogLeg step. Only EisenstatWalkerChoice1 reads it. A zero, non-finite or NaN fnorm_prev gives eta_max: the ratio of the rule is then undefined, and a residual that vanished ends the solve before the next step reads its tolerance, so the value is a guard and never a step's choice. A non-finite ratio, which a non-finite residual gives, gives eta_max as well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.compute_forcing"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.max_inner_iterations","page":"Internals","text":"max_inner_iterations(solver)\n\nThe largest count of GMRES inner iterations of any Newton step of the last solve of solver, a NonlinearSolver whose method is a Newton with GMRES. It is at most the maxiter of the linear method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.max_inner_iterations"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.GMRESWorkspace","page":"Internals","text":"GMRESWorkspace{T, R}\n\nThe workspace of one R5 solve: the Krylov basis V of min(restart, n) + 1 columns in the storage of the iterate, the host Hessenberg matrix H after the Givens rotations and H0 before them, the rotation tables cs and sn, the right-hand side g of the least-squares problem, the solution weights y, the weights cg, yN and yb of the matrix-free DogLeg and its reduced work vector sm, the vector slots rr, z, w, u, d and bb of length n, the jvp_eltype slots xT, vT and wT, the vector hd of min(restart, n) + 1 entries in the storage of the iterate, which holds the coefficients V_j^H w of an Arnoldi step and the weights y of a product V_j y, its host copy hh, the flag mgs of the path of the last solve, which reduced_direction! follows, and the counters of the solve.\n\nf_calls and jvp_calls count the residual evaluations and the Jacobian–vector products of the solve in Int32, gmres_iterations is the inner count of the last Newton step, max_inner the largest inner count of the solve, arnoldi the number of Arnoldi steps of the last inner solve, which the matrix-free DogLeg reads, and step the zero-based index of the current Newton step, which the forcing term reads. last_eta, prev_fnorm, linres and beta are the eta_k, F_k-1, linear residual and beta_0 of the last step, linres being that of the step a line search accepted on an unrestarted inner solve and that of the direction otherwise; start!! sets them for the solve and finish!! returns them to NaN, so they are NaN on a state that no start!! saw and on the state that solve!! returned, and a step there fails closed. The counters are mutable, so that a step reports them without rebuilding the workspace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.GMRESWorkspace"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.init_gmres_workspace","page":"Internals","text":"init_gmres_workspace(g::GMRES, x::AbstractVector)\n\nAllocate the GMRESWorkspace of a solve of g on an iterate like x: the Krylov basis holds min(g.restart, length(x)) + 1 columns, and the host matrices H and H0, the host vectors and the vector hd in the storage of x are of that width. The slots of jvp_eltype are nothing unless g.jvp_eltype names a type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.init_gmres_workspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.GMRESInfo","page":"Internals","text":"GMRESInfo\n\nWhat one gmres_solve! reports: the inner iterations it used, its residual norm b - J d_2, the beta_0 = b_2 it was given, whether it met the tolerance, and whether the Krylov space became invariant before the cap, a happy breakdown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.GMRESInfo"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.gmres_solve!","page":"Internals","text":"gmres_solve!(ws::GMRESWorkspace, jvp_op!, P, b, bnorm, tol, maxiter, restart;\n             mgs = false) -> (d, GMRESInfo)\n\nSolve J d = b with right-preconditioned GMRES in the workspace ws, and return the direction d = M⁻¹ u in the slot ws.d and a GMRESInfo. jvp_op!(Jv, v) writes the product J v into Jv; P is the right preconditioner; b is the right-hand side and bnorm its norm b_2, which the caller already holds; tol is the relative tolerance, so that the solve stops at b - J d_2 le tol cdot bnorm; maxiter caps the inner iterations and restart the iterations between restarts, neither of which may exceed the width the workspace was allocated for.\n\nThe Arnoldi process orthogonalises by classical Gram–Schmidt with a second pass (CGS2), as the mgs field of GMRES describes, and by modified Gram–Schmidt with mgs = true; both take the conjugated inner product, so a complex iterate is supported and every norm is in real(T). The solution update u mathrel+= V_j y of a cycle is one matrix–vector product on the CGS2 path and one update per column of V_j on the MGS path, which ws.mgs records for reduced_direction!. A zero H[j+1, j] is a breakdown: the Krylov space is invariant, and the solve stops there rather than dividing by zero. The residual of the reduced problem is then zero, unless J is singular on that space: the rotated diagonal entry is then zero too, the residual is that of the last entry of the right-hand side, and the solve reports no convergence. A restarted cycle forms its new residual with one more product, b - J M⁻¹ u, so a restart costs one Jacobian–vector product. The least-squares problem is solved from the Givens-rotated Hessenberg matrix, whose diagonal entry is 0 only where the reduced system is singular, and the corresponding weight is then 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.gmres_solve!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.gmres_ir!!","page":"Internals","text":"gmres_ir!!(d, ref, state, fac, J, Fx, fnorm) -> (d_ir, ok, ran, linrel, state)\n\nStage 2 of the mixed-precision remedy chain of a Newton step, GMRES-IR after the LU-IR passes of d have failed: a left-preconditioned GMRES in the working precision T on hatU^-1hatL^-1P J delta d = hatU^-1hatL^-1P r_mathrmlu, with the copy of the factors in TF that promote_lu_factors! writes into the working-precision factor slot, a cap of min(ref.gmres_maxiter, n) inner iterations, and the tolerance ref.gmres_tol on the true linear residual r_mathrmtrue = -F(x) - J (d + delta d) recomputed in T, which the stage accepts up to ten times its tolerance (Amestoy et al. 2024, p. 13; Kelley 2023, p. 7).\n\nd is the direction the LU-IR passes reached, fac the factorisation in TF they solved with, J the regularised Jacobian the step holds in T, and fnorm the norm F(x) the tolerance is relative to. It returns the direction (the GMRES-IR one where ok, else d), whether the stage met its bound, whether it ran at all (it does not where the solve keeps no working-precision factor slot and no Krylov basis, where the factor is no LU factorisation, and for any refinement that is not a GMRESRefinement), the relative true residual of the direction it returned, and the state that carries the promoted factor slot it wrote.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.gmres_ir!!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.promote_lu_factors!","page":"Internals","text":"promote_lu_factors!(fac_promoted, fac_TF)\n\nCopy the LU factors of fac_TF into fac_promoted, whose element type is the working precision T, and return the promoted factor. L, U and ipiv travel as they are, together with the info and the scale_exponent of the factorisation, so that a solve with fac_promoted gives the step of the factorisation in TF and never factorises the Jacobian again: the copy is what a GMRES-IR stage applies to its Krylov vectors, in T (Amestoy et al. 2024, p. 13). Both factors are dense or device factors of one shape, and the copy allocates nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.promote_lu_factors!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.arnoldi_dot","page":"Internals","text":"arnoldi_dot(a, b)\n\nThe conjugated inner product sum_i overlinea_i b_i of two vectors, in the element type of b: the coefficient of modified Gram–Schmidt, which is real for a real iterate and complex for a complex one. It reduces through a lazy broadcast, so it allocates nothing and indexes no entry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.arnoldi_dot"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.R5Operator","page":"Internals","text":"R5Operator(prep, prob, x, p)\n\nThe Jacobian–vector product of one gmres_solve! at the Newton iterate x: the callable (Jv, v) -> jvp!!(Jv, prep, prob, x, v, p), whose fields are the preparation, the problem, the iterate and the parameters. It is a struct and not a closure, so that a step allocates none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.R5Operator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.R5JVP","page":"Internals","text":"R5JVP(prep, ws)\nR5JVP{P, W, TJ}(prep, ws)\n\nThe Jacobian–vector preparation prep of an R5 solve, which counts its products in the workspace ws and, where TJ names an element type, evaluates the product in TJ and promotes it to the working type of the iterate: the iterate, the direction and the product are converted to TJ, the product is formed there, and its result is copied back. That is a defect-correction solve, where the residual in the working precision keeps the accuracy of the outer solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.R5JVP"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.CountingResidual","page":"Internals","text":"CountingResidual(F, ws)\n\nThe residual F of an R5 solve with the workspace ws, whose f_calls it increments: every evaluation of the residual of the solve goes through it, the trial evaluations of a globalization included, so that the count of the status is the count of the solve. Both call forms of residual!! are methods of it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.CountingResidual"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.sat16","page":"Internals","text":"sat16(n)\n\nThe saturated count that a call count occupies in a SolverStatus: n clipped to 0:typemax(Int16), so that a long solve reports its count without overflowing the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.sat16"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.with_calls","page":"Internals","text":"with_calls(status::SolverStatus, f_calls, jvp_calls)\n\nstatus with its residual and Jacobian–vector call counts replaced by f_calls and jvp_calls, saturated to the Int16 of their fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.with_calls"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.jacobi_rotation","page":"Internals","text":"jacobi_rotation(α, β, γ)\n\nThe rotation (cs, sn) that makes the Hermitian 2 × 2 matrix [α γ; γ̄ β] diagonal, with real α and β: with G = [cs sn; -conj(sn) cs], the product Gᴴ [α γ; γ̄ β] G is diagonal, and θ with tan θ = t is at most π/4 in modulus, which is what makes the sweep converge rather than merely rearrange.\n\nγ must not be zero: the phase of the rotation is γ / |γ|.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.jacobi_rotation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.ToReal","page":"Internals","text":"ToReal{R}()\n\nThe Adapt rule that converts every AbstractFloat in a method tree to R. Applied on the host with R = real(eltype(x)), it lets a method written with Float64 literals run in a Float32 kernel that never sees a Float64. An integer, a type and a function pass through unchanged.\n\nEach method type that holds a float adds Adapt.@adapt_structure for itself.\n\njulia> GeometricSolvers.Adapt.adapt(GeometricSolvers.ToReal{Float32}(), (0.7, Int32(3), sin))\n(0.7f0, 3, sin)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.ToReal"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.StripDual","page":"Internals","text":"StripDual()\n\nThe Adapt rule that strips every ForwardDiff.Dual and Complex{<:ForwardDiff.Dual} leaf to its primal value: a ForwardDiff.Dual leaf via ForwardDiff.value, a Complex{<:ForwardDiff.Dual} leaf in both real and imaginary parts, an array of duals elementwise, and a Tuple, NamedTuple or struct with Adapt.@adapt_structure recursively. Any other value passes through unchanged.\n\njulia> d = GeometricSolvers.ForwardDiff.Dual{Nothing}(3.5, 1.0);\n\njulia> GeometricSolvers.Adapt.adapt(GeometricSolvers.StripDual(), (d, Complex(d, d)))\n(3.5, 3.5 + 3.5im)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.StripDual"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.parameters","page":"Internals","text":"parameters(p, i::Integer)\n\nReturn the parameter value for system i: p.data[i] when p isa PerSystem, and p unchanged otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\nparameters(p::BatchParams, i::Integer)\n\nThe parameter value of system i of a BatchParams: the pair (p.shared, p.per_system[i]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.record","page":"Internals","text":"record(status::SolverStatus{R}, info::StepInfo{R})\n\nFold the result of one step into the status: count the iteration, take the code and the norms of the step, add its failures, keep promoted once it is set, and preserve stationary, rank, nullnorm and gradratio.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.record"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.converged","page":"Internals","text":"converged(options::Options{R}, status::SolverStatus{R}, fnorm₀::R, xnorm::R)\n\nThe stopping test of Options at status, for a start with residual norm fnorm₀ and an iterate of norm xnorm. It holds once min_iterations steps are taken and either the residual test or, after a step of nonzero norm, the step test holds. At 0 iterations it is the test before the first step: the relative residual test then holds only for F(x₀) = 0, so a start passes it through f_abstol. A zero step is no progress, so it never passes the step test.\n\nsolve!! returns SUCCESS only through the residual test: a step test that holds while the residual test fails returns STALLED.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.converged"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.isconverged","page":"Internals","text":"isconverged(status::SolverStatus)\nisconverged(counts::NamedTuple{(:SUCCESS, :STALLED, :MAXITERS, :SINGULAR, :NONFINITE, :LINESEARCH_FAILED)})\n\nWhether the solve ended with SUCCESS, or whether every system in a batch return-code count summary ended with SUCCESS (counts.SUCCESS == sum(counts)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.isconverged"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.isstalled","page":"Internals","text":"isstalled(status::SolverStatus)\n\nWhether the solve ended with STALLED.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.isstalled"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.norm2","page":"Internals","text":"norm2(a)\n\nThe squared Euclidean norm sum_i a_i^2 in real(eltype(a)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.norm2"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.rnorm","page":"Internals","text":"rnorm(a)\n\nThe Euclidean norm sqrtsum_i a_i^2 in real(eltype(a)).\n\nWhere norm2(a) is finite and at least length(a) * floatmin(R), it is sqrt(norm2(a)), one reduction. Otherwise it scales the entries by 2^-e, where 2^e is the power of two of the largest a_i (of the largest real or imaginary part for a complex a), so that it neither overflows where the norm is finite nor underflows to zero where the norm is positive. That path takes two more reductions, the largest entry and the sum, and sums in at least Float32. Scaling can round the squares of entries much smaller than the largest to zero, which changes the result in the last bits at most. A NaN entry gives NaN, an Inf entry and no NaN gives Inf. Where real(eltype(a)) is no AbstractFloat, such as an integer or a ForwardDiff.Dual, it is the unscaled sqrt(norm2(a)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.rnorm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.rdot","page":"Internals","text":"rdot(a, b)\n\nThe real part of the inner product, mathrmRe sum_i bara_i b_i, in real(promote_type(eltype(a), eltype(b))). That is the dot product of the real vector space under a complex T, which a merit derivative needs. Arguments with different axes raise a DimensionMismatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.rdot"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.BatchEliminatedJVPPrep","page":"Internals","text":"BatchEliminatedJVPPrep(xa, Fa, xp, Fp, ε, tol)\n\nThe Jacobian–vector preparation of a BatchEliminated under a matrix-free outer Newton: the anchor-iterate and perturbed-iterate slots xa and xp, their residual slots Fa and Fp, the relative step ε = \\sqrt{\\operatorname{eps}(R_{\\text{eff}})} with R_eff the coarser of the batch's and the outer iterate's element type, and the inner tolerance tol of both evaluations. The product is the one-sided difference (F(x + h v) - F(x))/h at the step h = ε max(1, ‖x‖)/‖v‖.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.BatchEliminatedJVPPrep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.PIC1DParams","page":"Internals","text":"PIC1DParams(x, v, E)\n\nThe per-particle data of one Crank–Nicolson orbit step of the one-dimensional implicit electrostatic PIC problem: the position x, the velocity v, and the field E gathered at x, all of the previous time level and in the batch's element type. It is isbits, so a batch kernel takes it by value, and make_pic_elimination_problem builds one per particle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.PIC1DParams"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.pic_gather","page":"Internals","text":"pic_gather(E, ξ, Δx, n_cells)\n\nThe field of the periodic grid E gathered at ξ with the quadratic B-spline weights _pic_weights, and its derivative with respect to ξ, as the pair (E(ξ), dE/dξ). The shape function is C¹, so the derivative is continuous across a cell boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.pic_gather"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.pic_inner_residual","page":"Internals","text":"pic_inner_residual(y, c, Δt, Δx, n_cells)\n\nThe residual of one particle's Crank–Nicolson orbit step of the one-dimensional implicit electrostatic PIC problem, at the new position and velocity y = (x^{n+1}, v^{n+1}):\n\ng_1(y) = beginpmatrix x^n+1 - x^n - tfracDelta t2(v^n + v^n+1) \nv^n+1 - v^n - tfracDelta t2(E(x^n) + E(x^n+1)) endpmatrix\n\nwith E(x^{n+1}) gathered from the field the batch shares and E(x^n) the value that the per-system data PIC1DParams holds. c = (E, ps) is the pair that BatchParams passes to system i: the shared field E and that system's PIC1DParams ps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.pic_inner_residual"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.pic_inner_jacobian","page":"Internals","text":"pic_inner_jacobian(y, c, Δt, Δx, n_cells)\n\nThe 2 × 2 derivative of pic_inner_residual with respect to y = (x^{n+1}, v^{n+1}),\n\nfracpartial g_1partial y = beginpmatrix 1  -tfracDelta t2 \n-tfracDelta t2 E(x^n+1)  1 endpmatrix\n\nwith E′(x^{n+1}) the gathered field's derivative. It is analytic, so the inner solve takes no derivative of the gather, whose shape function is piecewise polynomial.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.pic_inner_jacobian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.deposit_pic_moments!","page":"Internals","text":"deposit_pic_moments!(moments, Y, shared, per_system, Δt, Δx, n_cells)\n\nScatter the moments of the batch Y onto the grid of moments in the canonical order of the batch, and accumulate every sum in the batch's element type typeof(Δt): the number density rho_j, the current J_j, the momentum flux F_j, and the temperature T_j = F_jrho_j - (J_jrho_j)^2 where rho_j  0. The order and the precision are fixed by the loop and by the accumulators, so one batch state deposits one set of moments bit for bit, however often it is deposited.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.deposit_pic_moments!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.pic_outer_residual!","page":"Internals","text":"pic_outer_residual!(F_out, E, moments, E_previous, Δt)\n\nThe Ampère residual of one field update of the one-dimensional implicit electrostatic PIC problem, at the new field E,\n\ng_2(E) = fracE - E^nDelta t + J(E)\n\nwith J(E) the current that deposit_pic_moments! deposited from the particles advanced under E, and E^n the previous field. Both terms and their sum are evaluated in the batch's element type typeof(Δt), and the value is converted to the element type of F_out: the current is known to the batch's precision only, so a residual summed in the outer precision would report a distance to the root that the batch cannot resolve. That floor of F is a property of F, not of the outer factorisation, so on the problem of make_pic_elimination_problem the outer solve at a tolerance below it ends on a stall and never on a factorisation failure (§1.4).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.pic_outer_residual!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/internals/#GeometricSolvers.counting_problem","page":"Internals","text":"counting_problem(prob::BatchEliminated, ws)\n\nThe problem a globalization of an R5 outer step reads: prob with its residual counted in the workspace ws, so that the trial evaluations of a line search add to the f_calls of the status. A Jacobian–vector product is not an evaluation of F, and jvp!! of this problem is not counted here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.counting_problem"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/nonlinear/#nonlinear","page":"Nonlinear methods","text":"A nonlinear method holds its configuration and nothing about the problem or the iterate. It enters the loop of solve!! through init_state, start!!, step!! and finish!!, so one method object serves each regime that its state supports, and the combinators can compose any of them.","title":"Nonlinear methods"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/nonlinear/#Newton","page":"Nonlinear methods","text":"Newton solves J(x) d = -F(x) at each step and updates the iterate along d with its globalization. Its keywords are:\n\nkeyword default meaning\nlinsolve LUFactorization() the linear solver: a factorisation or GMRES\nglobalization Static() the globalization: a line search, DogLeg or LevenbergMarquardt\njacobian nothing the Jacobian source: nothing (the problem's J, else AutoForwardDiff()), an ADTypes back end, a callable (J(x, p) on a value iterate, J!(J, x, p) on an array) except with GMRES, or a JVPFunction for GMRES\nrefinement NoRefinement() iterative refinement of the direction: Refinement or GMRESRefinement, see Mixed precision\nregularization 0 the diagonal shift mu of J + mu I\nreuse nothing the factor-reuse period k\nforcing, preconditioner nothing the forcing term and the right preconditioner of a GMRES\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers\n\njulia> F!(y, x, p) = (y[1] = x[1]^2 + x[2]^2 - p; y[2] = x[1] - x[2]; y);\n\njulia> prob = NonlinearProblem(F!);\n\njulia> method = Newton(; linsolve = QRFactorization(), globalization = Backtracking());\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, [1.0, 0.5], method, 2.0);\n\njulia> x ≈ [1.0, 1.0]\ntrue","title":"Newton"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/nonlinear/#Factor-reuse","page":"Nonlinear methods","text":"With reuse = k, a Newton step factorises the Jacobian and reuses the factor for up to k consecutive steps. It refactorises early after a STALLED step and after a failed refinement on a reused factor. QuasiNewton(k) is Newton(; reuse = k). A reused factor saves the evaluation and the factorisation of the Jacobian at the price of a linear rate, so it pays where those dominate the cost of a step.\n\njulia> x, status = solve(prob, [1.0, 0.5], QuasiNewton(3), 2.0);\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && x ≈ [1.0, 1.0]\ntrue\n\nDogLeg and LevenbergMarquardt take no reuse, because their model needs the Jacobian at the current iterate.","title":"Factor reuse"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/nonlinear/#Regularisation","page":"Nonlinear methods","text":"regularization = μ factorises J + mu I instead of J. With an SVDFactorization a singular Jacobian gives the truncated minimum-norm step instead, and SolverStatus reports the numerical rank in rank.","title":"Regularisation"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/nonlinear/#GaussNewton","page":"Nonlinear methods","text":"GaussNewton is a Newton with linsolve = QRFactorization() and Static(), for a LeastSquaresProblem. SVDFactorization serves a rank-deficient Jacobian. For a damped step, use Newton(; globalization = LevenbergMarquardt()), which takes any of the four factorisations. See Problem types for an example.","title":"GaussNewton"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/nonlinear/#Picard","page":"Nonlinear methods","text":"Picard is the damped fixed-point iteration x_k+1 = x_k - omega F(x_k). It needs no Jacobian, and with F(x) = x - G(x) it is the fixed-point iteration of G. The step converges where I - omega J is a contraction, and its rate is linear.\n\njulia> fp = NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x - cos(x));\n\njulia> x, status = solve(fp, 1.0, Picard(; damping = 0.8));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && isapprox(x, 0.7390851332; atol = 1e-8)\ntrue\n\nA line search (Backtracking, Bisection or StrongWolfe) along the Picard direction uses a measured slope. Anderson and Broyden accelerate a Picard iteration; see Composition and preconditioning.","title":"Picard"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/nonlinear/#Implicit-differentiation","page":"Nonlinear methods","text":"solve differentiates through a nonlinear solve. When x₀ or p carries a ForwardDiff.Dual, solve strips the duals, runs the primal solve to x^*, and on SUCCESS solves the tangent equation of the implicit function theorem,\n\nJ(x^* p) dot x = -fracpartial Fpartial p(x^* p)  dot p\n\nfrom one factorisation of J(x^* p). The cost of the derivative is therefore one factorisation and one back substitution per partial, independent of the number of iterations of the primal solve.\n\njulia> using ForwardDiff\n\njulia> sq = NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x^2 - p);\n\njulia> dxdp = ForwardDiff.derivative(p -> solve(sq, 1.0, Newton(), p)[1], 2.0);\n\njulia> dxdp ≈ 1 / (2 * sqrt(2.0))\ntrue\n\nA non-SUCCESS primal solve returns NaN partials. The accuracy of the tangent is that of the factor element type of linsolve. init and solve! refuse dual inputs. A StationarityProblem, a LeastSquaresProblem, a matrix-free GMRES solve, the combinators and the preconditioning methods have no implicit differentiation, and solve raises an ArgumentError for a dual input there. A Deflated problem differentiates the undeflated problem at the root it found.","title":"Implicit differentiation"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/nonlinear/#Methods-on-the-other-pages","page":"Nonlinear methods","text":"Globalization: Static, Backtracking, Bisection, StrongWolfe, DogLeg, LevenbergMarquardt.\nLinear solvers: the factorisations, GMRES and the sparse LU.\nComposition and preconditioning: Additive, Multiplicative, Repeat, Switch, Anderson, Broyden, RAS, LeftPreconditioned.\nProblem types: least squares, stationarity, deflation, elimination and reparametrization.","title":"Methods on the other pages"},{"category":"section","location":"#GeometricSolvers","page":"Home","text":"Documentation for GeometricSolvers.\n\nGeometricSolvers provides line searches, linear solvers and nonlinear solvers that run on the CPU and on CUDA, ROCm and Metal devices through KernelAbstractions. One solver loop serves three regimes, which the storage of the initial iterate selects:\n\nR1: a Number or an SVector, with value semantics, on the host or in one GPU thread,\nR2: a batch of small systems, one work-item per system on the CPU or a GPU, through solve_batch!,\nR3: an in-place AbstractVector, on the CPU or on a device array.\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers\n\njulia> x, status = solve(NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x^2 - p), 1.0, Newton(), 2.0);\n\njulia> x ≈ sqrt(2) && status.code === SUCCESS\ntrue","title":"GeometricSolvers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contents-of-this-documentation","page":"Home","text":"Manual\n\nGetting started: problems, call forms, solve, init and solve!, options, status and return codes, parameters, and the SciML extension.\nThe three regimes: R1, R2 and R3, and how to choose one.\nMethods by regime: the methods that run in R1, R2, R3 and the matrix-free R5, and the conditions of each.\nProblem types: least squares, stationarity, reparametrization, deflation and elimination.\n\nAlgorithms\n\nNonlinear methods: Newton, GaussNewton, Picard, factor reuse and implicit differentiation.\nGlobalization: line searches, the dogleg trust region and Levenberg–Marquardt.\nLinear solvers: static, dense, device and sparse factorisations, and the matrix-free GMRES.\nAutomatic differentiation: ForwardDiff, DifferentiationInterface and sparse Jacobians.\nComposition and preconditioning: combinators, Anderson and Broyden acceleration, restricted additive Schwarz and RASPEN.\n\nArchitecture\n\nCPU and GPU backends: the storage and reduction seams, dispatch, kernel-safe types, vendor libraries and device precision.\nMixed precision: the factorisation precision, the scaled solve and iterative refinement.\nNumerical Stability and Backward-Error Bounds: the bounds of each factorisation that the test suite verifies.\n\nThe API section documents every name, starting with Problems and solves.\n\n","title":"Contents of this documentation"},{"category":"module","location":"#GeometricSolvers.GeometricSolvers","page":"Home","text":"Solvers with the problem/method/solver interface of SimpleSolvers, built so that the same solvers run on the CPU and on CUDA, ROCm and Metal devices through KernelAbstractions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeometricSolvers.GeometricSolvers"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/ad/#ad","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"A Newton step needs the Jacobian, and a line search or a refinement needs Jacobian–vector products. The jacobian field of Newton selects their source:\n\njacobian source\nnothing (default) the problem's own J when it has one, else AutoForwardDiff(); with a GMRES solve, always AutoForwardDiff()\nAutoForwardDiff() ForwardDiff, in the forms below\nanother ADTypes back end DifferentiationInterface, on a host Array iterate\nAutoSparse(...) DifferentiationInterface with a sparsity pattern and a colouring\na callable, J(x, p) on a value iterate and J!(J, x, p) on an array the caller's Jacobian operator; a GMRES solve refuses it with an ArgumentError\nJVPFunction(jvp!) the caller's product, for a GMRES solve\n\nprepare_ad builds the preparation once, at init, from the back end and the storage of the iterate. jacobian!!, jvp!! and gradient_hessian!! then dispatch on the object it returns, so the solver makes no choice at run time.","title":"Automatic differentiation"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/ad/#ForwardDiff-on-a-static-iterate","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"In R1 the preparation of AutoForwardDiff() is the back end itself. A Jacobian is one tagged ForwardDiff evaluation of F on an SVector of duals, and a Jacobian–vector product is one evaluation with a single partial. Everything stays isbits, so it runs in a kernel. A back end other than AutoForwardDiff() has no R1 method.","title":"ForwardDiff on a static iterate"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/ad/#Chunked-ForwardDiff-on-arrays","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"In R3, AutoForwardDiff() uses the package's own chunked forward mode, ChunkedForwardDiff: N Jacobian columns per residual evaluation, in preallocated dual buffers. It indexes no scalar, so it runs on a host Array and on a device array alike. For a StationarityProblem, ChunkedHessianForwardDiff takes nested duals for the gradient and the Hessian block of the action.","title":"Chunked ForwardDiff on arrays"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/ad/#DifferentiationInterface","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"Any other ADTypes back end goes through DifferentiationInterface on a host Array (DIJacobian, and DIStationarity for a stationarity problem). A back end is named with ADTypes, and an explicit AutoForwardDiff() takes the chunked path above:\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers, ADTypes\n\njulia> F!(y, x, p) = (y .= x .^ 2 .- p; y);\n\njulia> x, status = solve(NonlinearProblem(F!), ones(3), Newton(; jacobian = AutoForwardDiff()), [1.0, 4.0, 9.0]);\n\njulia> x ≈ [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]\ntrue\n\nAnother back end goes through DifferentiationInterface, and its package must be loaded:\n\nusing FiniteDiff\nsolve(NonlinearProblem(F!), ones(3), Newton(; jacobian = AutoFiniteDiff()), [1.0, 4.0, 9.0])\n\nOn a device array only AutoForwardDiff() applies; another back end raises an ArgumentError in prepare_ad, because DifferentiationInterface indexes scalars there. AutoEnzyme needs runtime activity for the parameter context, as the section Automatic differentiation of the API explains.","title":"DifferentiationInterface"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/ad/#Jacobian–vector-products","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"prepare_jvp builds the product preparation. With an analytic three-argument J!(J, x, p) on the problem, JacobianMatrixJVP evaluates J! into a buffer of its own and multiplies. A product is prepared only where a step reads one: a line search other than Static, a Refinement along a reused factor, and every GMRES step. A GMRES step does not read J!: with jacobian = nothing its product comes from AutoForwardDiff(), so its residual must accept dual numbers.","title":"Jacobian–vector products"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/ad/#Sparse-Jacobians","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"For a sparse R3 Jacobian on a host Array, an AutoSparse back end takes either the jacobian_prototype of the NonlinearProblem as its pattern, or, when SparseMatrixColorings is loaded and the problem has no prototype, a sparsity detector together with a colouring algorithm:\n\n# Not run here: needs SparseConnectivityTracer and SparseMatrixColorings.\nusing GeometricSolvers, ADTypes, Sparspak\nusing SparseConnectivityTracer: TracerSparsityDetector\nusing SparseMatrixColorings: GreedyColoringAlgorithm\n\nad = AutoSparse(AutoForwardDiff();\n    sparsity_detector = TracerSparsityDetector(),\n    coloring_algorithm = GreedyColoringAlgorithm())\n\nsolve(NonlinearProblem(F!), ones(3), Newton(; jacobian = ad), [1.0, 4.0, 9.0])\n\nThe extension GeometricSolversSparseADExt then reads the detected pattern through jacobian_pattern. With Sparspak loaded, a detected pattern gives the sparse LU (see Sparse direct solves) only for an LUFactorization whose factor element type is that of a real Vector iterate; any other method keeps the dense factor (see has_sparse_factor). A LevenbergMarquardt step, and a nonzero regularization on a detected pattern without the whole diagonal, keep the dense factor too. An explicit prototype takes precedence over a detected pattern.","title":"Sparse Jacobians"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/ad/#The-parameters","page":"Automatic differentiation","text":"The parameters are a context of the preparation and not a differentiated argument. Their values, and the parameter object itself, may change on every call, so one preparation serves a whole time-stepping loop. Differentiation with respect to the parameters is the implicit differentiation of solve.","title":"The parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/problems/#problems","page":"Problem types","text":"Every problem subtypes AbstractNonlinearProblem. The three base problems hold functions only. The wrappers take a base problem and change the system that the solver sees.\n\nproblem solves methods\nNonlinearProblem F(x p) = 0, square every method\nLeastSquaresProblem min_x tfrac12 F(x p)_2^2, m ge n residuals GaussNewton, Newton with QR, SVD or LevenbergMarquardt\nStationarityProblem (nabla S(x p))_E + b = 0 Newton, Picard, the combinators\nReparametrized the inner problem in chart coordinates x = g(y p) as the inner problem\nDeflated the inner problem with known roots removed as the inner problem\nBlockEliminated the outer equations after the inner unknowns are eliminated Newton\nVariableProjection a separable least-squares problem by variable projection Newton, GaussNewton\nBatchEliminated an outer system whose residual solves a batch of inner systems a matrix-free Newton","title":"Problem types"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/problems/#NonlinearProblem","page":"Problem types","text":"NonlinearProblem(F) and NonlinearProblem(F, J) hold the residual and, optionally, its Jacobian. The call forms are those of Getting started. Without J, the Jacobian comes from the jacobian field of Newton, whose default nothing takes AutoForwardDiff().\n\nThree keywords add structure:\n\njacobian_prototype: a matrix that gives the storage type and the sparsity pattern of the R3 Jacobian. A SparseMatrixCSC prototype on a host Vector, with Sparspak loaded, gives a sparse LU factorisation (see Sparse direct solves).\nblock_residuals: the local residuals F_i!(f_i, x_i, x, p) of a BlockPartition, which the restricted additive Schwarz method RAS reads (see Composition and preconditioning).\nblock_jacobians: optionally, with block_residuals, the block Jacobians J_i!(J_i, x_i, x, p), one per block, which fill the Jacobian of block i.","title":"NonlinearProblem"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/problems/#LeastSquaresProblem","page":"Problem types","text":"A LeastSquaresProblem has a residual of length m ge n. An R1 problem infers m from the residual; an in-place problem needs output_length = m. GaussNewton is a Newton with QRFactorization(). The solve converges by the residual test, or, at a nonzero-residual minimum, by the stationarity test J^mathsfH F  (J_F F) le g_tol, which sets status.stationary:\n\njulia> using GeometricSolvers, StaticArrays\n\njulia> t = SVector(0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0); y = SVector(1.0, 2.7, 7.4, 20.1);\n\njulia> r(x, p) = x[1] .* exp.(x[2] .* p[1]) .- p[2];\n\njulia> x, status = solve(LeastSquaresProblem(r), SVector(1.0, 0.9), GaussNewton(), (t, y));\n\njulia> status.code === SUCCESS && status.stationary\ntrue\n\njulia> isapprox(x, SVector(1.0, 1.0); atol = 1e-2)\ntrue\n\nLevenbergMarquardt is the damped globalization of the same step; see Globalization.","title":"LeastSquaresProblem"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/problems/#StationarityProblem","page":"Problem types","text":"A StationarityProblem takes a scalar action S(x, p) and solves the stationarity equations (nabla S)_E + b = 0 for the unknowns x[U]. The residual is the gradient and the Jacobian is a block of the Hessian, both by automatic differentiation. This is the form of a discrete variational principle, whose stationary points are often saddle points: the merit of a line search is F_2^2 and never S itself.\n\njulia> S(x, p) = (x[1]^2 + x[2]^2) / 2 + x[1]^4 / 4 - p * x[1] + x[1] * x[2];\n\njulia> x, status = solve(StationarityProblem(S), SVector(0.5, 0.5), Newton(), 1.0);\n\njulia> x ≈ SVector(1.0, -1.0)\ntrue\n\nWith E == U the Jacobian is symmetric, so LDLTFactorization applies. The keyword b of solve and init sets the datum for one solve, and E and U select a subset of the equations and of the unknowns.","title":"StationarityProblem"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/problems/#Reparametrized","page":"Problem types","text":"Reparametrized(prob, g) iterates in chart coordinates y with x = g(y p). The solve returns x^* = g(y^* p), the solution in the original coordinates. The chart Jacobian is ∂g when given and forward-mode AD otherwise. A chart keeps the iterate on a constraint set, here the positive half-line:\n\njulia> inner = NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x - 2);\n\njulia> x, status = solve(Reparametrized(inner, (y, p) -> exp(y)), 0.0, Newton());\n\njulia> x ≈ 2.0\ntrue\n\nFor a StationarityProblem the residual is pulled back covariantly, tilde F = G^mathsfT F(g(y)), with G = partial g  partial y, and the keyword curvature selects whether the Jacobian includes the curvature term of the chart.","title":"Reparametrized"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/problems/#Deflated","page":"Problem types","text":"Deflated(prob, roots) multiplies the residual by the deflation factor M(x) = prod_i (x - r_i^-p + sigma) of Farrell, Birkisson and Funke (2015), so the known roots repel the solve, and a solve from the same start can converge to another root. Deflation does not guarantee that another root exists or that the solve converges to it, so read the status. The stopping test reads the undeflated residual.\n\njulia> cubic = NonlinearProblem((x, p) -> x^3 - x);\n\njulia> solve(cubic, 0.6, Newton())[1] ≈ 1\ntrue\n\njulia> abs(solve(Deflated(cubic, (1.0,)), 0.6, Newton())[1]) < 1e-8\ntrue\n\npower and shift set p and sigma. A Deflated problem with isbits roots also runs in a batch: every system of the batch is deflated at the same roots.","title":"Deflated"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/problems/#BlockEliminated-and-VariableProjection","page":"Problem types","text":"BlockEliminated(prob, inner) splits the unknowns into inner unknowns y = x[inner] and outer unknowns z. At each outer iterate it solves the inner equations for y^*(z), and the outer Newton steps on the remaining equations with the Schur complement J_zz - J_zy J_yy^+ J_yz. With linear_inner = true the inner solve is one linear solve, for inner equations affine in y.\n\njulia> F(x, p) = SVector(x[1] - x[2]^2, x[1] + x[2] - p);\n\njulia> x, status = solve(BlockEliminated(NonlinearProblem(F), (1,)), SVector(0.0, 0.5), Newton(), 2.0);\n\njulia> x ≈ SVector(1.0, 1.0)\ntrue\n\nVariableProjection does the same for a LeastSquaresProblem whose residual is linear (or easy) in the inner parameters: it minimises over them for each outer iterate, and the outer solver steps on the reduced residual with Kaufman's reduced Jacobian (jacobian_mode = :kaufman, Kaufman 1975) or the full Golub–Pereyra Jacobian (jacobian_mode = :golub_pereyra, Golub and Pereyra 1973). BlockEliminated on a LeastSquaresProblem forwards to VariableProjection.\n\nBatchEliminated eliminates a whole batch of independent inner systems inside the residual of an outer matrix-free Newton: every evaluation of the outer residual runs solve_batch! on the inner systems and deposits their moments. The section Reduced-space elimination with a batch inside the residual of the API describes it.","title":"BlockEliminated and VariableProjection"},{"category":"section","location":"manual/problems/#References","page":"Problem types","text":"P. E. Farrell, Á. Birkisson and S. W. Funke, Deflation Techniques for Finding Distinct Solutions of Nonlinear Partial Differential Equations, SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 37(4), A2026–A2045 (2015). doi:10.1137/140984798\nG. H. Golub and V. Pereyra, The Differentiation of Pseudo-Inverses and Nonlinear Least Squares Problems Whose Variables Separate, SIAM Journal on Numerical Analysis 10(2), 413–432 (1973). doi:10.1137/0710036\nL. Kaufman, A variable projection method for solving separable nonlinear least squares problems, BIT 15(1), 49–57 (1975). doi:10.1007/BF01932995","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/backends/#backends","page":"CPU and GPU backends","text":"GeometricSolvers runs the same solvers on the CPU and on CUDA, ROCm and Metal devices. This page describes how the code is built for that: the four kinds of object, the storage seam, the reduction seam, dispatch in place of checks, and the vendor libraries that replace some of the own kernels.","title":"CPU and GPU backends"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/backends/#Four-kinds-of-object","page":"CPU and GPU backends","text":"object holds example\nproblem functions NonlinearProblem\nmethod numbers, types and sub-methods, and no buffer of the solve Newton, LUFactorization, Backtracking\nstate everything that is allocated or that changes during a solve the result of init_state\nstatus the result SolverStatus\n\nA problem holds no parameters and no iterate, and a method holds no buffer of the solve. When every field of the problem is isbits (the residual, the Jacobian function and the prototype, if any), the problem and every method that runs in R1 are isbits, so a kernel takes them by value. A Matrix prototype, for example, makes the problem non-isbits, and solve_batch! rejects it. The state of an R1 solve is isbits too: every buffer slot is nothing.","title":"Four kinds of object"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/backends/#KernelAbstractions-backends","page":"CPU and GPU backends","text":"The device code is written once with KernelAbstractions. get_backend(x) of the iterate selects the backend: CPU() for a host Array, and the backend of CUDA, AMDGPU, Metal or any other GPU array package. The package depends on no vendor package; a vendor package loads its own backend, and the package extensions for CUDA and Metal load with it.\n\nsolve_batch! launches one kernel on get_backend(X) with one work-item per system.\nThe device factorisations (DeviceLU, DeviceQR, DeviceLDLT, DeviceSVD) are sequences of KernelAbstractions kernels on an AbstractGPUArray.\nThe chunked forward mode of ForwardDiff and the reductions run on any array type, with broadcasting and sum and no scalar indexing.","title":"KernelAbstractions backends"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/backends/#The-storage-seam:-one-algorithm-over-two-kinds-of-storage","page":"CPU and GPU backends","text":"R1 returns new values and R3 writes into buffers. That difference is at the leaves of an algorithm, not in its logic. Every algorithm — the loop, Newton, Picard, the line searches, DogLeg, the combinators and Anderson — is written once over a small set of functions with a !! suffix. Each has a method for a value and a method for a buffer:\n\nupdate_iterate!!(::Nothing, x, α, d) = x + α * d                   # R1: a new value\nupdate_iterate!!(y::AbstractArray, x, α, d) = (y .= x .+ α .* d)     # R3: into the buffer\n\nAn algorithm always rebinds the result, xt = update_iterate!!(state.xt, x, α, d), so it reads the same in both regimes. The seam consists of buffer, residual!!, update_iterate!!, map!!, mul!!, copy!!, scale!!, axpby!! and regularize!!, together with jacobian!!, jvp!!, factorize!! and ldiv!!. A backend or a library adds methods to these functions, and a vendor library to the back substitution backsolve! of its factor, never a new function.\n\nThe !! convention is that of BangBang.jl; the package defines its own methods and does not depend on BangBang.","title":"The storage seam: one algorithm over two kinds of storage"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/backends/#The-reduction-seam","page":"CPU and GPU backends","text":"Every norm, dot product and merit value goes through three functions: norm2 (the squared norm), rnorm (the norm, scaled against overflow and underflow) and rdot (the real part of the inner product). Each returns R = real(eltype(x)), and none of them indexes scalars, so they run on an SVector inside a kernel, on an Array and on a GPU array. A widened or compensated accumulator would be a change in these three functions and nowhere else.","title":"The reduction seam"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/backends/#Dispatch-in-place-of-checks","page":"CPU and GPU backends","text":"A pairing that the package does not support has no method. The package writes no flag that the storage could contradict and no runtime test of a type:\n\nwhat is selected or excluded how\nthe call form F(x, p) or F!(y, x, p) the storage of the iterate, through residual!!\nvalue code or in-place code nothing or an array in the buffer slots of the state\nthe factor init_linear dispatches on the method and the iterate type\na vendor fast path a more specific factorize!! method on the factor type, such as DeviceLU{<:CuMatrix}\nTF ≠ T in the Newton step a second ldiv!! method for a factor whose element type differs\none parameter set or one per system the PerSystem wrapper\na Float64 literal in a method reaching a Float32 kernel the ToReal adaptor, which converts every float of a method to real(T)\nan AD back end other than ForwardDiff in R1 no jacobian!! method for it\n\nIn R1 and R3 a missing method is a MethodError on the host. The value checks that a type cannot express, such as a negative tolerance, raise an ArgumentError in a constructor, on the host. Inside a kernel nothing throws: a numerical failure is a ReturnCode.","title":"Dispatch in place of checks"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/backends/#Kernel-safe-types","page":"CPU and GPU backends","text":"ReturnCode is one byte, and the counters of SolverStatus, StepInfo and Options are Int32 or Int16, because 64-bit integers cost registers on a GPU. Every real field is in R = real(T), so these types bring no Float64 into a Float32 kernel; the parameters and the factorisation precision are the caller's (see Device precision). A factorisation precision is a type parameter of the method (LUFactorization{Float32}) and not a field, because a type stored in a field is not isbits. Verbosity, traces and warnings do not exist in the core; a caller reads the status.","title":"Kernel-safe types"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/backends/#What-runs-where","page":"CPU and GPU backends","text":"The factor that a dense R3 solve uses:\n\nfactorisation host Array CuArray (CUDA) MtlArray (Metal) ROCArray (ROCm), other GPU arrays\nLUFactorization LAPACK getrf/getrs cuSOLVER getrf/getrs for BLAS element types MPS lu! for Float32; own kernel otherwise rocSOLVER getrf/getrs on ROCArray for BLAS element types; own kernel otherwise\nQRFactorization LAPACK geqrf/ormqr cuSOLVER geqrf/ormqr and cuBLAS trsv for BLAS element types own kernel rocSOLVER geqrf/ormqr and rocBLAS trsv on ROCArray for BLAS element types; own kernel otherwise\nSVDFactorization LAPACK gesvd cuSOLVER gesvdj, own kernel where it does not converge or where the rtol cutoff lies below √m · eps(R) (m rows, R the factor's real type) own kernel own kernel\nLDLTFactorization LAPACK sytrf/hetrf own kernel own kernel own kernel\nCholeskyFactorization LAPACK potrf/potrs for BLAS element types; generic loop otherwise no device factor: ArgumentError no device factor: ArgumentError no device factor: ArgumentError\nGMRES no factor no factor no factor no factor\n\nThe CUDA extension GeometricSolversCUDAExt adds methods for DeviceLU, DeviceQR and DeviceSVD on CuMatrix{T} with T <: LinearAlgebra.BlasFloat. Other element types stay on the own kernels.\nThe Metal extension GeometricSolversMetalExt adds the Metal Performance Shaders LU and triangular solve for MtlMatrix{Float32}. Every MPS failure status gives a singular factor. ComplexF32 and the other factorisations stay on the own kernels.\nThe ROCm extension GeometricSolversAMDGPUExt adds the rocSOLVER and rocBLAS methods for DeviceLU and DeviceQR on ROCMatrix{T} with T <: LinearAlgebra.BlasFloat. rocSOLVER geqrf and ormqr and rocBLAS trsv serve every shape of the QR factor: the solve with the m × n factor of a least-squares problem, m > n, runs trsv on its leading n × n block. Other element types and the other factorisations stay on the own kernels.\n\nA vendor method replaces only factorize!! and the back substitution of its factor type. The scaling by a power of two, the mixed-precision solve and the status handling are the same on every backend.\n\nThe factor that a sparse R3 solve uses, on a sparse jacobian_prototype:\n\nfactorisation host Vector, SparseMatrixCSC CuVector, CuSparseMatrixCSR (CUDA) other arrays\nLUFactorization Sparspak for a real element type, with Sparspak loaded cuDSS for BLAS element types, with CUDSS loaded no sparse factor\nUMFPACKFactorization UMFPACK for Float64 and ComplexF64 no sparse factor no sparse factor\n\nThe cuDSS extension GeometricSolversCUDSSExt, loaded with CUDA and CUDSS, adds the sparse LU of LUFactorization() on a CuSparseMatrixCSR{T} prototype and a CuVector{T} iterate, T <: LinearAlgebra.BlasFloat. The cuDSS analysis runs once, at the first factorisation, and every later factorisation is a refactorisation. Its Jacobian comes from an analytic J, because an AutoSparse back end needs a host array.\n\nA sparse factor has its own init_linear, factorize!! and ldiv!!, and factorises in the element type of the iterate only: it takes no scaling by a power of two and no mixed precision.\n\nR1 and R2 use the static factorisations on every backend, because they run in one thread. The sparse LU of Sparspak and that of UMFPACK run on a host Vector only. The stacked LevenbergMarquardt path, that of every factorisation other than SVD and of scaling = :diagonal, indexes scalars and runs on a host array only, and an AD back end other than AutoForwardDiff() needs a host array.","title":"What runs where"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/backends/#Device-precision","page":"CPU and GPU backends","text":"backend Float64 Float32 complex\nCPU yes yes yes\nCUDA yes yes yes\nROCm yes yes yes\nMetal no yes ComplexF32\n\nMetal has no Float64, so a Metal solve runs in Float32 throughout: the iterate, the residual, the tolerances, the factorisation and the status. The keywords build the Options in real(eltype(x)), and ToReal converts every float value of the method to that type. ToReal does not change a type parameter or the parameters p: a factorisation precision such as LUFactorization(Float64) and a Float64 parameter keep their types, so the caller chooses them in Float32. With T = Float32, the pair T = Float64, TF = Float32 of Mixed precision does not apply on Metal; it applies on the CPU, CUDA and ROCm.\n\nThe markers TF32, FP16 and BF16 name tensor-core precisions as a type only. No factorisation method takes a marker, so a marker selects no code path.","title":"Device precision"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/backends/#Device-tests","page":"CPU and GPU backends","text":"The device tests run by hand on the machines that have the devices; the repository's test/gpu/README.md describes the runs. The JLArrays reference GPU array runs the device code paths on the CPU in the regular test suite, with scalar indexing disallowed.","title":"Device tests"}]
}
